**B.III.2** οὐ π. ἑπτὰ ἡμερέων not **longer than**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.38.}}

4208:
Included with: ** πρόσω**,
_Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}}, Trag. (but _falsa lectio_ for{πρὸ ἕω} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.103)}}; _poetry_ **πρόσσω**; also **πόρσω**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, Trag.}}; later _Attic dialect_ **πόρρω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus, Comedy texts, Oratt. (πρόσω}} should be restored in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 858.3 and πόρσω in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 482): 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus never uses the word.}} —Regul. _comparative_ and _superlative_ προσωτέρω, πορρωτέρω, προσωτάτω, πορρωτάτω, see at {προσωτέρω}: _poetry_ _comparative_ πόρσιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.114}}: _superlative_ πόρσιστα {{Refs|[prev. author] “N.” 9.29}}. _adverb_: (πρό).
**A** _absolutely_:
**I** of Place, generally with a notion of motion, **forwards, onwards**, π. ἄγειν, φέρειν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.388, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.542, etc.; [δοῦρα] ὄρμενα πρόσσω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.572}}; ἵπποι πρόσσω μεμαυῖαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 615; πρόσω ἵεσθε 12.274, etc.; π. πᾶς πέτεται 16.265}}; π. κατέκυψε{{Refs|[prev. work] 611; π. ἀΐξας 17.734}}; π. τετραμμένος αἰεί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 598; νέμεσθαι π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.133}}; παραγγεῖλαι, πέμψαι π., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 294, 853}}; βῆναι, ἕρπειν π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 195, 547}}; μὴ πόρσω φωνεῖν speak no **further**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 213 (Lyric poetry); μηκέτι πάπταινε πόρσιον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.114: with _Article_, πορεύεσθαι αἰεὶ τὸ πρόσω 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.30, compare 9.57}}; also ἰέναι τοῦ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.1; ἤϊε αἰεὶ ἐς τὸ π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.25.}}
**II** of Distance, **far off**, παπταίνειν τὰ πόρσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.22; ἐγγὺς παρεστὼς καὶ πρόσω δ᾽ ἀποστατῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 65; ὡς ἀπ᾽ ὀμμάτων, πρόσω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 15}}; πρόσω λεύσσειν to see **at a distance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 858.3; πόρρω ποι ἀπεσκοποῦμεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 432e; ἐγγύς, οὐ πρόσω βεβηκώς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 596; ἡ δέ γ᾽ Εὔβοια.. παρατέταται μακρὰ πόρρω πάνυ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 212; εἴτ᾽ ἐγγύς, εἴτε πόρρω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e; πόρρω που ἐκτὸς ὄντι [prev. author] “R.” 499c, etc.}}; πόρρω ποιεῖν τι leave **at a distance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 22.18, compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.90 (uncertain); πάνυ π. γενέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.16}}; τὰ σκέλη κινεῖν ταχὺ καὶ π., of a runner, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1361b24; οἱ πόρρω βάρβαροι [prev. author] “EN” 1149a11.}}
**II.2** **too far**, καὶ νῦν ἴσως πόρρω ἀποτενοῦμεν [τὸν λόγον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 458b; οὐ πόρρω ἐθελήσαιμ᾽ ἂν πιεῖν [prev. author] “Smp.” 176d.}}
**II.3** **in front**, τὰ π. μέρη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.680, compare 15.141, 18(2).265.}}
**III** of Time, **forward**, πρόσσω καὶ ὀπίσσω, see at {ὀπίσω} 11; χρόνος.. ἰὼν πόρσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).55}}; of continuance, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 747}}; **hereafter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.111}}; ἀναβάλλομαι ὡς πόρσιστα **as late as possible**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “N.” 9.29}}; ἤδη πόρρω τῆς ἡμέρας οὔσης **far spent**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.122}}; μέχρι πόρρω till **late**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 581a26.}}
**B** with _genitive_:
**B.I** of Place, **further into**, π. τοῦ ποταμοῦ προβαίνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.28, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.2: especially _metaphorically_, προβήσεσθαι πόρρω μοχθηρίας}} will go **far** in wickedness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 30}}; π. ἀρετῆς ἀνήκειν to have reached **a high point** of virtue, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.237; οὕτω πόρρω σοφίας ἥκεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 294e; πόρρω σοφίας ἐλαύνειν [prev. author] “Euthphr.” 4b, compare “Grg.” 486a, “Cra.” 410e, “Ly.” 204b}}; π. τέχνης a **past** master, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 192 (see. below 11); π. πάνυ ἐλάσαι τῆς πλεονεξίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.39}}: also with _Article_, προβήσομαι ἐς τὸ π. τοῦ λόγου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5; ἐς τὸ π. οὐδὲν προεκόπτετο τῶν πρηγμάτων [prev. author] 3.56}}; ἐς τὸ π. μεγάθεος τιμῶνται are honoured **to a high point** of greatness, i.e. very greatly, {{Refs|[prev. work] 154.}}
**B.II** of Distance, **far from**, οὐ π. τοῦ Ἑλλησπόντου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.13; οὐ π. Σπάρτης πόλις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 733; στάντες οὐ πόρρω τῶν βωμῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 800d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.22, etc.: _metaphorically_, π. δικαίων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 414}}; πόρρω τέχνης,= οὐκ ἀπὸ τέχνης, i. e. φύσει, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 192 (_accusative_ to Scholia, sed see above 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1); π. τοῦ χειρίσματος 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 11; οὐκέτι πόρρω διθυράμβων φθέγγομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 238d}}; πόρρω που τῶν ἐμαυτῷ πεπολιτευμένων **far below** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.299; πόρρω εἶναι τοῦ οἴεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 96e; πόρρω τῶν πραγμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.16; πόρρω τοῦ διαφθείρειν [prev. author] 15.240; πόρρω λίαν τῆς ὑποθέσεως ἀποπλανηθῆναι [prev. author] 7.77}}; π. σαρκός **very far** (i. e. different) **from**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 504b11, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 581e: also followed by ἀπό, ἐξαναχωρέειν π. ἀπὸ τῶν φορτίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.196; πάνυ πόρρω ἀπὸ τῆς θαλάσσης 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.27; ἀπὸ τοῦ τείχους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.49}}; also οὕτω πόρρω εἶ περὶ τοῦ δικαίου so **far out** in your notions of right, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 343c.}}
**B.III** of Time, ὡς πρόσω ἦν τῆς νυκτός **far into** the night, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. ; ὡς π. τῆς νυκτὸς προελήλατο [prev. author] 9.44; διαλέγεσθαι πόρρω τῶν νυκτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217d; λίαν π. ἔδοξε τῶν νυκτῶν εἶναι [prev. author] “Prt.” 310c; ἐκάθευδον μέχρι π. τῆς ἡμέρας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.19; βιότου πόρσω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 910 (Lyric poetry); π. ἤδη ἐστὶ τοῦ βίου, θανάτου δὲ ἐγγύς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 38c; ὀψὲ καὶ π. τῆς ἡλικίας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 2.}}
**B.III.2** οὐ π. ἑπτὰ ἡμερέων not **longer than**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.38.}}

4209:
** πορφύρ-α** [ῠ],
_Ionic dialect_ **πορφύρ-η**, ἡ, **purple-fish, Murex trunculus** and **Purpura haemastoma,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 504, 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 23, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 528a10, +others, 4th c.BC: Speusippus Philosophus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.86c; τρέφουσα.. πορφύρας ἰσάργυρον κηκῖδα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 959.}}
**II** **purple dye obtained from it,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 44, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.39, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 847c; ἡ π. ἡ θαλαττία 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 45 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, etc.; π. βαθυτάτη 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 4.36}}; used as an application, βρέξαντες ἐν [τῷ νάρδῳ] τὴν π. ἐπιτιθέναι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “Eup.” 3.2.}}
**III** ={πορφυρίς} 1, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 23, etc.}}: in _plural_, **cloths of purple,** πορφύρας πατῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 957}}: collectively in _singular_, κωμῳδοῖς.. πορφύραν εἰσφέρων, ὥσπερ οἱ Μεγαρεῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1123a23.}}
**IV** **purple stripe** or other **adornment** of a garment, τῆς σκιᾶς τὴν π. πρῶτον ἐνυφαίνουσ᾽, εἶτα μετὰ τὴν π. τοῦτ᾽ ἔστιν οὔτε λευκὸν οὔτε π. ἀλλ᾽ ὥσπερ αὐγὴ τῆς κρόκης κεκραμένη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 561; ποτικεφάλαια.. μὴ ἔχοντα μήτε σκιὰν μήτε πορφύραν “IG” 5(1).1390.24 (Andania, 1st c.BC),compare “BGU” 1141.41 (1st c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 58, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).791}}; π. πλατεῖα, = Latin _latus clavus,_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.26.1, 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 108 (_plural_)}}; π. alone, {{Refs|“IGRom.” 3.1422 (from Prusias).}}
**V** _metaphorically_, σελήνη οὐρανοῦ π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 6. (Perh. formed from πορφύρεος 11, compare μαρμαίρω, μαρμάρεος, μάρμαρος.)}}

4210:
** πορφύρ-εος**, η, ον,
_Attic dialect_ **πορφῠροῦς**, ᾶ, οῦν, _Aeolic dialect_ **πορφύριος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 64, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 11.2 (πόρφυρον ἄνθος is corrupt in 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 94, and πορφυρ[α] as _neuter_ _plural_ (¯ ?~X ¯) is found in 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20a. 9)}}; -ύριος also in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.179 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}:
**I** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}
**I.1** of the sea (compare πορφύρω), **heaving, surging,** [χαράδραι] ἐς.. ἅλα πορφυρέην μεγάλα στενάχουσι ὁέουσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.391; ἀμφὶ δὲ κῦμα στείρῃ πορφύρεον μεγάλ᾽ ἴαχε νηὸς ἰούσης 1.482, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.428; π. κῦμα.. ποταμοῖο ἵστατ᾽ ἀειρόμενον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.326, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.243}}; θάλασσα Alc. [prev. cited]
**I.2** of blood, **gushing,** αἵματι δὲ χθὼν δεύετο π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.361}}; so, π. θάνατος **onrushing** death, of death in battle, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5.83, +others (but, π. θάνατος· ὁ μέλας καὶ βαθὺς καὶ ταραχώδης, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus).}}
**I.3** of the rainbow, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.547; to which a supernatural π. νεφέλη is compared, [prev. work] 551}}; perhaps **lurid.**
**II** **purple,** of stuff, cloths, etc., π. φᾶρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.221; χλαῖνα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.115; πέπλοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.796; δίπλαξ 3.126, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.242; ῥήγεα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.645; τάπητες 9.200, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.151; σφαῖρα 8.373; χλάμυς 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 64}}; σπάργανα, πτερά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.114, 183}}; χλανίς, χιτών, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 37.11, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 17.52, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 317, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1457 codices (Lyric poetry), etc.}}
**II.2** of human complexion, **bright-red, rosy, flushing,** π. Ἀφροδίτη {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 2.3; στόμα 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 72; παρῇδες 4th-5th c.BC: Phrynichus Tragicus 13}}; χείλη {{Refs|“IG” 3.1376.}}
**II.3** **purple-clad, in purple,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 20.}}
**II.4** _neuter_ _plural_ πορφυρᾶ **purple colour** or **purple spots,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 17.33.}}
**II.5** πορφυροῦν (i.e. ἄνθος), τό, **Woodfordia floribunda** (an Indian shrub), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Ctesias Historicus “fragment” 57.21.}}
**II.6** _adverb_ -ῶς, στύφειν mordant **for purple,** {{Refs|PHolm. 24.37.}}

4212:
** ποσάκις** [ᾰ],
_adverb_ **how many times? how often?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 353d}}; _poetry_ **ποσσάκι** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 119.}}
**II** Indef., **so many times,** οἱ π. ποσοὶ [ἀριθμοί], i.e. **square** numbers, and οἱ π. π. ποσοί **cubes,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1020b5.}}

4213:
** πόσῐς**, ιος,
_Attic dialect_ **εως**, ; _dative_ πόσει, _Ionic dialect_ πόσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.19}}: (πίνω):— **drinking, drink, beverage,** πόσιος καὶ ἐδητύος ἐζ ἔρον ἕντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.469, +others; βρῶσίς τε π. τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.176, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 395: _plural_, βρώσεσιν ἢ πόσεσιν 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 235}}; **carousal,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 101, 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 6.9 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; συγγίνεσθαι ἐς πόσιν to meet for a **carousal,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.172, compare “Bull.Soc.Alex.” 7.66; πρὸς πόσιν τετράφθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.73; λιπαρέειν τῇ πόσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.19; παρὰ τὴν π.}}**over their cups,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.121. ; ἐκ δὲ θοίνας π. ἐγένετο, ἐκ δὲ πόσιος μῶκος 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 148; πόσιος ἐν βάθει 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.29: _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 641a.}}
**2** **draught,** αἷμα πίεται τρίτην πόσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 578; ἐκπίνειν ὑστάτην π. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.20; πόσις φαρμάκου [prev. author] 6.22.}}

4214:
** πόσος**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κόσος**, η, ον, _interrogative_ _adjective_ **of what quantity?**
**1** of Number, **how many?** κόσοι τινές εἰσι οἱ Αακεδαιμόνιοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.234; π. καὶ ποῖα..; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.7; π. χρήματ; [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.1.35; π. ἄττα δὴ ὑποδήματα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1133a21}}: with _singular_ Nouns, **how great? how much?** π. πλῆθος νεῶ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 334; π. τις ἀριθμό; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 198c; π. χρυσίο; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.1.}}
**2** of Distance, **how far?** πόσον ἄπεστιν ἐνθένδε τὸ στράτευμ; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.3.10}}; π. τις ὁδὸς εἴη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.6.10; μέχρι πόσο; Anonymus cited in 2nd c.AD: Gellius, Aulus Grammaticus Latinus 1.3.9, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 953.32.}}
**3** of Time, **how long?** π. τινὰ χρόνο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 558, etc.; π. χρόνο;πότ; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 83.}}
**4** of Value, **how much?** πόσον δίδω; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pax” 1262; πόσο;}}**at what price?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 812, 898, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20b, etc.}}; ἐπὶ πόσ;{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 41a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.43}}; ἐκ πόσου is _falsa lectio_ for{ὁπόσου} in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.30.}}
**5** of Degree, **how great?** πόθο; πόσος τι; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 55; πόσης γέμει σωφροσύνη; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 216d}}: _neuter_ _adverb_ πόσο; πόσ; **to what amount?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 399, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.}}
**8**
**II** **ποσός**, ή, όν, _indefinite_ _adjective_ **of a certain quantity** or **magnitude,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 3D., 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 245d}}, etc.; ποσὰ τῶν περιφερῶν **a certain number of..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.50U.}}; ἐπὶ ποσόν **for a certain time,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.34.15, etc.; οὐδ᾽ ἐπὶ π. [prev. author] 1.1.2}}; κατὰ ποσόν to **a certain extent,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 81.22.}}
**II.2** ποσόν, τό, ={ποσότης}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 24c, 24d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 4b20, “Metaphorical” 1020a7, etc.}}; κατὰ ποσόν in point of **quantity,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1158b31.}}
**III** _adverb_ ποσῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 34, 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “Oss.” 18, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de signis fracturarum - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 2, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 238.24, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.120, 227. (I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **q[uglide]oty-os,** compareLatin _quot,_ Sanskrit _káti_ 'how many?')

4215:
** ποτᾰμός**, ὁ,
**river, stream,** Ὠκεανοῖο ἐξ οὗ περ πάντες π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.196}}; π. ἁλιμυρήεις, ἀργυροδίνης, βαθυδίνης, βαθύρροος, δεινός, διιπετής, δινήεις, ἐΰρροος, ἐρίδουπος, εὐρὺ ῥέων, θεῖος, ἱερός, ἴφθιμος, καλλίροος, κελάδων, λάβρος, πλήθων, χειμάρροος, ὠκύροος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.460, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.8, 212, 8, 25, 17.263, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.242, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.130, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.515 (_plural_), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.304, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.238, 10.351 (_plural_), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.749 (_plural_), 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.441, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.576, 21.270, 5.87, 87, 598}}; νυκτὸς π., of the **rivers** of hell, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 130.9: proverbial, ἄνω ποταμῶν}} of extraordinary events, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 335, etc. (in full, ἄνω π. ἱερῶν χωροῦσι παγαί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 410 (Lyric poetry)); π. οὐκ ἔστιν ἐμβῆναι δὶς τῷ αὐτῷ 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 91}}; π. θαλάσσῃ ἐρίζεις, of unequal combats, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus, etc.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **rivers of fire** or **lava,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.22, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 370: Comedy texts, ζωμοῦ π. κρέα θερμὰ κυλίνδων 5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 1.8, compare 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 108.3; also π. πραγμάτων 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 5.}}
**3** **artificial stream, canal,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.1.10, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.21.1; οἱ ὀρυχθέντες π. “OGI” 54.23 (Adule, 3rd c.BC).}}
**II** personified, **rivergod,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.7, 73, etc.}}
**III** name of the constellation Eridanus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.2.20, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 358, etc.}}

4217:
** ποδᾰπός**, ή, όν,
**from what country?**: hence, generally, **whence? where born?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.218 (as variant), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 575, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1160, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 276, etc.; τίς καὶ π.; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20b; π. τὸ γένο; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 186, compare “Av.” 108}}; of wine, π. ὁ Βρόμιο; Θάσιος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 230.}}
**2** generally, **of what sort?** π. τὸ δῶρο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 453}}: frequently in later Gr., {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Matt.8.27, NT.Luke.1.29, 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 1.1, etc.}}; in a play on both senses, π. τὸ γένο; πλούσιος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 90}}; π. (i.e. κύων); οἷος.. μὴ δάκνειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.40}}. _adverb_ **-πῶς**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. 2.925}}; ποταπῶς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum” (2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 39}} considers _signification_ 2 un-Attic, but S. [prev. cited] is cited by Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus p.239 H.: the spelling **ποταπός** (which is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 230 (codices 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus))}} is found in later Gr., [same places] above,{{Refs 1st c.BC+|compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.25J., 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.57, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.157 (variant), 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.16, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 22, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.138c. ποδ-}} = Latin _quod;_ for the termination compare ἀλλοδαπός (and see {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 20.15). Derived from ποίου δαπέδου}} by Phryn.[same place], {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.)}}

4218:
Included with: ** πότε**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κότε**, _Doric dialect_ **πόκα** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.7, +others}}:—_interrogative_ Particle used in direct and indirect questions, **when? at what time?** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.227, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.642; πότ᾽ εἰ μὴ νῦν..; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 101 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ch.” 394 (Lyric poetry)}}; πότ᾽ ἆρ; ={πότε} with ἆρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.4 or 11, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 563; πότε δ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 720 (Lyric poetry); ἐς πότε λήξε; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1185 (Lyric poetry); ἐκ πότε.. ἤρξασθε “SIG” 832.9 (Astypalaea, 2nd c.AD).}}
**II** _indefinite_ **ποτε** (_Attic dialect_, also {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus in οὔποτε “IG” 5(2).343.48,66 (Orchom., 4th c.BC), οὔτε ποτέ “Tab.Defix.” in “Philol.” 59.201, and 5th c.AD: Cypria in μήποτε “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 144H.)}}, _Ionic dialect_ **κοτε**, _Doric dialect_ **ποκα**, _Aeolic dialect_ **ποτα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.5, _enclitic_ Particle:}}
**II.1** **at some time** or **other, at some time,** τάχ᾽ ἄν ποτε θυμὸν ὀλέσσῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.205, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.76, etc.}}; π. καὶ ἄλλοτε **at certain** other **times,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.12, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1451a9, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 24.}}
**II.2** in _hypothetical_ clauses, questions, etc., **at any time, ever,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 755, 1133; εἴ κοτε Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.Oxy.” 2080.69}}; compare εἴ ποτε: also with relatives (compare δήποτε), Ζεὺς ὅστις ποτ᾽ ἐστίν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 160 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; ὅποι ποτέ, ὅπου π., etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 780, “Aj.” 194 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; after πω, πη, see at {πώποτε, πήποκα}: very frequently with negatives, οὔτε ποτ᾽.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.226; οὐδέ ποτ᾽ 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 759; οὐδέν ποτ᾽ ἄλλο 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 16; οὐδεὶς ἐρεῖ ποθ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 481; οὐκ ἂν δή ποτε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.271, etc.; τοῦτο μὴ δόξῃς ποτέ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 762, compare 648, 750, etc.}}; compare οὔποτε, μήποτε, οὐδέποτε, μηδέποτε, οὐπώποτε, μηπώποτε.
**II.3** in _correlative_ clauses it stands first, with accent, ποτὲ μὲν.., ποτὲ δὲ.. **at one time.., at another..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 170c (assuming variant), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.38.6, etc.; ποτὲ μὲν.., αὖθις δὲ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 560a; ποτὲ.., τοτὲ δ᾽ οὔ [prev. author] “Tht.” 192d; ποτ᾽ εἶχε.., εἶτά γε νῦν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.50; ποτὲ μὲν.. νῦν δέ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 11.1}}; ποτὲ δὲ.., without any preceding Part., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 9.7 (uncertain).}}
**III** of some unknown point of time,
**III.1** in _reference_ to the past, **once,** ὅν ποτ᾽ Ἀθήνη θρέψε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.547, etc.; οὕς ποτ᾽ ἀπ᾽ Αἰνείαν ἑλόμην, of the day before, 8.108 (see. Scholia), compare 14.45; ἤδη π. 1.260, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1142, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 346, “Ra.” 931; ποτ᾽ ἤδη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 50; πρόσθε πού ποτ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1549; χρόνῳ ποτ᾽ [prev. author] “Ant.” 303}}; especially in telling a story, **once upon a time,** οὕτω ποτ᾽ ἦν μῦς καὶ γαλῆ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1182, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 237b}}: with historic _present_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 715, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 416, “Ba.” 2}}: with a _substantive_, εἰς τήν π. φιλίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.22; τυράννου.. πάλαι π. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1043, compare “Ph.” 677 (Lyric poetry), “Tr.” 555.}}
**III.1.b** **at length,** μόγις δή κοτε εἶπε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116; μόγις οὖν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314e, etc.; ὀψὲ γοῦν π. 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 27p.484M.}}
**III.2** in _reference_ to the future, **at some time,** καί π. τοι.. παρέσσεται.. δῶρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.213, compare 240, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 386, “Ant.” 912, etc.}}: also to denote earnest expectation, **at length,** εὔχεταί π. οἶκον ἰδεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.293; ἔμελλον ἄρα παύσειν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 268; especially with _imperative_, μέθες π.}}**dimitte tandem aliquando,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 816; τείσασθ᾽, ἀλλὰ τῷ χρόνῳ π. [prev. work] 1041, etc.}}; ὀψέ π. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.31d.}}
**III.3** with intensive force, in questions, τίς ποτ; who **in the world?** τίνες ποτ᾽ ἐστ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 408, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 220, etc.}}; τί ποτ᾽ ἐστὶ τοῦτο τὸ πάθος what it can **possibly** be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 187d; οὐκ ἐξερεῖς ποτ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 335, compare 754, “Aj.” 1290, etc.}}; to strengthen ἀεί, ἀεί ποτε **from all time, always in the past,** ἀεί ποτε ζῇ ταῦτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 456, compare “Aj.” 320, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.82, +others, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 42.5; ἀεὶ δή π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.13, 8.73.}}

4219:
** πότε**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κότε**, _Doric dialect_ **πόκα** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.7, +others}}:
**I** _interrogative_ Particle used in direct and indirect questions, **when? at what time?** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.227, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.642; πότ᾽ εἰ μὴ νῦν..; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 101 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ch.” 394 (Lyric poetry)}}; πότ᾽ ἆρ; ={πότε} with ἆρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.4 or 11, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 563; πότε δ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 720 (Lyric poetry); ἐς πότε λήξε; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1185 (Lyric poetry); ἐκ πότε.. ἤρξασθε “SIG” 832.9 (Astypalaea, 2nd c.AD).}}
**II** _indefinite_ **ποτε** (_Attic dialect_, also {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus in οὔποτε “IG” 5(2).343.48,66 (Orchom., 4th c.BC), οὔτε ποτέ “Tab.Defix.” in “Philol.” 59.201, and 5th c.AD: Cypria in μήποτε “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 144H.)}}, _Ionic dialect_ **κοτε**, _Doric dialect_ **ποκα**, _Aeolic dialect_ **ποτα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.5, _enclitic_ Particle:}}
**II.1** **at some time** or **other, at some time,** τάχ᾽ ἄν ποτε θυμὸν ὀλέσσῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.205, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.76, etc.}}; π. καὶ ἄλλοτε **at certain** other **times,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.12, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1451a9, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 24.}}
**II.2** in _hypothetical_ clauses, questions, etc., **at any time, ever,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 755, 1133; εἴ κοτε Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.Oxy.” 2080.69}}; compare εἴ ποτε: also with relatives (compare δήποτε), Ζεὺς ὅστις ποτ᾽ ἐστίν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 160 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; ὅποι ποτέ, ὅπου π., etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 780, “Aj.” 194 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; after πω, πη, see at {πώποτε, πήποκα}: very frequently with negatives, οὔτε ποτ᾽.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.226; οὐδέ ποτ᾽ 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 759; οὐδέν ποτ᾽ ἄλλο 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 16; οὐδεὶς ἐρεῖ ποθ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 481; οὐκ ἂν δή ποτε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.271, etc.; τοῦτο μὴ δόξῃς ποτέ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 762, compare 648, 750, etc.}}; compare οὔποτε, μήποτε, οὐδέποτε, μηδέποτε, οὐπώποτε, μηπώποτε.
**II.3** in _correlative_ clauses it stands first, with accent, ποτὲ μὲν.., ποτὲ δὲ.. **at one time.., at another..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 170c (assuming variant), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.38.6, etc.; ποτὲ μὲν.., αὖθις δὲ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 560a; ποτὲ.., τοτὲ δ᾽ οὔ [prev. author] “Tht.” 192d; ποτ᾽ εἶχε.., εἶτά γε νῦν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.50; ποτὲ μὲν.. νῦν δέ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 11.1}}; ποτὲ δὲ.., without any preceding Part., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 9.7 (uncertain).}}
**III** of some unknown point of time,
**III.1** in _reference_ to the past, **once,** ὅν ποτ᾽ Ἀθήνη θρέψε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.547, etc.; οὕς ποτ᾽ ἀπ᾽ Αἰνείαν ἑλόμην, of the day before, 8.108 (see. Scholia), compare 14.45; ἤδη π. 1.260, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1142, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 346, “Ra.” 931; ποτ᾽ ἤδη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 50; πρόσθε πού ποτ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1549; χρόνῳ ποτ᾽ [prev. author] “Ant.” 303}}; especially in telling a story, **once upon a time,** οὕτω ποτ᾽ ἦν μῦς καὶ γαλῆ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1182, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 237b}}: with historic _present_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 715, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 416, “Ba.” 2}}: with a _substantive_, εἰς τήν π. φιλίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.22; τυράννου.. πάλαι π. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1043, compare “Ph.” 677 (Lyric poetry), “Tr.” 555.}}
**III.1.b** **at length,** μόγις δή κοτε εἶπε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116; μόγις οὖν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314e, etc.; ὀψὲ γοῦν π. 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 27p.484M.}}
**III.2** in _reference_ to the future, **at some time,** καί π. τοι.. παρέσσεται.. δῶρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.213, compare 240, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 386, “Ant.” 912, etc.}}: also to denote earnest expectation, **at length,** εὔχεταί π. οἶκον ἰδεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.293; ἔμελλον ἄρα παύσειν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 268; especially with _imperative_, μέθες π.}}**dimitte tandem aliquando,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 816; τείσασθ᾽, ἀλλὰ τῷ χρόνῳ π. [prev. work] 1041, etc.}}; ὀψέ π. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.31d.}}
**III.3** with intensive force, in questions, τίς ποτ; who **in the world?** τίνες ποτ᾽ ἐστ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 408, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 220, etc.}}; τί ποτ᾽ ἐστὶ τοῦτο τὸ πάθος what it can **possibly** be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 187d; οὐκ ἐξερεῖς ποτ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 335, compare 754, “Aj.” 1290, etc.}}; to strengthen ἀεί, ἀεί ποτε **from all time, always in the past,** ἀεί ποτε ζῇ ταῦτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 456, compare “Aj.” 320, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.82, +others, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 42.5; ἀεὶ δή π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.13, 8.73.}}

4220:
** πότερος**, α, ον;
_Ionic dialect_ **κότερος**, η, ον: **whether of the two?** both in direct and indirect questions; once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, οὐκ ἂν γνοίης ποτέροισι μετείη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.85; κότερα τούτων αἱρετώτερά ἐστι..; 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52, compare 1.126; πότερα τύχης καὶ πότερα γνώμης ἔργα κρίνει; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.4; σκόπει πρὸς ποτέρους διαλέγῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 528a, etc.}}: sometimes the two objects referred to follow in apposition, ἐρωτώσης τῆς μητρός, π. καλλίων.. δοκεῖ εἶναι, ὁ πατὴρ ἢ οὗτος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.2, compare “Mem.” 1.6.9; πότερος.., ὁ ἰατρὸς ἢ ὁ ὀψοποιό; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 464d, compare 498a, etc.}}; modified by τις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 715a, etc.}}; repeated in the same sentence, πότερος ποτερου φίλος γίγνετα; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ly.” 212a.}}
**II** _neuter_ πότερον and πότερα (interchangeable, πότερον δέδρακεν ἢ οὔ, καὶ πότερ᾽ ἄκων ἢ ἑκώ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.79, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.16), as}} _adverb_ at the beginning of an _interrogative_ sentence containing two alternative propositions, the second being connected by ἤ.., **whether.. or,**
**II.a** in direct questions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.22, “fragment” 213, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 17.33, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.88, etc.; τίνες κατῆρξαν, πότερον Ἕλληνες ἢ παῖς ἐμό; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 351, etc.; πότερα δικαστὴν ἢ δικηφόρον λέγει; [prev. author] “Ch.” 120, compare “Supp.” 336.}}
**II.b** in indirect questions, ἐπείρεσθαι κότερα τὴν ἑωυτοῦ ἢ τὴν Κύρου λέγοι ἀρχήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.91, compare 3.32, etc.: the Verb is sometimes repeated, π. οὐδενὶ δύναται ἀρέσαι, ἢ ἔστιν οἷς καὶ πάνυ ἀρέσκε; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.6: sometimes π.}} precedes the common Verb, π. βούλοιτο μένειν ἢ ἀπιένα; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.3.15, compare “Oec.” 18.1.}}
**II.2** sometimes a third clause (with ἤ) is inaccurately added, κότερα παρὰ δήμου ἢ ὀλιγαρχίης ἢ μουνάρχο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.82, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 247, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 112}}; and a fourth, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 539.}}
**II.3** the second alternative is sometimes left to be supplied, πότερα δὴ κερτομῶν λέγεις τάδε (i.e. ἢ μή..); {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1235, compare “OC” 333, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 239, “Th.” 94 (Lyric poetry), “Ag.” 274, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 228a, “R.” 501d, etc.}}
**II.4** πότερον is sometimes omitted in the first clause, ἐπισκέψασθαι.., ὀρθὴ ἢ ψευδής {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 161d, compare “Lg.” 670b.}}
**II.5** in affirmative sentences, π.. ἤ, **either..** or, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 14, +others; so π... ἤ.. ἤ [prev. work] 27.}}
**III** _indefinite_, **one of other, either of the two,** τί οὐ λέγει π. ὑμῶ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 181d; τούτων ποτέροις ἐπακολουθῆσαι [prev. author] “Chrm.” 171b, compare “R.” 499c, “Phlb.” 20e, “Tht.” 145b, etc.}}; ἅτεροι πότεροι, ={ὁποτεροιοῦν}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 421.31 (Thermon, 3rd c.BC): for ὁπότερος, ἐξέστω αὐτῷ πότερον ἂν βούληται, ἤ.. ἤ.. “Abh.Berl.Akad.” 1925(5).7 (Cyrene, 3rd c.BC): in this sense 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}} would make it _oxytone_ ποτερός, ά, o/n.
**IV** _adverb_ ποτέρως, see entry (I.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **q[uglide]o-tero-s,** cf. Sanskrit _katarás,_ Gothic _hwapar_ 'which of two?')

4221:
** ποτήριον**, τό,
**drinking-cup, wine-cup,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 52, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20a. 10, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37, 3.148, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 120, 237, etc.}}; οὔποτ᾽ ἐκ ταὐτοῦ μεθ᾽ ἡμῶν πίεται π.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1289; π. ἀργυρᾶ “IG” 12.232, +others; κεραμεᾶ 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.464a, etc.}}
**2** **the Cup** in the Eucharist, {{NT|NT.1Cor.11.25f}}
**3** **jar,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.385.}}
**4** **receptacle** for offerings in temples, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 6.27 (_plural_, 2nd c.BC).}}
**II** **absorbent preparation,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.258, 6th c.AD: Alexander Trallianus Medicus 10 (_plural_).}}
**III** see at {ποτίρριον}.

4222:
** ποτίζω**,
_Attic dialect_ _future_ ιῶ {{Refs|“Geoponica” 17.12.3}}: (πότος):— **give to drink,** ἄκρητον ποτίσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 7.46; ἐπότισεν.. ὁ ἰατρὸς τὸ φάρμακον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 199a34, compare 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “fragment” 118; οἶνον [ὑποζυγίοις] 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 27.14}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.11, +others}}
**2** with _double_ _accusative_, τοὺς ἵππους νέκταρ ἐπότισε **gave** them nectar **to drink,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 247e; μικρὸν ὕδωρ π. τινά LXX.Gen.24.17; ποτήριον π. τινά NT.Mark.9.41, cf. NT.1Cor.12.13 (_passive_).}}
**3** **water,** [τὰ φυόμενα] {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.25, cf. LXX.Gen.2.6}}; **irrigate,** φυτά {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 72.4 (3rd c.BC); π. τὴν γῆν ἀπὸ χερός [prev. work] 155.3 (3rd c.BC)}}; also **water** cattle, ταύρως καὶ πόρτιας {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.121}}:—_passive_, of land, **to be watered, to be irrigated**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“CPR” 1.9 (1st c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Abd.” 27, etc.}}
**4** π. οἴνῳ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“OGI” 200.16 (Axum, 4th c.AD)}}; οἴνου {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.54 (cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 4.16}}; οἴνῳ codices {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus).}}
**5** **moisten,** μετά τινος Zos.Alch.p.167 {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}; τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Moses Alchemista cited in [same work] p.183 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}

4225:
** που**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κου**, _Aeolic dialect_ **ποι** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 25.17, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.101, “BCH” 37.157 (Cyme, 3rd c.BC), probably in 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 9}}:—_enclitic_ _adverb_ **anywhere, somewhere,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.514, etc.}}; frequently with other Advs. of Place, οὐχ ἑκάς π. **somewhere** not far off, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 41; πέλας π. [prev. work] 163 (_anapaest meter_); μηδαμοῦ.. π. [prev. work] 256 (uncertain reading)}}; π. πέραν τοῦ ποταμοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.3; ἄλλοθί π. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.41; τῇδέ π. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.108.3, etc.: with _genitive_, ἀλλά π. αὐτοῦ ἀγρῶν}} in **some part** there of the fields, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.639}}; ἐμβαλεῖν π. (perhaps ποι) τῆς χώρας **some part** of the country, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.42; εἴ π. τῆς χώρας ταὐτὸ τοῦτο πάθος συνέβη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.195.}}
**II** without reference to Place, **in some degree,** καί πού τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.87}}: frequently to qualify an expression, **perhaps, I suppose,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}; added to introductory Particles, οὕτω π... {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.116; Ζεὺς μέν π. τό γε οἶδε 3.308; ὡς ὅτε π. 11.292}}; ἤν π., εἰ μή π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 3.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372a: strengthened, τάχ᾽ ἄν π. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1116; ἴσως π. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 518}}: attached to single words to limit their significance, πάντως κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.73}}; τί π. δράσει; what **in the world?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 743; οὐδείς π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 64d}}; with numerals, ἔτεα τρία καὶ δέκα κ. μάλιστα **about** thirteen years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119, compare 209, 7.22, etc.: οὔ τί που}} denies with indignation or wonder, **surely it cannot be..,** οὔ τί π. οὗτος Ἀπόλλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.87, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1233, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1260, “Pax” 1211, “Ra.” 522, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 362d, etc.}}; οὐ δήπου adds a shade of suspicion, οὐ δήπου Στράτω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 122, compare “Av.” 269, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 194b}}: for δήπου, ἦπου, (see entry).—In late writers {{LXX+NT+2nd c.AD+|(LXX.Josh.2.5, +others, NT.John.7.35, +others, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.27.9, 4.1.93, etc.) ποῦ, που}} take the place of ποῖ, ποι, with Verbs of motion, as in Engl. **where** for **whither?** This idiom (condemned by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 30, ποῦ ἄπει.. ἁμάρτημα)}} is found occasionally in early authors, ποῦ τοι ἀπειλαὶ οἴχοντα; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.219; ἐξελθών που 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.8; ἰόντα που 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.16}}; but in pure _Attic dialect_ only as _falsa lectio_ for{ποῖ},{ποι}.

4226:
** ποῦ**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κοῦ**, _interrogative_ _adverb_ **where?** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.; frequently with _dative_ _person_, ποῦ δέ οἱ ἔντεα κεῖται..; 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.407; π. τοι τόξο; 5.171; π. τοι Δηΐφοβος.., π. δέ τοι Ὀθρυονεύ; 13.770, 772; ἀλλ᾽ ἡμὶν Αἴας ποὖστι; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 733; π. μοί ποτε ναίε; [prev. author] “OC” 137 (Lyric poetry)}}; rarely with Verbs of motion in early authors, see που entry near the end:—with _genitive_ loci, π. χθονό; **where** in the world? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 231; π. γῆ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 984, “OT” 108, etc.; π. τῆς χώρα; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 7.14}}; τὴν σοφίαν.. π. χοροῦ τάξομε; **in what part of** the chorus? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 279c.}}
**2** so in a sense not strictly local, π. ποτ᾽ εἶ φρενῶ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 390; π. γνώμης ποτ᾽ ε; [prev. author] “Ant.” 42; π. ποτ᾽ εἰμὶ πράγματο; [prev. author] “Tr.” 375}}; π. σοι τύχης ἕστηκε; **at what point** of fortune stands he? {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 102.}}
**II** of manner, **how?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 406, “Or.” 802}}; to express an inference very strongly, κοῦ γε δὴ.. οὐκ ἂν χωσθείη κόλπος..; **how** then would it not.. ? i.e. it certainly would.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.11, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.27 codices}}; in Trag., in indignant questions, **how? by what right?** π. σὺ στρατηγεῖς τοῦδ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1100; ποῦ σὺ μάντις εἶ σαφή; [prev. author] “OT” 390, compare “Ph.” 451, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 369 (Lyric poetry), 510; π. γάρ ἐστι δίκαιο; 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.41, compare 23.58.}}

4228:
** πούς**, ὁ, ποδός, ποδί, πόδα
(not ποῦν, Thom.Mag.p.257 R.): _dative_ _plural_ ποσί, _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry ποσσί (also {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 100 (Lyric poetry)), πόδεσσι}} once πόδεσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 240 (Lyric poetry): _genitive_ and _dative_ dual ποδοῖν}} _Epic dialect_ ποδοῖιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.537}}:—_Doric dialect_ _nominative_ **πός** (compare ἀρτίπος, πούλυπος, etc.) {{Refs|“Lyrica Adespota” 72}}, but **πούς** {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 2.34 (perhaps Hellenistic)}}; **πῶς·** πός, ὑπὸ Δωριέων, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}} (perhaps πός· πούς, ὑ.Δ.); _Laconian dialect_ **πόρ**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] (on the accent see Hdn.Gr. 2.921, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 134.24)}}:—**foot,** both of men and beasts, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.212, 8.339 (both _plural_), etc.}}; in _plural_, also, a bird's **talons,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.526}}; **arms** or **feelers** of a polypus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 524}}: properly **the foot from the ankle** down wards, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.386; ταρσὸς ποδός 11.377, 388}}; ξύλινος π., of an artificial **foot,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.37}}: but also of **the leg with the foot,** as χείρ for the arm and hand, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.772, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.149, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 59.}}
**2** **foot** as that with which one runs, πόδας ὠκὺς Ἀχιλλεύς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.215, +others; or walks, τῷ δ᾽ ὑπὸ ποσσὶ μέγας πελεμίζετ᾽ Ὄλυμπος 8.443}}; frequently with reference to swiftness, περιγιγνόμεθ᾽ ἄλλων πύξ τε.. ἠδὲ πόδεσσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.103}}; ποσὶν ἐρίζειν to race **on foot,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.325, compare 23.792; πόδεσσι πάντας ἐνίκα 20.410, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.261; ἀέθλια ποσσὶν ἄροντο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.124, etc.}}; ποδῶν τιμά, αἴγλα, ἀρετά, ὁρμά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 12.15, 13.36, “P.” 10.23, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 9.20; ἅμιλλαν ἐπόνει ποδοῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 213 (Lyric poetry): the _dative_ ποσί}} (ποσσί, πόδεσσι) is added to many Verbs denoting motion, π. βήσετο, παρέδραμον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.389, 23.636; π. θέειν, πηδᾶν, σκαίρειν, πλίσσεσθαι, [prev. work] 622, 21.269, 18.572, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.318; ὀρχεῖσθαι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 3; ἔρχεσθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.39; πάρος ποσὶν οὖδας ἱκέσθαι 8.376; νέρθε δὲ ποσσὶν ἤϊε μακρὰ βιβάς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.212}}; also emphatically with Verbs denoting **to trample** or **tread upon,** πόσσι καταστείβοισι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 94; ἐπεμβῆναι ποδί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 456}}; πόδα βαίνειν, see at {βαίνω} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.4}}; πόδα τιθέναι to journey, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1100: _metaphorically_, νόστιμον ναῦς ἐκίνησεν πόδα}} started on its homeward way, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 940 (Lyric poetry); νεῶν λῦσαι ποθοῦσιν οἴκαδ᾽.. πόδα [prev. work] 1020}}; χειρῶν ἔκβαλλον ὀρείους πόδας ναός, i. e. oars, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Timotheus Lyricus “Persae” 102}}; φωνὴ τῶν π. τοῦ ὑετοῦ sound of the **pattering** of rain, {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.18.41.}}
**3** as a point of measurement, ἐς πόδας ἐκ κεφαλῆς from head to **foot,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.353; ἐκ κεφαλῆς ἐς πόδας ἄκρους 16.640; and reversely, ἐκ ποδῶν δ᾽ ἄνω.. εἰς ἄκρον κάρα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 169; ἐκ τῶν ποδῶν ἐς τὴν κεφαλήν σοι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 650; also ἐκ τριχὸς ἄχρι ποδῶν “Anthologia Graeca” 5.193 (3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus or 3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus); ἐς κορυφὰν ἐκ ποδός [prev. work] 7.388 (from Bianor).}}
**4** πρόσθε ποδός or ποδῶν, προπάροιθε ποδῶν, **just** before one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.877, 21.601, 13.205; τὸ πρὸ ποδὸς.. χρῆμα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).13; αὐτὰ τὰ πρὸ τῶν ποδῶν ὁρᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 3.4, compare “An.” 4.6.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 432d.}}
**4.b** παρά or πὰρ ποδός **off-hand, at once,** ἀνελέσθαι πὰρ ποδός {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 282; γνόντα τὸ πὰρ ποδός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.60, compare 10.62; πὰρ ποδί}}**close at hand,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “O.” 1.74}}; but παραὶ ποσὶ κάππεσε θυμός sank to their **feet,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.280; παρὰ πόδα}}**in a moment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 838 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 242a}}; **close behind,** Νέμεσις δέ γε πὰρ πόδας (to be read πόδα) βαίνει Prov. cited in {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}; also παρὰ πόδας **immediately afterwards** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.35.3, 5.26.13, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.272; παρὰ π. οἱ ἔλεγχοι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist. Conscr.” 13, compare 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.115 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; τὰ ἔμπροσθεν αὐτοῦ καὶ παρὰ πόδας **at his very feet,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 174a; περὶ τῶν παρὰ πόδας καὶ τῶν ἐν ὀφθαλμοῖς [prev. work]c; τὸ πλησίον καὶ παρὰ π. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cal.” 1.}}
**4.c** ἐν ποσί **in one's way, close at hand,** τὸν ἐν π. γινόμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.79, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.32; τἀν ποσὶν κακά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1327, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 397; τοὐν ποσὶν κακόν [prev. author] Alcaeus739; τὴν ἐν ποσὶ [κώμην] αἱρεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.97; τὰ ἐν ποσὶν ἀγνοεῖν}}**everyday matters,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1263a18, etc.}}
**4.d** τὸ πρὸς ποσί, ={τὸ ἐν ποσί}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 130.}}
**4.e** all these phrases are opposed to ἐκ ποδῶν **out of the way, far off,** written ἐκποδών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.35, etc.; also, βίαια πάντ᾽ ἐκ ποδὸς ἐρύσαις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.67.}}
**5** to denote close pursuit, ἐκ ποδὸς ἕπεσθαι follow **in the track,** i.e. **close behind,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.68.1, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.57, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.33, etc.; ἐκ ποδῶν διώξαντες 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 11.}}
**5.b** in earlier writers κατὰ πόδας **on the heels** of a person, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.98, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.98, 8.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.20, LXX.Gen.49.19 (also κατὰ πόδα ὑπολαβεῖν}}**on the moment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 243d)}}; ἡ κατὰ πόδας ἡμέρα the **very next** day, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.12.1 (but κατὰ πόδας αἱρεῖν}} catch it **running,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.40, compare “Mem.” 2.6.9): with _genitive_ _person_, κατὰ πόδας τινὸς ἐλαύνειν, ἰέναι}} march, come close **at his heels, on his track,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.89, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.64}}; τῇ κατὰ π. ἡμέρᾳ τῆς ἐκκλησίας on the day **immediately after** it, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.45.5; κατὰ π. τῆς μάχης 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.157J., etc.}}
**6** various phrases:
**6.a** ἀνὰ πόδα **backwards,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**6.b** ἐπὶ πόδα **backwards facing the enemy,** ἐπὶ π. ἀναχωρεῖν, ἀνάγειν, ἀναχάζεσθαι, to retire **without turning to fly, leisurely,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.32, “Cyr.” 3.3.69, 7.1.34, etc.; also ἐπὶ πόδας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 12}}; but γίνεται ἡ ἔξοδος οἷον ἐπὶ πόδας the offspring is as it were born **feetforemost,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 752b14.}}
**6.c** περὶ πόδα, properly of a shoe, **round the foot,** i.e. **fitting exactly,** ὡς ἔστι μοι τὸ χρῆμα τοῦτο περὶ πόδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 197, compare 129: with _dative_, ὁρᾷς ὡς ἐμμελὴς ἡ ἀρχὴ καὶ περὶ πόδα τῇ ἱστορίᾳ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 14, compare “Ind.” 10, “Pseudol.” 23.}}
**6.d** ὡς ποδῶνἔχει as he is off **for feet,** i. e. as quick as he can, ὡς ποδῶν εἶχον [τάχιστα] ἐβοήθεον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.116; ἐδίωκον ὡς ποδῶν ἕκαστος εἶχον [prev. author] 9.59; φευκτέον ὡς ἔχει ποδῶν ἕκαστος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 507d; so, σοῦσθε.. ὅπως ποδῶν [ἔχετε] 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 837 (Lyric poetry).}}
**6.e** ἔξω τινὸς πόδα ἔχειν keep one's **foot** out of a thing, i. e. be clear of it, ἔξω κομίζων πηλοῦ πόδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ch.” 697; πημάτων ἔξω πόδα ἔχει [prev. author] “Pr.” 265; ἐκτὸς κλαυμάτων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1260; ἔξω πραγμάτων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 109: without a _genitive_, ἐκτὸς ἔχειν πόδα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.289: opposed to εἰς ἄντλον ἐμβήσῃ πόδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 168; ἐν τούτῳ πεδίλῳ.. πόδ᾽ ἔχων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.8.}}
**6.f** ἀμφοῖν ποδοῖν, etc., to denote energetic action, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 35, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.78; συνέχευε ποσὶν καὶ χερσὶν 15.364; χερσίν τε ποσίν τε καὶ σθένει 20.360; τιμωρήσειν χειρὶ καὶ ποδὶ καὶ πάσῃ δυνάμει 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.115, compare 3.109}}; τερπωλῆς ἐπέβημεν ὅλῳ ποδί with all the **foot,** i.e. **entirely,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1166, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 13.19 (probably); καταφεύγειν ἐπὶ τὴν πόλιν ὥσπερ ἐκ δυοῖν ποδοῖν 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.117J.; opposed to οὐκ ἂν προβαίην τὸν πόδα τὸν ἕτερον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 161; οὐκ ἂν ἔφασκεν ἐξελθεῖν οὐδὲ τὸν ἕτερον πόδα 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.82.}}
**6.g** τὴν ὑπὸ πόδα [κατάστασιν] **just below them,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.68.9}}; ὑπὸ πόδας τίθεσθαι trample under **foot,** scorn, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1097c}}; οἱ ὑπὸ πόδα those **next below them** (in rank), {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 25.2}}; ὑπὸ πόδα χωρεῖν **recede, decline,** of strength, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus}} _middle_ cited in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus (?) 21.16.}}
**6.h** for ὀρθῷ ποδί, see at {ὀρθός} {{Refs|II.1.}}
**6.k** ἁλιεῖς ἀπὸ ποδός probably fishermen who fish **from the land,** not from boats, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 221.5 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; ποτίσαι ἀπὸ ποδός perhaps irrigate **by the feet** (of oxen turning the irrigation-wheel), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 157.21 (2nd c.AD)}}; τόπον.. ἀπὸ ποδὸς ἐξηρτισμένον uncertain meaning in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POsl.” 55.11 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**1** ἀγγεῖον.. τρήματα ἐκ τῶν ὑπὸ ποδὸς ἔχον **round the bottom,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.72.}}
**7** πούς τινος, as _periphrastic_ for a person **as coming,** etc., σὺν πατρὸς μολὼν ποδί, i.e. σὺν πατρί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 661; παρθένου δέχου πόδα [prev. author] “Or.” 1217, compare “Hec.” 977, “HF” 336; χρόνου πόδα [prev. author] “Ba.” 889 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 100}}; also ἐξ ἑνὸς ποδός, i.e. μόνος ὤν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 91}}; οἱ δ᾽ ἀφ᾽ ἡσύχου π., i.e. οἱ ἡσύχως ζῶντες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 217.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, of things, **foot, lowest part,** especially **foot of a hill,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.824, 20.59 (_plural_), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.36, etc.}}; of a table, couch, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 530, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.16, etc.}}; compare πέζ; of the side strokes at the foot of the letter Ω, Callias cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.454a}}; ={ποδεών}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.1, ἀσκοῦ.. λῦσαι π. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 679.}}
**II.2** in a ship, πόδες are **the two lower corners of the sail,** or **the ropes fastened therelo,** by which the sails are tightened or slackened, **sheets** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare (ποδεών 11.4), 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.260}}; χαλᾶν πόδα ease off **the sheet,** as is done when a squall is coming, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 707}}; τοῦ ποδὸς παρίει let go hold **of it,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 436; ἐκδοῦναι ὀλίγον τοῦ ποδός 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 3}}; ἐκπετάσουσι πόδα ναός (with reference to the sail), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1135 (Lyric poetry): opposed to τεῖναι πόδα}} haul **it** tight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 715}}; ναῦς ἐνταθεῖσα ποδί a ship with her **sheet** close hauled, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 706; κὰδ᾽ δ᾽.. λαῖφος ἐρυσσάμενοι τανύοντο ἐς πόδας ἀμφοτέρους 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.932; ἱστία.. ἐτάνυσσαν ὑπ᾽ ἀμφοτέροισι πόδεσσι 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 9.438.}}
**II.2.b** perhaps of the **rudder** or **steering-paddle,** αἰεὶ γὰρ πόδα νηὸς ἐνώμων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.32 (cf. Scholia (at [prev. work])); πὰρ ποδὶ ναός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.55.}}
**III** **a foot,** as a measure of length, = {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4 palms (παλασταί) or 6 fingers, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.149, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 82c, etc.}}
**IV** **foot** in Prosody, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1323 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 400a, 4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Harmonica” p.34 M., 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 3.1, etc.}}; so of a metrical **phrase** or **passage,** ἔκμετρα καὶ ὑπὲρ τὸν π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pr.Im.” 18}}; of a long passage declaimed in one breath, κήρυκες ὅταν τὸν καλούμενον πόδα μέλλωσιν ἐρεῖν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.459, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Demon.” 65, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.91.}}
**V** **boundary stone,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator “fragment” 27}}. (Cf. Latin _pes,_ Gothic _fotus,_ etc. 'foot'; related to πέδον as noted by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 706a33.)}}

4229:
** πρᾶγμα**, ατος, τό,
_Ionic dialect_ **πρῆχμα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 688 B 16, C 5 (Chios, 5th c.BC), “GDI” 5598.4 (from Ephesus)}}; also **πρῆγμα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33, +others, but 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} perhaps wrote **πρῆχμα**, which is see{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|l. in 1.133 cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.144a (codex A), and in 3.49, 57 (“POxy.” 1619.326, 379)}}; compare πρήχματος οὐχ ὁσίου imitated from {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus in “epigram Gr.” 1092 (from Erechtheum)}}: (πράσσω):— **deed, act,** the concrete of πρᾶξις, but frequently approaching to the abstract sense, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 116, +others}}; opposed to λόγοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.12, etc.; πραγμάτων ὀρθὰν ὁδόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.46}}; γυναίου π. ἐποίει did the **act** of a woman, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.57, compare 18.24, etc.}}
**II** **occurrence, matter, affair,** πᾶσαν τελευτὰν πράγματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.75, compare “P.” 4.278; τί τοῦδε σοὶ μέτεστι π. λέγε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 575, compare 584; π. τοιόνδε συνηνείχθη γενέσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33, compare 9.92, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64; ἐς μέσον σφι προετίθεε τὸ π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.206; τί δ᾽ εἰδὼς τοῦδε π. πέρ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 747 codices; τὸ π. εἰς ὑπέρδεινόν μοι περιέστη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.111}}; ὁρᾶτε τὸ π., οἷ προελήλυθε κτλ., {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.9, compare 8.7.}}
**II.2** **thing, concrete reality,** ἆρ᾽ ἔστιν αὔλησίς τι π.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 171.1}}; opposed to ὄνομα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 391b, 436a, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.27; δύο π... τοιάδε, οἷον Κρατύλος καὶ Κρατύλου εἰκών 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 432b}}; ὡς ἀργαλέον π. ἐστίν with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1; οὐδὲ π. οὐθέν ἐστι παρὰ τὰ μεγέθη.. τὰ αἰσθητὰ κεχωρισμένον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 432a3, compare “Mete.” 379a32, “Ph.” 226b30, 227b28; διαμάχονται περὶ τοῦ λευκὸν ἢ μὴ λευκὸν εἶναι τὸ π. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1109d, compare 1112d; ἐξ ἀζωΐας καὶ ζωῆς συγκείμενον π. 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 21: _plural_, τῶν π. ἀΐδιος ἔσσα 5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 6, compare 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 164, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Xenophane” 974a25, “Pol.” 1252a24; τὰ μετέωρα π. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 228, compare 250, 741}}; opposed to ὀνόματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 39o e, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.15.}}
**II.2.b** contemptuously, **thing, creature,** κακῷ πράγματι wretched **creature,** _i.e. _ the sophist, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 312c}}; τούτῳ τῷ π., _i.e. _ the demos, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 520b; ὁ δῆμος ἀσταθμητότατον π. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.136}}; ἄμαχον π., of a woman, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.36.}}
**II.3** like{πρᾶξις}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.2, ὄφελος 1, in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, πρῆγμά ἐστι or ἐστί μοι}} with _infinitive_, it is **advantageous** for me, εὕρισκε π. οἱ εἶναι ἐλαύνειν {{Refs|1.79, compare 4.11}}: with a _negative_, εὕρισκέ οἱ οὐ π. εἶναι στρατεύεσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.12: also with _accusative_ et}}_infinitive_, οὐδὲν ἂν εἴη π. γνώμας ἐμὲ σοὶ ἀποφαίνεσθαι there will be no **advantage** or **need,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.207}}; π. ἂν ἦν μοῦνον the only **thing needful,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.130.}}
**II.4** **thing of consequence** or **importance,** π. ποιήσασθαι [τι{{Refs|] [prev. work] 150; π. οὐδὲν ἐποιήσαντο [prev. author] 6.63}}; οὐδὲν π., ὦ Σώκρατες no **matter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 447b, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 451; ὡς.. οὐδὲν ὂν π., εἰ καὶ ἀποθάνοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4d}}; δῆλον ἦν ὅτι π. τι εἴη that there was **something the matter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.17.}}
**II.4.b** π. ἐστί μοι it **concerns** me, σφίσι τε καὶ Ἀθηναίοισι εἶναι οὐδὲν π. they had **nothing to do** with the {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.84, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.283}}; ᾧ μὴ π. μὴ εἰσίναι no admittance except on **business,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Sammelb. 6152.22 (1st c.BC):}} with _genitive_ _of things_, οἷς μηδὲν ἦν π. τοῦ πολέμου who were not **concerned** in the war, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 65}}; τὸ σὸν τί ἐστι π.; what is your **pursuit** or **business,** what are you about? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20c, cf. Alcaeus1.104d.}}
**II.4.c** μέγα π. a man **of consequence,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.15; τὸ μέγα π. ἐν τῇ πόλει 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 175}}; ἦν μέγιστον π. Δημοκήδης παρὰ βασιλέϊ he was made much of by the king, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.132}}; π. μέγα φρέατος a **fine large** tank, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 179.}}
**II.5** used of a battle, **action, affair,** ὡς οἱ σωθέντες ἐκ τοῦ π. ἀπέφυγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.17.}}
**II.6** τὸ π. the love-**affair** of Harmodius and Aristogeiton, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.132.}}
**II.7** **fact,** opposed to λόγος, ὄνομα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 146a3, “SE” 175a8; πρὸς τὸ π. καὶ τὴν ἀλήθειαν [prev. author] “Ph.” 263a17: _plural_, οὐκ ἐχρῆν τῶν π. τὴν γλῶσσαν ἰσχύειν πλέον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1188.}}
**II.8** **matter in hand, question,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 39; πρὸς τὸ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 87a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 70a32, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.26; διαιρεῖν κατὰ τὸ π. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1299b18; ἔξω τοῦ π., see at {ἔξω} 1.2b.}}]
**III** in _plural_, πράγματα,
**III.1** **circumstances, affairs,** τὰ ἀνθρωπήϊα π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.207; ἐν εἰρήνῃ καὶ ἀγαθοῖς π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82, compare 1.89}}; τοῖς π. τέθνηκα τοῖς δ᾽ ἔργοισιν οὔ by **circumstances,** not by acts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 286; ἐν τοιούτοις π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.2, “An.” 2.1.16, etc.; δεινὸς πράγμασι χρῆσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.1; τύχη τὰ θνητῶν πράγματ᾽, οὐκ εὐβουλία 4th c.BC: Chaeremon Tragicus 2}}; ἀποτυγχάνειν τῶν π. fail **to prosper,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.28}}; the **condition** of a patient, τὰ τῶν νοσεύντων π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 1 (also in _sing._, ἐξαπίνης ὅλῳ τῷ π. μεταβάλλειν [prev. author] “Acut.” 35}}; so also _poetry_, ποῦ ποτ᾽ εἰμὶ πράγματο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 375, compare “Aj.” 314).}}
**III.2** **state-affairs,** τὰ πολιτικὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 31d}}; ἔστ᾽ ἐν ἡμῖν τῆς πόλεως τὰ π. the **fortunes** of the state, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 32}}; a **state** or **empire,** τὰ Περσικὰ π. the Persian **power,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.137; τὰ Περσέων π. [prev. author] 7.50, etc.; διαπεπόρθηται τὰ Περσῶν π. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 714; ἐν ταῖς ναυσὶ τῶν Ἑλλήνων τὰ π. ἐγένετο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.74; μὴ ὥσπερ θεῷ νομίζετ᾽ ἐκείνῳ τὰ παρόντα πεπηγέναι π. ἀθάνατα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.8, compare 44, etc.; παρασπάσασθαί τι τῶν ὅλων π. [prev. author] 1.3}}; of **government,** καταλαμβάνειν τὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.39, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.30 (_passive_); ἔχειν τὰ π. [prev. work] 62, 72,compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.83; κατέχειν τὰ π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.2; ἐς μέσον Πέρσῃσι καταθεῖναι τὰ π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80}}; οἱ ἐν τοῖς π. those who are in **power** or **office,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.28, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.56, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1307b10; οἱ ἐπὶ τοῖς π. ὄντες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.2; οἱ ἐπὶ τῶν π. [prev. author] 18.247, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.69.4, LXX.1Mac.3.32}}; **ἐν** τυραννίδι καὶ πλούτῳ καὶ πράγμασι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.150c}}; τριῶν ἀνδρῶν δημοσίων π. ἀποκαταστάσεως, = Latin _triumvir reipublicae constituendae,_ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Sammelb. 4224.2 (1st c.BC)}}; κοινωνοὶ τῶν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.17; νεώτερα π.}}**innovations,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.59, etc., compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.19}}; but εὐνούστατος τοῖς π. a friend **to things as they are,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.65.}}
**III.3** **fortunes, cause, circumstances,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.236, 237; κοινὰ π. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1062; τέρας γὰρ ὁ βίος καὶ τὰ π. ἐστί μου [prev. author] “Hel.” 260; ἔρρει τἀμὰ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.15, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 280: τὰ π.}} alone, one's **all,** one's **fortunes,** ἐν ᾧπέρ ἐστι πάντα μοι τὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 474}}; ={κτήματα}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus (“Legal” 5 ?) cited in 1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus: in _sing._, φαῦλον γὰρ ἂν εἴη τὸ ἐμὸν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 286e, compare “Cri.” 53d, “Ap.” 42a.}}
**III.4** **business,** especially **lawbusiness,** πρός τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.12, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.128; οὔτε ἐμαυτοῦ οὔτε ἀλλότρια π. πράξας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.3; δικῶν γὰρ οὐ δέομ᾽ οὐδὲ πραγμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1426; πράγματα κἀντιγραφάς [prev. author] “Nu.” 471: _metaphorically_ in _sing._, πονηρὸν τὸ π. ἔχειν}} to have a bad **case,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1415b22.}}
**III.5** in bad sense, **trouble, annoyance,** ἁπάντων αἰτίους τῶν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 310}}; πρήγματα ἔχειν, with _participle_, to have **trouble** about a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.147, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 174b, etc.; π. ἔχειν ἐν τῷ δείπνῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.4, etc.; π. λαμβάνειν [prev. author] “Lac.” 2.9}}; π. παρέχειν τινί to cause one **trouble,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.155, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 20, +others}}: with _infinitive_, cause one the **trouble** of doing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 115a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.46, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 313; πραγμάτων.. ἀπαλλαγείς [prev. author] “Ach.” 269 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 41d, “R.” 406e}}; ἄνευ πραγμάτων, σὺν πράγμασι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.6: less frequently in _sing._, μηδὲν πρῆγμα παρέχειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.239.}}

4230:
** πραγμᾰτ-εία**,
_Ionic dialect_**πρηγμᾰτίη**, _poetry_ **πραγμᾰτίη** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 1.38}}: ἡ:— **prosecution of business, diligent study,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.44, 5.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 408a, +others; πόνων πολλῶν καὶ πραγματείας εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.48; πλείονος εἶναι πρηγματίης 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 7}}; ἡ μάταιος π. [λογισμῶν] this idle **attention to** argumentations, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.7.8}}; μετὰ πολλῆς π. with a great deal of **trouble,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 19.4 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** **occupation, business,** ἡ π. αὐτῆς (i.e. τῆς ῥητορικῆς) ἅπασα.. εἰς τοῦτο τελευτᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 453a}}; ἡ τοῦ διαλέγεσθαι π. the **business** of dialectic, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 161e}}; τοῦ πολιτικοῦ.. πᾶσα ἡ π. περὶ πόλιν [ἐστί] Arist {{Refs|“Pol.” 1274b37, compare “EN” 1105a11}}; ἡ δημηγορικὴ π. the **business** of oratory, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1354b24; ἀπὸ τῆς ἀναισχύντου π. ἀποστῆναι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.242}}; πραγματεῖαι **official duties,** opposed to ἀρχαί, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work]13,compare “PTeb.” 5.143, +others (2nd c.BC)}}; especially **law-business, lawsuit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.18, +others; ἡ περὶ τὰ δικαστήρια π. [prev. author] 15.31}}: _plural_, **affairs** in general, κάτω βλέπειν εἰς ἀνθρώπων πραγματείας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 500c; μεθισταμένων πραγματειῶν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.9 (nisi to be read πραγμάτων)}}; **troubles,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.37, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.28U.}}; πρὸς ἔθνη τὴν π. ἔχειν to have **dealings** with.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.2.2.}}
**II.b** _plural_, **works,** of the buildings of Solomon, {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.9.1.}}
**III** **treatment** of a subject, εἰδέων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 4}}; ἡ τοῦ ἐπιπέδου π., as a definition of plane geometry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 528d}}; ἡ Πλάτωνος π. Plato's **system,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 987a30, compare 986a8, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.3U., 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.17}}; **manner of dealing with,** ἡ περὶ τοὺς μάρτυρας π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1376b4.}}
**III.2** **philosophical argument** or **treatise,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Top.” 100a18, 101a26; τοῦ εἰδέναι χάριν ἡ π. [prev. author] “Ph.” 194b18; ἡ παροῦσα π. οὐ θεωρίας ἕνεκα [prev. author] “EN” 1103b26}}; **the subject of such a treatise,** τρεῖς αἱ π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 198a30, compare “SE” 183b4; ἡ περὶ τῶν ἀγαθῶν ἐκδοθεῖσα π. 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.2, etc.}}
**III.3** **systematic** or **scientific historical treatise,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.1.4, 1.3.1, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.1, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.74, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 13}}; Τρωϊκὴ π. **the legends** of the Trojan war, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Arg. Sophocles Tragicus}}**Aj.;** π. συνέταξεν ἐν δράματι τῶν Δαρδάνου πράξεων τὰς μνημοσύνας {{Refs|“BMus.Inscription” 3.444.18 (from Iasus).}}
**III.4** **magical operation, spell,** ἡ Σολομῶνος π. {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.853, compare776.}}

4231:
** πραγμᾰτ-εύομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ **πρηγμ-**, _aorist_ ἐπραγματευσάμην, _Ionic dialect_ ἐπρηγμ-, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.8.32, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 10.9, etc.}}; also ἐπραγματεύθην, _Ionic dialect_ ἐπρηγμ-, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.87, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.249}}: _perfect_ πεπραγμάτευμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 11.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 99d, 100b, +others}}; also in _passive_ sense, see below:—**busy oneself, take trouble,** ἀπέδωκαν τὸν νεκρὸν οὐδὲν ἔτι πρηγματευθέντες Hdt.[same place],{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 437c; π. περὶ σωφροσύνης [prev. author] “R.” 430d, compare “Cra.” 425c; περὶ τὰ ὄντα [prev. author] “Tht.” 187a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.7, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1102a22, etc.}}; πολλὰ ἐπί τινι π. **work at** at thing, **labour to bring** it **about,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.15; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 398a}}; πραγματεύονται ὅπως ἄρξουσι **exert themselves** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 14.5: _absolutely_, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.4.1}}; μηδὲν πραγματεύου do not **worry,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Char.” 18.9}}: c._infinitive_, **exert oneself** to.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 19.}}
**2** **to be engaged in business, spend one's time in business,** ὅληντὴννύκτα all nightlong, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.26; π.καὶ κακοπαθεῖν τὸν βίον ἅπαντα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1176b29, compare 1122a9}}; simply, **conduct a business,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 199.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; **transact business,** of clerks, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 647.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; π. ἀπὸ ἐμπορίας καὶ δανεισμῶν **make money** by trade and loans, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 50, compare “Sull.” 17, etc.}}; οἱ πραγματευόμενοι, = Latin _negotiatores_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 532.6 (from Galatia), compare “SIG” 797.10 (Assus, 1st c.AD): with _accusative_, π. τὸ συνηγορικὸν καὶ (τὸ) ἐπιδέκατον, of a tax-farmer, “Ostr.” 1537 (2nd c.BC), “PLeid.F” in “Ostr.” i ; τὴν ὑϊκὴν π. “PSI” 4.384.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; generally, of officials, **to be employed in public affairs,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PGnom. 174 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _of things_, **take in hand, treat laboriously, be engaged in,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 361d, “Hp.Ma.” 304c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.26, etc.}}; **undertake,** τὸν δεύτερον πλοῦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 99d.}}
**II.2** of authors, **elaborate** a work, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 526}}; of a science, **work out,** ἃ θέλει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 4}}; **treat of,** περὶ φύσεως πάντα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 989b33, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 15.34; περί τινος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 193b31; περί τι [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1025b17, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.96K., +others}}; τοιαύτην οὐκ ἐπραγματεύθησαν ἀκριβολογίαν περὶ τὰς φλέβας **did** not **use** such precision **in treating** of.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 513a9.}}
**II.3** of historians, **treat systematically,** τὰς πράξεις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.4.3: _absolutely_, οἱ πραγματευόμενοι}}**systematic historians,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.33.5, etc.}}
**II.4** simply, **write, treat,** ποιητὴς ὢν πεπραγμάτευται περὶ τὸ ἱερόν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 11(4).544.5 (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}; τὰ πεπραγματευμένα ὑπ᾽ αὐτῶ his **works, composilions**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 721.8 (Crete, 2nd-1st c.BC), compare 702.5 (Delph., 2nd c.BC).}}
**III** _passive_, mostly _perfect_ πεπραγμάτευμαι, **to be laboured at, elaborated,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22b, “Prm.” 129e}}; δόρυ ὡσαύτως -ευμένον X {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Eq.” 8.10; αἱ εἰς τὸν παῖδα -ευμέναι μεταφοραί 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.167}}; also _present_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1215a30.}}

4232:
** πραιτώριον**, τό,
= Latin _Praetorium, official residence of a governor,_ {{NT|NT.Matt.27.27}}; later, of private **residences,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 159 “Praef.”}}
**II** **praetorian guard**: ἔπαρχος πραιτωρίον, τοῦ π., = **praefectus praetorio**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 707 (Tyre, 2nd c.AD), “IG” 14.911, etc.}}
**II.2** **imperial household, Ep. Phil.**1.13.

4233:
** πράκ-τωρ**, ορος, ὁ,
={πρακτήρ}, **one who does** or **executes, accomplisher,** Ζεὺς ὅτου π. φανῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 251; π. τῶν ἀκουσίων 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.6}}; with _feminine_ _substantive_, Κύπρις.. τῶνδ᾽ ἐφάνη π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 861 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **official who executes a judgment for debt,** especially **public debt, bailiff,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.75.49, +others, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.49, Decrees cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.77, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.28, “IG” 12(8).51.9 (Imbros, 2nd c.BC), “OGI” 483.7 (Pergam.), NT.Luke.12.58; βασιλικὸς π. “PSI” 4.335.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; τῶν ξενικῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 5.222 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.2** **collector** of taxes, π. βαλανήου “Ostr.” in Wilcken **Grundzüge** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|p.213 (1st c.AD), “Ostr.” 399 (1st c.AD)}}; π. ἀργυρικῶν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PIand.” 29.1 (2nd c.AD), “BGU” 434.3 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; π. σιτικῶν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.367a1, +others (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.3** in Poets, **one who exacts punishment, avenger,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 647 (Lyric poetry); π. αἵματος [prev. author] “Eu.” 319 (_anapaest meter_); φόνου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 953}}: as _adjective_, with a _feminine_ _substantive_, **avenging,** σὺν δορὶ καὶ χερὶ πράκτορι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 111 (Lyric poetry).}}

4234:
** πρᾶξις**, εως,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **πρῆξις**, ιος, ἡ: (πράσσω):— **doing, transaction, business,** [πλεῖν] κατὰ πρῆξιν **on a trading voyage,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.72; ἐπὶ πρῆξιν ἔπλεον “hymnus ad Apollinem” 397}}; π. δ᾽ ἥδ᾽ ἰδίη, οὐ δήμιος a private, not a public **affair,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.82; π. μηδὲ φίλοισιν ὅμως ἀνακοινέο πᾶσιν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 73}}; ἡ περί τινος π. the **transaction** respecting.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88.}}
**2** **result** or **issue of a business,** especially **good result, success,** οὐ γάρ τις πρῆξις πέλεται.. γόοιο no **good** comes of weeping, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.524; οὔ τις π. ἐγίγνετο μυρομένοισιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.202}}; λυμαίνεσθαί τινι τὴν π. to spoil one's **market,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.16}}; π. φίλαν δίδοι grant a happy **issue.** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.85; π. οὐρίαν θέλων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 814 (Lyric poetry)}}; ταχεῖά γ᾽ ἦλθε χρησμῶν π. their **issue,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 739; ἄνευ τούτων οὐκ ἂν εἵη π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 2.2; δὸς πόρον καὶ π. τῷ τόπῳ τούτῳ “PMag.Par.” 1.2366.}}
**II** **doing,** τῶν ἀγαθῶν (of persons) πρήξιες {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1026; ἡ τῶν ἀγαθῶν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 163e; ἡ π. τῶν ἔργων 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.9}}; **achievement,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.114}}; π. πολεμική, πολιητική, πολιτική, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 399a, “Sph.” 266d, “Men.” 99b}}; **action,** opposed to πάθος, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 876d}}; opposed to ἕξις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 434a}}; **moral action,** opposed to ποίησις, τέχνη, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1140a2, 1097a16}}; opposed to ποιότης, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Po.” 1450a18, compare “EN” 1178a35 (_plural_); ἤθη καὶ πάθη καὶ π. [prev. author] “Po.” 1447a28}}; opposed to οἱ πολιτικοὶ λόγοι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.44; ἔργῳ καὶ πράξεσιν, οὐχὶ λόγοις [prev. author] 6.3}}; ἐν ταῖς πράξεσι ὄντα τε καὶ πραττόμενα exhibited **in actual life,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 271d}}; **action** in drama, opposed to λόγος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1454a18; μία π. ὅλη καὶ τελεία [prev. work] 1459a19, compare 1451b33 (_plural_).}}
**II.2** **action, exercise,** χειρῶν, σκελῶν, στόματος, φωνῆς, διανοίας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 192a.}}
**II.3** euphemistic for **sexual intercourse,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 127, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.158, etc.; in full, ἡ π. ἡ γεννητική 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 539b20.}}
**II.4** magical **operation, spell,** {{Refs|PMag.Par. 1.1227, +others, “PMag.Lond.” 125.40.}}
**III** **action, act,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 895 (Lyric poetry, _plural_), “OC” 560, etc.; μιᾶς μόνον μνησθήσομαι π. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.127.}}
**III.b** **military action, battle,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.19.11, etc.}}
**IV** **doing, faring** well or ill, **fortune, state, condition,** ἀπέκλαιε.. τὴν ἑωυτοῦ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.65, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 695 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 790, 792; εύτυχὴς π. [prev. author] “Tr.” 294; κακαὶ π. [prev. author] “Ant.” 1305.}}
**V** **practical ability,** π. καὶ σύνεσις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.47.5; ἡ ἐν τοῖς πολεμικοῖς π. [prev. author] 4.77.1.}}
**V.2** **practice,** i.e. **trickery, treachery,** ἐπὶ τὴν πόλιν {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.9.2}}; κατὰ τῆς πόλεως, ἐπὶ τοὺς Αἰτωλούς, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.71.6, 5.96.4.}}
**VI** **exaction of money, recovery of debts, arrears,** etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.57.13, +others; συμβολαίων πράξεις 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.88; τοῦ μισθοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328b; τελῶν [prev. author] “R.” 425d (_plural_)}}; παρὰ Ἀρτέμωνος.. ἔστω ἡ π. τοῖς δανείσασι let the lenders have **an action of recovery** against Artemon, Syngr. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.12, compare “SIG” 364.61,67 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC), “Test.Epict.” 5.31; ἡ π. ἔστω καθάπερ ἐκ δίκης “PEleph.” 1.12 (4th c.BC), etc.}}; αἱ π. τῶν καταδικασθέντων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1321b42.}}
**VI.2** **exaction of vengeance, retribution,** βαρβάρων χάριν γάμων πρᾶξιν ὡς Ἑλλὰς λάβοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 272 (Lyric poetry).}}
**VII** **public office,** ἡ διοικηθεῖσα π. Epist. Macrin. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 5.1.2.}}
**VIII** **discourse, lecture** of a rhetorician or philosopher, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.59c, 5th-6th c.AD: Marinus Biographus “Procl.” 22.}}

4235:
** πρᾶος**, ον,
also **πρᾱΰς**, _Ionic dialect_ **πρηΰς**, εῖα, ΰ:—**πρᾶος** supplies _singular_ in _Attic dialect_, Trag., and Comedy texts, except that the _feminine_ is always πραεια (πρᾶος as _feminine_ only in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.168d)}}; but _singular_ **πραΰς**, _Ionic dialect_ **πρηΰς**, is used in _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry (also in {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus and usually in LXX, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus, etc.)}}:—_plural_, _nominative_ πρηέες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.10, πρηεῖς “Anthologia Graeca” 5.208 (3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus or 3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus), πρᾶοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562d, etc.; _feminine_ πρηεῖαι “Anthologia Graeca” 6.244 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus); _neuter_ πραέα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 15.4, “Eq.” 9.10, etc., πρᾶα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 488b22, _falsa lectio_ in 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.351; _genitive_ πραέων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.9; _dative_ πραέσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 888a, 930a, _feminine_ πραίαις}} written for πραείαις {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“IG” 7.3101 (Lebad., 3rd c.AD)}}; _accusative_ πραεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.37.7, πράους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.55}}: _comparative_ πραότερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.21, πραΰτερος 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 153, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 85a, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ πρηΰτ- {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.181}}: _superlative_ πραότατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116c, etc., πραΰτατος “Syria” 5.337 (from Sidon)}}; _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ πρηΰτατος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.937, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.349 (1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus); πράϋστος “MAMA” 1.237 (from Phrygia)}}. (The ι subscr. is frequently written in codices, but Πρᾶος is written in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.1928.20 (_proper name_, 4th c.BC), “IGRom.” 4.504 (Pergam.), compare 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., “Etymologicum Gudianum” 478.31}}; πραύτερος is found once in codices, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ti.” [prev. cited] (proved wrong by πραΰς “Comica Adespota” in “Gött. Nachr.” 1922.31, πραέα (_neuter_ _plural_) in “PCair.Zen.” 33.12 (3rd c.BC), πραέως “UPZ” 144.6 (2nd c.BC), and by the absence of iota in _Ionic dialect_ πρηΰς))}}:—**mild, soft, gentle** (not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” or 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”):}}
**1** of things, πρηῢ σέλας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“H.Hom.” 8.10; πραῢς ὄαρος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.136}}; **mild,** πυρετοί Hp.[same place]; ἰητρείη πρηεῖα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Art.” 69}}; of sound, **soft, gentle,** τὴν φωνὴν πραοτέραν ποιοῦνται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.10; ἀνέμων πρηΰτατε Ζέφυρε “Anthologia Graeca” 6.349 (1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus)}}; ὠδῖνες{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 244 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus); κέντρον [prev. work] 229 ([prev. author]).}}
**2** of persons, **mild, gentle, meek,** πραῢς ἀστοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.71; πᾶσιν ἵλεώς τε καὶ πρᾶος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 566e}}; πρὸς τοὺς οἰκείους π.{{Refs|[prev. work] 375c; π. τὸ ἦθος [prev. author] “Phdr.” 243c; π. ἐν τοῖς λόγοις [prev. author] “Euthd.” 303d}}; especially after having been angry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.181 (_comparative_)}}; ὁ θὴρ ὅδ᾽ ἡμῖν π., of Dionysus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 436}}; of a horse, **gentle,** ἀλλήλοις πραότεροι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.29}}; of other animals, **tame,** ἰχθύων μεγάλων καὶ πραέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 1.4.9, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 488b22; ζῷα.. πραέα πρὸς τοὺς ἀνθρώπους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 15.4: proverbial, πραΰτερος μολόχας 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 153; also, τόπος ἡμερώτερος καὶ πραότερος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.67.}}
**3** of actions, feelings, etc., **mild,** τιμωρίαι πραότεραι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 867b; πραότεραι ἡδοναί [prev. work] 815e; λόγοι, ἤθη, φύσις, [prev. work] 888a, 930a, “R.” 375c; ὄσσοισι πρηέσιν δέρκευ “BMus.Inscription” 921b8 (Branchidae, 1st-2nd c.AD)}}; τὰ πραέα **caresses,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.10}}; πραότερα πάσχειν {{Refs|Pl “Cri.” 49b.}}
**II** **making mild, taming,** φάρμακον πραῢ τείνων ἀμφὶ γένυι, of a bridle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.85; προκινεῖν αὐτὸν [τὸν ἵππον] ὡς πραοτάτοις σημείοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.3.}}
**III** _adverb_ **πράως**, **mildly, gently,** πράως πείθειν τινά, φέρειν τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 589c, “Cri.” 43b; πράως ἔχειν πρός τι [prev. author] “Ly.” 211e}}; πράως λέγειν τὸ πάθος to speak **lightly** of it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.14}}; πράως διακεῖσθαι, opposed to ὀργίζεσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.183; πράως, μὴ πικρῶς [prev. author] 18.265}}; in physical sense, **gently,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 134: _comparative_, πραότερον προδιδάσκειν, κολάζειν, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 489d, “Phd.” 94d}}; πραοτέρως (variant{-υτέρως}) ἔχειν τινί {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.6.4}}: _superlative_, φέρειν.. ὡς πραότωτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 387e}}: later form **πραέως** (πράως [Ἀττικοί], οὐ πραέως {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.), “UPZ” 144.6 (2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.36, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.13, uncertain in “Comica Adespota” 336.5 (compare πρατίας)}}, etc.: _Ionic dialect_ **πρηέως** conjecture in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 46 (πράως and πραέως codices)}}. (Cogn. with Sanskrit _prīṇā´ti_ 'love, give pleasure to', Gothic _frijon_ 'love', **frijonds** 'friend'.)

4237:
** πρᾰσι-ά**,
_Ionic dialect_ **πρᾰσ-ιή**, ἡ, **bed in a garden, garden-plot,** {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.127, 24.247, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.4.3, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 532, LXX.Sir.24.31, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.17, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 9.6; ἀνθῶν πρασιαί 3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 4.2: _metaphorically_, πρασιαὶ πρασιαί}} **in companies** or **groups,** {{NT|NT.Mark.6.40. (Prob. from πράσον}} and so properly **bed of leeks.**)
**II** a surgical instrument, {{Refs|“Hermes” 38.283.}}

4238:
** πράσσω**,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **πρήσσω**, _Attic dialect_ **πράττω** (first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.7.11, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus and 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus)}}, Cretan dialect **πράδδω** {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 1.35}}: _future_ πράξω, _Ionic dialect_ πρήξω: _aorist_ ἔπραξα, _Ionic dialect_ ἔπρηξα: _perfect_ πέπραχα, _Ionic dialect_ πέπρηχα, (_transitive_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.106, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.32, “Cyr.” 3.1.15, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.21, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 619, “IG” 9(2).517.36 (Larissa, 3rd c.BC), “PHib.” 1.80.11 (3rd c.BC), (_intransitive_) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 187 codices, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1440a36}}: _pluperfect_ ἐπεπράχει (ν) (_transitive_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus [same place], (_intransitive_) 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.83}}: _perfect_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2 πέπρᾱγα, _Ionic dialect_ πέπρηγα, (_intransitive_) 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.73, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.172, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 629, “Ra.” 302, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.4.2, (_transitive_) 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1168b35, +others, “SIG” 364.70 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC)}}: _pluperfect_ ἐπεπράγεσαν (_intransitive_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.4, 7.24}}:— _perfect_ πέπραγα _Attic dialect_, πέπραχα Hellenistic, _accusative_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|to Moer. p.293 P., 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.103 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}, but see above:—_middle_, _future_ πράξομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator “fragment” 67, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.36 (also in _passive_ sense, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.243 (probably), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 452a)}}: _aorist_ ἐπραξαμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 287, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.65, etc.:}} —_passive_, _future_ (see.above), also πραχθήσομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.98, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1359a11, etc.}}; _future_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3 πεπράξομαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 861, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 847, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 9.3 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}: _aorist_ ἐπράχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 679, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.54, etc.}}: _perfect_ πέπραγμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 75, etc. (sometimes in middle sense, see below vi). [ᾱ by nature, as is shown by the _Ionic dialect_ form πρήσσω, and by the accent in πρᾶγμα, πρᾶξις, etc.]}}
**I** in _Epic dialect_ only, **pass through, pass over,** δὶς τόσσον ἅλα πρήσσοντες ἀπῆμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.491; ῥίμφα πρήσσοντε κέλευθον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.282, 23.501; ῥίμφα πρήσσουσι κέλευθον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.83; ὁδὸν πρήσσουσιν ὁδῖται “hymnus ad Mercurium” 203: with _genitive_, ἵνα πρήσσωμεν ὁδοῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.264, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.219}}; ὄφρα πρ. ὁδοῖο{{Refs|[prev. work] 47; ἵνα πρήσσῃσιν ὁδοῖο 3.476}}: Grammars note that this sense is found only in _present_, {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 1.355, “Etymologicum Magnum” 688.1.}}
**II** **experience certain for-** **tunes, fare** well or ill, ὁ στόλος οὕτως ἔπρηξε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.26, compare 4.77, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.24; so ὡς ἔπρηξε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.18; κατὰ νόον π. [prev. author] 4.97, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 549; πράξασαν ὡς ἔπραξεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1288; εὖ πέπραγεν, ὅτι.. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.73, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, 42, etc.; φλαύρως π. τῷ στόλῳ [prev. author] 6.94; π. καλῶς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 979; χαλεπώτατα π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.95; ταπεινῶς π. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.64; ὅστις καλῶς πράττει, οὐχὶ καὶ εὖ πράττε; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.116b; π. εὐτυχῶς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 701; κάλλιστα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 794}}; μακαρίως, εὐδαιμόνως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 629, 802}}: frequently with _neuter_ _pronoun_ or _adjective_, εὖ π. τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1006, compare “OC” 391; μηδὲν εὖ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.8; χρηστόν τι π. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 341; καλά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16; χείρω [prev. author] 7.71; μεγάλα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 346; πάντ᾽ ἀγαθά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 302, compare “Eq.” 683 (Lyric poetry); εὐδαίμονα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1359 (_anapaest meter_); πολλὰ καὶ ἀγαθά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.8; οἷον ἥθελεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1704 (Lyric poetry); πράξας ἅπερ ηὔχου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 355 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.9.14.}}
**III** **achieve, effect, accomplish,** οὔ τι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.562, 11.552, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.191, etc.; οὐδέ τι ἔργον ἐνθάδ᾽ ἔτι πρήξει 19.324, compare 16.88; χρῆμα μὲν οὐ πρήξεις, σὺ δ᾽ ἐτώσια πόλλ᾽ ἀγορεύσεις 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 402}}; κλέος ἔπραξεν **won** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).8}}; ἔπραξε δεσμόν **achieved** bondage, i.e. brought it on himself, {{Refs|[prev. author] “P.” 2.40; τινὰ Νηρεΐδων π. ἄκοιτιν [prev. author] “N.” 5.36}}; ὕμνον π. **grant power of** song, {{Refs|[prev. work] 9.3}}; λεόντεσσι π. φόνον **do** slaughter upon them,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 3.46; τὴν Κυπρίων ἀπόστασιν π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.113}}; π. εἰρήνην, φιλίαν, **bring** it **about,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.7, 18.162}}; π. τι παρά τινος **get** something from.., ὧν δέονται πάντων πεπραγότες εἶεν παρὰ βασιλέως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.4.2; ἐλπὶς πράξειν τι παρὰ τῶν θεῶν ἀγαθόν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.20}}; also, **attempt, plot,** δήμου κατάλυσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.6: with _dative_ _person_, δαίμοσιν π. φίλα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 660; Αοξίᾳ χάριν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 37, compare 896 (Lyric poetry), “El.” 1133, etc.; σὺ τοῦτο πράξεις ὥστε..; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 896}}:—_passive_, πέπρακται τοὖργον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pr.” 75; φεῦ φεῦ πέπρακται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 680; τὰ πεπραγμένα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.15, etc.; ἡ ἐπὶ τοῖς πεπρ. ἀδοξία 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.11; τὰ πεπρ. λῦσαι [prev. author] 24.76; τὰ πραχθέντα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 683, etc.}}; τὰ ἔργα τῶν πραχθέντων the facts **of what took place,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.22; οὐ γὰρ ἂν τό γε πραχθὲν ἀγένητον θείη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 324b.}}
**III.2** _absolutely_, **effect an object, be successful,** δὸς Τηλέμαχον πρήξαντα νέεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.60; ἔπρηξας καὶ ἔπειτα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.357.}}
**III.3** of sexual intercourse, ἐπράχθη τὰ μέγιστα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.143.}}
**III.4** **to be busy with,** σὺ μὲν τὰ σαυτῆς πρᾶσσ᾽ **mind** your own **business,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 678; πράττων ἔκαστος τὸ αὑτοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 247a, compare “Plt.” 307e; τὰ αὑτοῦ π. καὶ μὴ πολυπραγμονεῖν [prev. author] “R.” 433a, compare 400e, etc. (whereas πολλὰ π. ={πολυπραγμονεῖν}, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 266, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 228, etc.); φιλοσόφου τὰ αὑτοῦ πράξαντος καὶ οὐ πολυπραγμονήσαντος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 526c, compare “Ap.” 33a, etc.}}]; οὐδ᾽ εὖ.. οἰκοῦνται αἱ πόλεις, ὅταν τὰ αὑτῶν ἕκαστοι πράττωσι (ironical) {{Refs|[prev. author] Alcaeus1.127b}}; μὴ τὰ αὑτῶν π. not to **act** their **part,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 452c; π. τὰ δέοντα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.8.1.}}
**III.5** **manage** affairs, **do** business, **act,** εἰπεῖν τε καὶ πρᾶξαι {{Refs|[prev. work] 2.9.4, compare 2.8.6}}; πράττειν τὰ πολιτικὰ πράγματα, τὰ τῆς πόλεως, **manage** state-affairs, take part in government, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 31d, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.20; τὰ Ἀθηναίων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 216a; οἱ τὰ κοινὰ π. καὶ πολιτευόμενοι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1324b1: _absolutely_, without any addition, ἱκανωτάτω λέγειν τε καὶ πράττειν, of able statesmen, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.15, compare 4.2.1, 4; πολιτεύεσθαι καὶ π. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.45, compare 59, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317a.}}
**III.6** generally, **transact, negotiate, manage,** οἱ πράξαντες πρὸς αὐτὸν τὴν λῆψιν τῆς πόλεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.114}}; Θηβαίοις τὰ πράγματα π. **manage** matters for their interest, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.77:}} —so in _passive_, τῷ Ἱπποκράτει τὰ.. πράγματα ἀπό τινων ἀνδρῶν.. ἐπράσσετο matters **were negotiated** with him by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.76}}: but frequently _absolutely_, **treat, negotiate, manage, act,** οἱ πράσσοντες αὐτῷ {{Refs|[prev. work] 110, compare 5.76; π. πρός τινα [prev. author] 2.5, 4.73, etc.}}; ἐς (variant{πρὸς}) τοὺς βαρβάρους, ἐς τοὺς Εἵλωτας, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.131, 132}}:—_passive_, ἐπράττετο οὐ πρὸς τοὺς ἄλλους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.64; also π. τι ὑπὲρ τῶν κοινῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.2; π. ὑπὲρ τῆς πόλεως τὰ πάτρια [prev. author] 59.73; π. περὶ εἰρήνης 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.3; π. τῇ δύναιτο ἄριστα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.30; π. ὡς ἄριστα καὶ πιστότατα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.129; οἱ πράσσοντες}}**the traitors,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.89, 113}}:—followed by dependent clauses, πρᾶσσε καὶ τὰ ἐμὰ καὶ τὰ σὰ ὅπῃ κάλλιστα ἕξει {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.129; ἐς τὴν Πελοπόννησον ἔπρασσεν, ὅπῃ ὠφελία τις γενήσεται [prev. work] 65; π. ὅπως πόλεμος γένηται [prev. work] 57; π. ὅπως τιμωρήσονται [prev. work] 56, compare 3.4, 70}}, etc.: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, μὴ δεῦρο πλεῖν τὴν ναῦν ἔπραττεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.22.}}
**III.6.b** especially of secret practices and intrigues, εἴ τι μὴ ξὺν ἀργύρῳ ἐπράσσετ᾽ ἐνθένδ᾽ unless some bribery **was being practised,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 125; καί τι αὐτῷ καὶ ἐπράσσετο ἐς τὰς πόλεις προδοσίας πέρι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.121, compare 5.83; μετάστασις ἐπράττετο 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.10; τούτοις ἔπρασσον τὴν πόλιν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.17.12}}; νῦν δ᾽ αὔτ᾽ Ἀτρεῖδαι φωτὶ παντουργῷ φρένας ἔπραξαν **have jobbed them** (the arms) **away** to a villain, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 446.}}
**IV** **practise,** πόνῳ π. θεοδμάτους ἀρετάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).11; δίκαια ἢ ἄδικα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28b, etc.; ταῦτ᾽ ἔπραξάν τε καὶ ἔλεξαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.1; ἃ καὶ λέγειν ὀκνοῦμεν οἱ πεπραχότες 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 619}}: then _absolutely_, **act,** π. ἔργῳ μὲν σθένος βουλαῖσι δὲ φρήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.26}}; ὡς πράττοντες as **doing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 527a}}; μεθ᾽ ἡμῶν ἔπραττεν, i. e. he took our side, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.14.}}
**IV.2** **study,** δράματα {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} see at {Ἀριστοφάνη; συλλογισμούς} {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.17.27}}; ἐν τοῖς πραττομένοις in the poems **which are now studied, made the subject of commentaries,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Scholia 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 11.}}
**V** with _double_ _accusative_ _person_ et _of things_, πράττειν τινά τι **do** something **to** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1394, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.93; ἀγαθόν τι π. τὴν πόλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 108.}}
**VI** **exact payment from** one, αὐτοὺς ἑκατὸν τάλαντα ἔπρηξαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.58}}; πράσσει με τόκον he **makes** me **pay** interest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 185; π. τινὰ χρέος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.7, compare “P.” 9.104; ὅσοι πράξεις πεπράγασιν “SIG” 364.70 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC)}}; τοὐφειλόμενον π. Δίκη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 311 (_anapaest meter_); ἀντίποινα [prev. author] “Pers.” 476}}: frequently of tax-gatherers or other collectors of public debt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.116.16, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 774d; π. τὰς εἰσφοράς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.77, etc.}}; φόρον ἔπρησσον παρ᾽ ἑκάστων **obtained** or **demanded** from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.106}}: with _accusative_ _person_, **press for payment,** μὴ π. τοὺς ὀφειλέτας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 38.11.10}}; π. τινά τι ὑπέρ τινος **demand** from one **as the price** for a thing, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 18: _metaphorically_, φόνον π.}} **exact punishment** or **vengeance for** a murder: hence, **avenge, punish,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 624; τὰ περὶ τὸν φόνον ἀγριωτέρως π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 867d:}} —_passive_, ὑπὸ βασιλέως πεπραγμένος φόρους **called on to pay up** the tribute, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.5}}; πραχθεὶς ὑπὸτῶνδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.21 codices, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 921c}}:—_middle_, **exact for oneself,** πράξασθαί τινα μισθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).30}}; ἀργύριον, χρήματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.65, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 561, etc.; τὴν διπλασίαν π. τὸν ὑποφεύγοντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 762b, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.54.11; π. τοὺς ἐξάγοντας τριακοστήν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.32; πράσσεσθαι χρέος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator “fragment” 67}}; φόρους πράσσεσθαι ἀπό, ἐκ τῶν πόλεων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.5, 37; παρ᾽ αὐτῶν ἃ ὤφειλον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.11}}: _metaphorically_ of exacting punishment, etc., μεγάλ᾽ ἀντ᾽ ὀλίγων ἐπράξαο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Lavacrum Palladis” 91:}} —_passive_ _perfect_ and _pluperfect_ in _middle_ sense, εἰ μὲν ἐπεπράγμην τοῦτον τὴν δίκην if I **had exacted** from him the full amount, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.2.}}
**VII** with _accusative_ _person_, πράττειν τινά **deal with, finish off,** euphemistic, ἔπρασσε δ᾽ ᾇπέρ νιν, ὧδε θάπτει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 440 (Lyric poetry); πεπραγμένοι is _falsa lectio_ [prev. work] 132.}}

4239:
Included with: ** πρᾶος**, ον,
also **πρᾱΰς**, _Ionic dialect_ **πρηΰς**, εῖα, ΰ:—**πρᾶος** supplies _singular_ in _Attic dialect_, Trag., and Comedy texts, except that the _feminine_ is always πραεια (πρᾶος as _feminine_ only in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.168d)}}; but _singular_ **πραΰς**, _Ionic dialect_ **πρηΰς**, is used in _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry (also in {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus and usually in LXX, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus, etc.)}}:—_plural_, _nominative_ πρηέες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.10, πρηεῖς “Anthologia Graeca” 5.208 (3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus or 3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus), πρᾶοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562d, etc.; _feminine_ πρηεῖαι “Anthologia Graeca” 6.244 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus); _neuter_ πραέα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 15.4, “Eq.” 9.10, etc., πρᾶα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 488b22, _falsa lectio_ in 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.351; _genitive_ πραέων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.9; _dative_ πραέσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 888a, 930a, _feminine_ πραίαις}} written for πραείαις {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“IG” 7.3101 (Lebad., 3rd c.AD)}}; _accusative_ πραεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.37.7, πράους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.55}}: _comparative_ πραότερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.21, πραΰτερος 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 153, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 85a, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ πρηΰτ- {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.181}}: _superlative_ πραότατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116c, etc., πραΰτατος “Syria” 5.337 (from Sidon)}}; _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ πρηΰτατος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.937, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.349 (1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus); πράϋστος “MAMA” 1.237 (from Phrygia)}}. (The ι subscr. is frequently written in codices, but Πρᾶος is written in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.1928.20 (_proper name_, 4th c.BC), “IGRom.” 4.504 (Pergam.), compare 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., “Etymologicum Gudianum” 478.31}}; πραύτερος is found once in codices, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ti.” [prev. cited] (proved wrong by πραΰς “Comica Adespota” in “Gött. Nachr.” 1922.31, πραέα (_neuter_ _plural_) in “PCair.Zen.” 33.12 (3rd c.BC), πραέως “UPZ” 144.6 (2nd c.BC), and by the absence of iota in _Ionic dialect_ πρηΰς))}}:—**mild, soft, gentle** (not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” or 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”):}}
**1** of things, πρηῢ σέλας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“H.Hom.” 8.10; πραῢς ὄαρος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.136}}; **mild,** πυρετοί Hp.[same place]; ἰητρείη πρηεῖα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Art.” 69}}; of sound, **soft, gentle,** τὴν φωνὴν πραοτέραν ποιοῦνται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.10; ἀνέμων πρηΰτατε Ζέφυρε “Anthologia Graeca” 6.349 (1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus)}}; ὠδῖνες{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 244 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus); κέντρον [prev. work] 229 ([prev. author]).}}
**2** of persons, **mild, gentle, meek,** πραῢς ἀστοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.71; πᾶσιν ἵλεώς τε καὶ πρᾶος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 566e}}; πρὸς τοὺς οἰκείους π.{{Refs|[prev. work] 375c; π. τὸ ἦθος [prev. author] “Phdr.” 243c; π. ἐν τοῖς λόγοις [prev. author] “Euthd.” 303d}}; especially after having been angry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.181 (_comparative_)}}; ὁ θὴρ ὅδ᾽ ἡμῖν π., of Dionysus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 436}}; of a horse, **gentle,** ἀλλήλοις πραότεροι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.29}}; of other animals, **tame,** ἰχθύων μεγάλων καὶ πραέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 1.4.9, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 488b22; ζῷα.. πραέα πρὸς τοὺς ἀνθρώπους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 15.4: proverbial, πραΰτερος μολόχας 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 153; also, τόπος ἡμερώτερος καὶ πραότερος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.67.}}
**3** of actions, feelings, etc., **mild,** τιμωρίαι πραότεραι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 867b; πραότεραι ἡδοναί [prev. work] 815e; λόγοι, ἤθη, φύσις, [prev. work] 888a, 930a, “R.” 375c; ὄσσοισι πρηέσιν δέρκευ “BMus.Inscription” 921b8 (Branchidae, 1st-2nd c.AD)}}; τὰ πραέα **caresses,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.10}}; πραότερα πάσχειν {{Refs|Pl “Cri.” 49b.}}
**II** **making mild, taming,** φάρμακον πραῢ τείνων ἀμφὶ γένυι, of a bridle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.85; προκινεῖν αὐτὸν [τὸν ἵππον] ὡς πραοτάτοις σημείοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.3.}}
**III** _adverb_ **πράως**, **mildly, gently,** πράως πείθειν τινά, φέρειν τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 589c, “Cri.” 43b; πράως ἔχειν πρός τι [prev. author] “Ly.” 211e}}; πράως λέγειν τὸ πάθος to speak **lightly** of it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.14}}; πράως διακεῖσθαι, opposed to ὀργίζεσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.183; πράως, μὴ πικρῶς [prev. author] 18.265}}; in physical sense, **gently,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 134: _comparative_, πραότερον προδιδάσκειν, κολάζειν, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 489d, “Phd.” 94d}}; πραοτέρως (variant{-υτέρως}) ἔχειν τινί {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.6.4}}: _superlative_, φέρειν.. ὡς πραότωτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 387e}}: later form **πραέως** (πράως [Ἀττικοί], οὐ πραέως {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.), “UPZ” 144.6 (2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.36, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.13, uncertain in “Comica Adespota” 336.5 (compare πρατίας)}}, etc.: _Ionic dialect_ **πρηέως** conjecture in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 46 (πράως and πραέως codices)}}. (Cogn. with Sanskrit _prīṇā´ti_ 'love, give pleasure to', Gothic _frijon_ 'love', **frijonds** 'friend'.)

4241:
** πρέπ-ω**,
_imperfect_ ἔπρεπον, which were the tenses chiefly in use: _future_ πρέψω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 995 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 269c, 288c}}: _aorist_ ἔπρεψα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Chrm.” 158c:}} — properly of impressions on the senses,
**1** on the eye, **to be clearly seen, to be conspicuous** among a number, ὁ δ᾽ ἔπρεπε καὶ διὰ πάντων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.104; μετὰ δὲ π. ἀγρομένοισιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.172, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 92}}; **to be distinguished in** or **by** a thing, φάρεσιν μελαγχίμοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 12, compare “Th.” 124 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 512, 1050; π. παρηῒς φοινίοις ἀμυγμοῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 24 (Lyric poetry)}}; **shine forth, show itself,** πειρῶντι χρυσὸς ἐν βασάνῳ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.67; πανσέληνος ἐν σάκει π. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 390, compare “Pers.” 239 (troch.), “Ag.” 389 (Lyric poetry); πρέπουσά θ᾽ ὡς ἐν γραφαῖς [prev. work] 242 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐπί τοι πρέπει ὄμμασιν αἰδώς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 214; Ζεὺς πρέπων δι᾽ αἰθέρος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 216 (Lyric poetry)}}: sometimes with _participle_, **to be clearly seen** as doing or being, ὁ φρυκτὸς ἀγγέλλων πρέπει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 30}}; σπλάγχνα.. πρέπουσ᾽ ἔχοντες{{Refs|[prev. work] 1222, compare “Eu.” 995 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**2** on the ear, βοὰ π. the cry **sounds loud and clear,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.67, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 321.}}
**3** on the smell, **to be strong** or **rank,** ὅμοιος ἀτμὸς ὥσπερ ἐκ τάφου π. {{Refs|[prev. work] 1311.}}
**II** **to be conspicuously like, resemble,** π. τινὶ εἶδος **to be like** one in form, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.38; πρέποντα.. ταύρῳ δέμας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 301; εἴ τι σῇ δοκεῖ πρέπειν γυναικί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1121; πρέπεις.. θυγατέρων μορφὴν μιᾷ [prev. author] “Ba.” 917: with}}_infinitive_, τοῦδε γὰρ δράμημα φωτὸς Περσικὸν πρέπει μαθεῖν his running **is like** Persian to behold, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 247, compare “Supp.” 719}}; more frequently with ὡς or ὥστε, πρέπει ὡς τύραννος εἰσορᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 664; ὡς πένθιμος πρέπεις ὁρᾶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1056; πρέπει γ᾽ ὥστε θὴρ ἄγραυλος φόβῃ [prev. author] “Ba.” 1188 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** **to be conspicuously fitting, beseem,** with _dative_ _person_, θνατὰ θνατοῖσι πρέπει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).16; τοῖς ὀλβίοις γε καὶ τὸ νικᾶσθαι πρέπει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 941, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 158c, etc.; with Preps., ποῦ τάδ᾽ ἐν χρηστοῖς πρέπε; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 510; οἷα δὴ εἰς πλῆθος πρέπει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.24}}: with _participle_, ὅ τι γιγνόμενον ἂν πρέποι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 976c, compare “Plt.” 269c, 288c}}; πρέποι γὰρ ἂν (i.e. λεχθεῖσα) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 219c.}}
**III.2** frequently in _participle_, ὕμνοι πρέποντες γάμοις {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 460a, etc.}}; especially in _participle_ _neuter_, πρέπον τε εἶναι καὶ ἁρμόττειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 503e; ἤν τι ἄλλο π. δοκῇ εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.25; τὸ π. τῇ γραφῇ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.40.3: rarely with _genitive_, π. ἦν δαίμονος τοὐμοῦ τόδε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 534, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 14, Thom.Mag. p.306R.}}; τὸ π. that which is **seemly, propriety,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 294a; πρὸς τὸ μέτριον καὶ τὸ π. [prev. author] “Plt.” 284e, etc.: _plural_, πρέποντα πάσχειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.9; πρέποντα τῇ συγγενείᾳ ποιοῦντες 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.23.}}
**III.3** rarely with personal subject, πρέπων ἔφυς πρὸ τῶνδε φωνεῖν art **the fit person** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 9}}; Πομπήϊος.. πάνυ τοῖς ἔπεσι πρέπων **suiting** them, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 72, compare “Publ.” 17.}}
**III.4** mostly _impersonal_, πρέπει **it is fitting,** both of outward circumstances and moral fitness, with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.79, etc.; οὐ πρέπει νῷν.. δάσασθαι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.147; πρέπει ἐσλοῖσιν ὑμνεῖσθαι [prev. author] “fragment” 121, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 483 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 115, etc.: with}}_infinitive_ unexpressed, πρέπει γοῦν σοι [ἀποκρίνεσθαι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.37.}}
**III.4.b** with _accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, πρέπει τὸν Αἰνησιδάμου ἐγκωμίων τε μελέων λυρᾶν τε τυγχανέμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.46, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 203, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 728, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.86, etc.}}
**III.4.c** with _infinitive_ only, πρέπει γαρυέμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.82, compare “P.” 5.43, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 656, “Ag.” 636, etc.}}
**III.4.d** with _infinitive_ understood, an _accusative_ may be subject, ἀπήλλαξαν οὕτω ὡς κείνους ἔπρεπε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.68.α᾽,compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 195, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 312b; or object, τείσασθαι οὕτως, ὡς κείνους [τείσασθαι] πρέπει 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.139}}; so with _dative_ of indirect object, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.114.}}
**IV** _transitive_, **liken,** τινί τι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1328 (cited in 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.).}}

4242:
** πρεσβ-εία**, ἡ,
**age, seniority, right of the elder,** κατὰ πρεσβείαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 4 (_anapaest meter_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1259b12: hence}}
**2** **rank, dignity,** πρεσβείᾳ καὶ δυνάμει ὑπερέχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 509b.}}
**II** **embassy,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 570, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 422d, +others}}
**II.2** **body of ambassadors,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 647, “Eq.” 795, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.72, 4.118, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.1, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.23 (_plural_), etc.; καλέσαι ἐπὶ ξένια τὴν π. “IG” 12.19.14, +others; καλέσαι τὴν π. ἐπὶ δεῖπνον [prev. work]22.1.54.}}
**III** **intercession,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 33.}}

4243:
** πρεσβ-εύω**,
_perfect_ πεπρέσβευκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 610, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.310}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ἐπρεσβευσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.92, etc.:}} —_passive_, _perfect_ πεπρέσβευμαι (see. below):
**I** properly of age,
**I.1** _intransitive_, **to be the elder** or **eldest,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1422; οἱ ἀεὶ πρεσβεύοντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 951e}}: with _genitive_ _person_, **to be older than, be the eldest of** a number, τῶν προτέρων ἐπρέσβευε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.2; π. τῶν ἄλλων κατὰ τὴν ἡλικίαν 4th-3rd c.BC: Timaeus Historicus 114}}; π. ἀπ᾽ αὐτοῦ **to be** his **eldest** son, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.55}}; of wine, πολλαῖς π. ἐτέων.. ὥραις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 60.2; π. τοῖς χρόνοις τὰ ἡρωικά 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.19a: hence}}
**I.1.b** **take the first place, be best,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 720}}; οἷσι πρεσβεύει γένος, of the male sex, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 45.}}
**I.1.c** with _genitive_, **rank before, take precedence of** others, π. τῶν πολλῶν πόλεων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 752e}}: hence, **rule over,** Ολύμπου π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1389; Ἴναχε.. μέγα πρεσβεύων Ἄργους γύαις [prev. author] “fragment” 270 (_anapaest meter_)}}; also, **have at one's command** or **disposal,** with _genitive_, καὶ εἰ (καὶ σὺ Kaibel) τῶν λόγων αὐτοῦ πρεσβεύεις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.352d.}}
**I.2** _transitive_, **place as oldest** or **first, put first in rank,** πρῶτον.. πρεσβεύω θεῶν Γαῖαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 1}}: hence, **pay special honour** or **worship to,** πρῶτον τόνδε πρεσβεύσω τάφον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ch.” 488, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1065, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 186b; joined with τιμῶ [prev. author] “Cri.” 46c; τὰ δίκαια πρὸ παντὸς ἰδίου συμφέροντος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 3, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 6.30.3}}:—_passive_, **hold the first place,** Παλλὰς.. ἐν λόγοις π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 21}}; ὁ δ᾽ ὕστατός γε τοῦ χρόνου π. **is first** in point of time, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1300: with _genitive_, κακῶν πρεσβεύεται τὸ Αήμνιον}} **is most notable** of mischiefs, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 631 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὸ πρεσβύτερον τοῦ νεωτέρου ἐστὶ πρεσβευόμενον is **more honoured than..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 879b.}}
**I.2.b** later, **cultivate** arts, etc., διαλεκτικόν, τὸ.. τοὺς λόγους πρεσβεῦον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.18; π. παρὰ Ῥοδίοις ἃ μήπω ἐγίγνωσκον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1}} **Praef.;** πόλις ὄρχησιν μάλιστα πρεσβεύουσα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 76.}}
**II** **to be an ambassador** or **serve as one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.135.5; ἀπὸ Κορίνθου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.93; εἰς Θετταλίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.41}}; παρά or πρός τινα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 158a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.3}}; τινος **for** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 479: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 610, etc.: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἃς ἐπρέσβευσεν [εἰς Θήβας] πρεσβείας 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.16.}}
**II.b** at Rome, **act as legatus,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 4.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ objecti, π. εἰρήνην **negotiate** peace, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.177, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.134, etc.; π. ὑπὲρ τουτωνὶ τὰ βέλτιστα [prev. work]189; π. πολλὰ καὶ δεινά [prev. passage]:}} — _passive_, τὰ αὑτῷ πεπρεσβευμένα his **negotiations,** {{Refs|[prev. work]20; πολλὰ καὶ δεινὰ πεπρεσβεῦσθαι [prev. work]240.}}
**II.3** _middle_, **send ambassadors,** ἐς χωρία, ἐς τὴν Θουρίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.7, 6.104; πρεσβεύεσθαι παρά τινας [prev. author] 4.41, etc.; πρός τινας [prev. author] 1.126; ἐς Αακεδαίμονα περὶ καθόδου [prev. author] 3.85.}}
**II.3.b** **go as ambassador,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.39.}}
**III** with _accusative_ _of things_, **represent, urge, maintain,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 23}}; [δόγματα] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.753; τὴν ὄψιν κατὰ εἰσδοχὴν π. 6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 5.6: with}}_infinitive_, κατὰ ἐκπομπὴν εἶναι τὴν ὄψιν{{Refs|[prev. work] 10.}}

