4248:
** πρᾱν-ής**, ές,
_Ionic dialect_ **πρηνής** (also in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 350a11, “Spir.” 484b29, “fragment” 106, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.3.1, 19.8.2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.680a, “Tim.” 11, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 2.2, 7.22, “PMag.Par.” 1.194, etc.)}}, _genitive_ έος, _Attic dialect_ _contraction_ οῦς:—of posture, **with the face downwards, lying on the front, falling forwards,** opposed to ὕπτιος, πρηνεῖς τε καὶ ὕπτιοι ἔκπεσον ἵππων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.179; ἐκ δίφροιο.. ἐξεκυλίσθη πρηνὴς ἐν κονίῃσιν ἐπὶ στόμα 6.43, compare 2.418, 4.544, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 365; πρηνὴς ἐπὶ γαίῃ κεῖτο ταθείς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.118; mostly with Verbs of falling, πρηνὴς κάππεσε, ἤριπε, ἐλιάσθη, 16.413, 5.58, 15.543}}; πρηνέα.. τανύσσας [Ἕκτορα{{Refs|] 23.25; κατὰ πρηνὲς βαλέειν Πριάμοιο μέλαθρον}}**headlong down,** {{NT|2.414; π. γενόμενος NT.Act.1.18}} (perhaps ={πρησθείς} **becoming distended**); ἐπὶ τὸ πρηνὲς ῥέπειν incline towards **pronation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 1; ἐς τὸ π. [prev. author] “Mochl.” 8}}; of the arm and hand, **with the palm downwards,** variant in {{Refs|“Fract.” 2}}; opposed to ὕπτιος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Spir.” [same place], 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 11}}; of ἀστράγαλοι, ὀρθοὶ πίπτοντες ἢ πρηνεῖς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 2.680a, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.204}}; of seeds, **hollow side downwards,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.6.1}}; of a ship, **bottom upwards,** implied in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tim.” [prev. cited]}}
**II** of parts of animals or man, that part which is uppermost and visible when the animal or man is in the πρανής position (the normal one for a quadruped), the **back** part, τὰ τετράποδα.. ἐν τοῖς ὑπτίοις οὐκ ἔ χει τὰς τρίχας, ἀλλ᾽ ἐν τοῖς πρανέσι μᾶλλον· οἱ δ᾽ ἄνθρωποι τοὐναντίον ἐν τοῖς ὑπτίοις μᾶλλον ἢ ἐν τοῖς πρανέσιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 658a17, compare “HA” 498b20, 519a21, 540a2, “GA” 717b30.}}
**II.2** of leaves and of the hand, the **back** or **'wrong'** side, τὰς ἶνας καὶ τὰς φλέβας ἐν τοῖς π. ἔχουσιν ὥσπερ ἡ χείρ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.10.2}} (misunderstood as the opposite by {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 16.88), compare 3.14.2.}}
**III** of the sides of hills, πρὸς ἄναντες καὶ κατὰ πρανοῦς καὶ πλάγια ἐλαύνειν **down hill,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 3.7, compare “An.” 1.5.8, 4.8.28, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 18; κατὰ τὰ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 8.6}}; τὸ π., opposed to τὸ ὄρθιον, {{Refs|[prev. passage],compare “Cyr.” 2.2.24.}}
**III.2** **convex,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 350a11.}}

4249:
** πρίζω**,
={πρίω}, **saw,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 124b (assuming variant), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.76, 1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 47.14.3}}: _imperfect_ ἔπριζον {{LXX|LXX.Amos.1.3.}}
**II** **file,** ῥίνῃ πρίζειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.848.}}

4250:
** πρίν**,
_adverb_ and _conjunction_, **before, until.** [πρῐν 19 {{Refs 8th c.BC+|times in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.344, +others}}; πρῑν in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.81, 13.172, +others}}; once written **πρείν**, {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 7.40}}, but **πρίν** {{Refs|“IG” 12.60.11, 94.9, 114.46, etc.}}; Trag. and Comedy texts always πρῐν (πρίν γ᾽ must be read in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 176).]}}
**A** _adverb_ of Time, **before,** either in the sense of **sooner** or in that of **formerly, erst** (implying duration up to a certain time):
**I** of future time, with _future_ Indic., πρίν μιν καὶ γῆρας ἔπεισιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.29, compare 18.283, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.198, etc.}}: with Subj. = _future_, πρὶν καὶ κακὸν ἄλλο πάθῃσθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.551}}: with Opt. and κεν, πρίν κεν ἀνιηθεὶς σὴν πατρίδα γαῖαν ἵκοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.117, compare 11.330, 14.155, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1076, 1112: with Opt., 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.800: with Imper., 9.250: with Inf. (expressing a wish), 2.413, (expressing an oath) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.254.}}
**II** of past time,
**II.1** **formerly, once,** πρὶν μέν μοι ὑπέσχετο.., νῦν δὲ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.112, variant in 9.19, compare 23.827; πρὶν μὲν πόσιν ἐσθλὸν ἀπώλεσα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.724, compare 3.408.}}
**II.2** **formerly** (up to a certain point), **before,** in this sense frequently with _Article_, τὸ πρίν γ᾽ ἐκέκαστο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.54; τὸ πρίν γε.., νῦν δὲ.. 13.105; νῦν δὲ.. τὸ πρίν γε 16.208, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 443, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.129: without _Article_, τὰς ἐπιστήμας ἅς ποτε καὶ π. εἴχομεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75e: with ellipsis of}}_participle_ γενόμενος, τὰ π. πελώρια (i.e. γενόμενα) the giants **of old,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 151 (Lyric poetry)}}; τοῦ π. Αἰγέως Aegeus **gone before,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 69; ἐν τῷ π. χρόνῳ [prev. author] “Ph.” 1224; ἐν τοῖς π. λόγοις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.62}}: with _participle_ expressed, τὸ π. γενόμενον τέρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.37; τοὺς πρὶν φυλαττομένους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 547c, etc.}}
**II.3** **hitherto,** π. μέν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.114}}; **until that time,** and so **meanwhile,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 15.13.}}
**II.4** sometimes followed by _genitive_, π. ὥρας {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.43; π. ἀνηκέστου πάθους 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.6.1; π. γενέσεως 2nd c.AD(?): Theodotion LXX “Su.” 42; π. τῆς συνόδου 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.371; π. φάους 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 3.18.6}}; π. τοῦ βλέψαι, π. τοῦ ἀποθανεῖν, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 7.162, variant in LXX.Tob.14.15}}; also πρὶν οὗ with _infinitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 953.16 (Calymna, 2nd c.BC)}}; with _indicative_, Test. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 46.21.}}
**B** _conjunction_ **before, ere**: frequently following an antecedent clause with adverbial π. (chiefly in _Epic dialect_), or its equivalents πρότερον, πρόσθεν, πάρος (_poetry_), especially with _negative_, οὐδὲ π..., π... {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.98, 7.481, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.475; μὴ π..., π... 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.355, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 605; π..., π... 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.348, 8.453, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.586; οὐ πρότερον.., π... 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 620, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.17, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.61; μὴ πρότερον.. π... 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 199 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 62c, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.10; πρότερον.., π... 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.9; οὐ πρόσθεν.., π... 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.23; μὴ πρόσθεν.., π... [prev. author] “An.” 1.1.10; πρόσθε., π. τυχεῖν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.92; οὐ πάρος.., π... 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.128, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.219}}; preceded by φθάνω,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|16.322, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.29, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.79, 104, 6.97, 8.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.21, “Cyr.” 2.4.25; sometimes followed by ἤ, οὐ.. πρίν γ᾽ ἀποπαύσεσθαι πρίν γ᾽ ἢ ἕτερόν γε πεσόντα αἵματος ἆσαι Ἄρηα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.288, compare 22.266, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.136, 165, +others}}; uncertain and perhaps always corrupt in _Attic dialect_ and {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.61, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.19 (variant), 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 128, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.132 (variant), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.23, “An.” 4.5.1}}, but frequently in later Greek, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.29.26, etc.}}
**B.I** with _infinitive_, the prevailing _construction_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, after positive and negative clauses alike: in _Attic dialect_ mostly after positive clauses, and always used with them when the action does not or is not to take place: the tense is}}
**B.I** regularly _aorist_,
**B.I.a** after a positive clause, ναῖε δὲ Πήδαιον, πρὶν ἐλθεῖν υἷας Ἀχαιῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.172, compare 8.453, 16.322, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.210; Ζεὺς ὀλέσειε βίην, πρὶν ἥβης μέτρον ἱκέσθαι 4.668, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.465, 24.245, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.92.3.9, “N.” 8.19, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.119, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 712, “Ag.” 1539 (_anapaest meter_). 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 120 (Lyric poetry), “Tr.” 396, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 281, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 258, +others, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.67, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.125, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 350b, +others}}; after _negative_ questions which expect a positive answer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1069, “Ion” 524, “Rh.” 684, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 481, etc.}}
**B.I.b** after a _negative_ clause, οὐδ᾽ ὅ γε πρὶν Δαναοῖσιν ἀεικέα λοιγὸν ἀπώσει, πρίν γ᾽ ἀπὸ πατρὶ φίλῳ δόμεναι ἑλικώπιδα κούρην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.98, compare 19.423, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.128, 4.747: after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus a negative antecedent is commonly followed by πρίν with finite Verb (see. below 11)}}; but Inf. is found where π. precedes, π. ἰδεῖν δ᾽, οὐδεὶς μάντις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1419 (_anapaest meter_); π. μὲν γὰρ κριθῆναι, οὐ ῥᾴδιον ἦν εἰδέναι τὰς αἰτίας 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.8; π. νικῆσαι.., οὐκ ἦν.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.28; π. δὲ ταῦτα πρᾶξαι, μὴ σκοπεῖτε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.12, compare 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 135; also, οὔτε.. π. ἱδρῶσαι δεῖπνον ᾑρεῖτο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.38}}; also after Verbs of fearing (the positive being the thing dreaded), ὅταν.. δεδίωσι μὴ πρότερόν τι μάθῃς, π. τέλος ἐπιθεῖναι τοῖς πραττομένοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.70, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 453.6, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 632}}; in unfulfilled conditions and wishes, οὔθ᾽ ὁ Πλούτωνος κύων οὔθ᾽ οὑπὶ κώπῃ ψυχοπομπὸς ἂν Χάρων ἔσχον π. εἰς φῶς σὸν καταστῆσαι βίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 362, compare “Rh.” 61}}; otherwise not common, ὤφθην οὐδεπώποτε π. ταύτην τὴν συμφορὰν γενέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.55; οὐδὲ παύσεται χόλου.., π. κατασκῆψαί τινα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 94, compare “HF” 605; καί μοι μὴ θορυβήσῃ μηδεὶς π. ἀκοῦσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.15, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 4.24: after _negative_ _optative_ with ἄν, οὕτω γένοιτ᾽ ἂν οὐδ᾽ ἂν ἔκβασις στρατοῦ καλή, π. ὅρμῳ ναῦν θρασυνθῆναι a. “Supp.” 772, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 769e: after a past tense (in indirect speech), ὤμοσαν μὴ π. ἐς Φώκαιαν ἥξειν, π. ἢ τὸν μύδρον τοῦτον ἀναφανῆναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.165, compare 4.9, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.50, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61a.}}
**B.I.2** also _present_, to convey a special sense of continuance, effort, or the like, 'before undertaking to', 'before proceeding to', π. ἐξοπλίζειν Ἄρη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 702 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ag.” 1067; π. νυν τὰ πλείον᾽ ἱστορεῖν.., ἔξελθε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 36, compare “El.” 20; π. κλαίειν τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 577, compare “Or.” 1095; π. λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 380, compare “Ach.” 383, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.1, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 666a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.25, “Mem.” 1.2.40, etc.}}
**B.I.3** also _perfect_, after a _future_, π. τόδ᾽ ἐξηντληκέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 79}}; after _present_ or _imperfect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 1069, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.25; π..., τί μέλλετ᾽..; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1145; π. καὶ τεθύσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1034, compare “V.” 1156, “Pax” 375, “Lys.” 322 (Lyric poetry), “Ra.” 1185, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.21, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 164c, “Prt.” 320a, etc.}}; with ἥκειν in _perfect_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.116; οὐ βουλόμενος διαγωνίσασθαι π. οἱ τοὺς βοηθοὺς ἥκειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.10.}}
**B.II** with a finite Verb:
**B.II.1** with Ind., chiefly _aorist_: not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (first in “hymnus ad Apollinem” 357), who uses Ind. only with πρίν γ᾽ ὅτε, πρίν γ᾽ ὅτε δή}} after both positive and _negative_ clauses, ἠλώμην.., πρίν γ᾽ ὅτε.. ἤγαγες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.322; πρίν γ᾽ ὅτε δή με.. κάλεσσεν 23.43, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.437; οὐδέ κεν ἡμέας ἄλλο διέκρινεν.., πρίν γ᾽ ὅτε δὴ θανάτοιο.. νέφος ἀμφεκάλυψεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.180: rarely with}}_imperfect_, οὐδ᾽ ὧς τοῦ θυμὸν.. ἔπειθον, πρίν γ᾽ ὅτε δὴ θάλαμος πύκ᾽ ἐβάλλετο (**began to be hit**) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.588: frequently after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, with}}_aorist_,
**B.II.1.a** after _negative_ clauses: of a fact in the past, οὐκ ἦν ἀλέξημ᾽ οὐδὲν.., πρίν γ᾽ ἐγώ σφισιν ἔδειξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 481; οὐ πρότερον ἀπανέστη.. Μαρδόνιος, πρὶν ἤ σφεας ὑποχειρίους ἐποιήσατο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.45}}; ἀλλ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ὣς.. ἠξίωσαν νεώτερόν τι ποιεῖν ἐς αὐτο᾽ν.., πρίν γε δὴ αὐτοῖς.. μηνυτὴς γίγνεται (historical _present_ = _aorist_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.132, compare 3.101, 5.61, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.79, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 700, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.23, 4.5.13 (historical _present_), “HG” 5.4.58, etc.; once in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, “Phdr.” 266a}}; as part of an unfulfilled condition, οὐκ ἂν κατέσχε δῆμον οὐδ᾽ ἐπαύσατο π. ἀνταράξας πῖαρ ἐξεῖλεν γάλα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 12.5; οὐκ ἂν ἐσκεψάμεθα πρότερον.., πρὶν ἐζητήσαμεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 86d, compare “Tht.” 165e; χρῆν τοίνυν Αεπτίνην μὴ πρότερον τιθέναι τὸν ἑαυτοῦ νόμον, πρὶν τοῦτον ἔλυσε γραψάμενος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.96}}; after verbs implying a _negative_, ἀμφιγνοεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.33, θαυμάζειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.51, λανθάνειν [prev. author] 3.29}}; also with _imperfect_, οὔπω ᾔδει.. π. ἐν τῷ κακῷ ἦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.19, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.17, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.61.}}
**B.II.1.b** after positive clauses (both combined, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 481, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.118)}}, with the sense **until,** ἠγόμην δ᾽ ἀνὴρ ἀστῶν μέγιστος.., πρίν μοι τύχη τοιάδ᾽ ἐπέστη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 776}}; σπουδαὶ δὲ λόγων ἦσαν ἴσαι πως, πρὶν.. πείθει (historical _present_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 131 (_anapaest meter_); πρίν γ᾽ ὁρᾷ [prev. author] “Med.” 1173; frequently followed by δή, π. δή τις ἐφθέγξατο [prev. author] “Andr.” 1147}}; τὰ περὶ τοὺς ἀγῶνας κατελύθη (_negative_ idea) ὑπὸ ξυμφορῶν, πρὶν δὴ οἱ Ἀθηναῖοι τότε τὸν ἀγῶνα ἐποίησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.104, compare 7.39 (historical _present_), 71.}}
**B.II.2** with Subj. only after _negatives_ or equivalent of _negative_, ={ἕως} or ἢν μή (in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.173 ἢν μή and πρὶν ἄν are used almost as synonyms)}}; οὐ καταδυσόμεθ᾽, ἀχνύμενοί περ.., πρὶν μόρσιμον ἦμαρ ἐπέλθῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.175; μή πω καταδύσεο.., πρίν γ᾽ ἐμὲ.. ἴδηαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.135, compare 190, 24.781; ἀλλ᾽ ὄμοσον μὴ μητρὶ φίλῃ τάδε μυθήσασθαι, πρίν γ᾽ ὅτ᾽ ἂν ἑνδεκάτη τε δυωδεκάτη τε γένηται 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.374, compare 4.477: in Prose usually πρὶν ἄν (πρίν κα “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.161 (from Cyrene)), rarely π. alone, as also πρὶν ἤ:}}
**B.II.2.a** generally with _aorist_, to express an action preceding the action of the _antecedent_ clause, the Verb in which is _future_ (or some equivalent of the _future_) or _imperative_, οὐ γαμέεται παρθένος οὐδεμία, πρὶν ἂν τῶν πολεμίων ἄνδρα ἀποκτείνῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.117, compare 1.82 (variant), 3.109 (variant)}}; νῦν δ᾽ οὐδέν ἐστι τέρμα μοι προκείμενον μόχθων (the sense here is _future_), πρὶν ἂν Ζεὺς ἐκπέσῃ τυραννίδος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 756, compare 166 (Lyric poetry), 177 (_anapaest meter_)}}; οὐ γάρ ποτ᾽ ἔξει τῆσδε τῆς χώρας, πρὶν ἂν.. στήσῃς ἄγων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 909, compare 48, 1041, “OT” 1529, etc.; οὐκ ἂν ἐκμάθοις.., πρὶν ἂν θάνῃ τις [prev. author] “Tr.” 2; οὐκ ἄπειμι πρὸς δόμους πάλιν, πρὶν ἄν σε.. ἔξω βάλω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 276, compare 680, Alcaeus1145, “IA” 324, “IT” 19, 1302; μὴ προκαταγίγνωσκε.., π. ἄν γ᾽ ἀκούσῃς ἀμφοτέρων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 920, compare “Ach.” 176, 230, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 6.13, “Cyr.” 1.2.8, “An.” 1. 1.10, 5.7.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228c, “La.” 187e (ἂν added in later codices), etc.}}; μηδέν᾽ ὀλβίζειν π. ἀ᾽ν τέρμα τοῦ βίου περάσῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1529 (troch.); οὐχὶ μὴ παύσησθε, π. ἄν.. ὑμᾶς τις ἐκτραχηλίσῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 704: π. without ἄν, μὴ στέναζε, π. μάθῃς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 917, compare “Ant.” 619 (Lyric poetry), “Aj.” 742, 965, “Tr.” 608, 946; οὐκ ἔστιν ὅστις αὐτὸν ἐξαιρήσεται.., π. γυναῖκ᾽ ἐμοὶ μεθῇ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 849, compare “Or.” 1218, 1357 (Lyric poetry); π. χαρίσωνται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 629 (assuming variant); οὐ γὰρ δή σφεας ἀπίει τῆς ἀποικίης, πρὶν δὴ ἀπίκωνται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.157; π. διαγνῶσι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.29; π... βεβαιωσώμεθα [prev. work] 10 (uncertain reading)}}; πρὶν ἀνάγκην τινὰ θεὸς ἐπιπέμψῃ [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 62c codices; π. ἐξετάσωσιν 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 4: πρὶν ἤ (never with ἄν), π. ἢ ἀνορθώσωσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.19, compare 136, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 57b, etc.: with _negative_ implied, ὁ δὲ ἀδικέει ἀναπειθόμενος π. ἢ ἀτρεκέως ἐκμάθῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10. ; αἰσχρὸν ἡγοῦμαι πρότερον παύσασθαι, π. ἂν.. ψηφίσησθε 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.4; ὅστις οὖν οἴεται τοὺς ἄλλους πράξειν τι.., π. ἂν.. διαλλάξῃ, λίαν ἁπλῶς ἔχει 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.16 (where ὅστις οὖν οἴεται ={οὐ δεῖ οὔεσθαι}, as is shown by ἀλλὰ δεῖ}}] in the next sentence,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.24).}}
**B.II.2.b** less frequently (never in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus) with}}_present_ _subjunctive_: μήπω π. ἂν τῶν ἡμετέρων ἀΐῃς (the Verb has no _aorist_) μύθων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1409 (_anapaest meter_); ὁ νομοθέτης τὰ διδασκαλεῖα ἀνοίγειν ἀπαγορεύει μὴ πρότερον π. ἂν ὁ ἥλιος ἀνίσχῃ 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.10, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 271c.}}
**B.II.3** πρίν with Opt.:
**B.II.3.a** representing _subjunctive_ after historical tenses, οὐκ ἔθελεν φεύγειν π. πειρήσαιτ᾽ Ἀχιλῆος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.580}}; πρίν γ᾽ ὅτε, as with _subjunctive_,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|9.488; ἔδοξέ μοι μὴ σῖγα π. φράσαιμί σοι τὸν πλοῦν ποεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 551, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.14, “HG” 6.5.19 (compare 2.4.18), “An.” 1.2.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36c, etc.}}
**B.II.3.b** by assimilation, ὄλοιο μήπω π. μάθοιμι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 961, compare “Tr.” 657 (Lyric poetry)}}; οὐδὲ γὰρ εἰδείης (potential _optative_).. π. πειρηθείης {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 126; after _optative_ with ἄν, οὐκ ἂν πρότερον ὁρμήσειε π. βεβαιώσαιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 799d, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 505 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.II.4** π. ἄν with _optative_ is doubtful, and (if not corrupt) due to the change required by _indirect speech_, ἀπαγορευόντων τῶν φίλων τῶν ἐμῶν μὴ ἀποκτείνειν τὸν ἄνδρα, πρὶν ἄν ἐγὼ ἔλθοιμι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.34 (assuming variant), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.48, 2.4.18.}}
**B.II.5** without a Verb, πρὶν ὥρη (i.e. γένηται) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.394.}}

4253:
** πρό**,
**before, forth**:
**A** PREP. WITH GENIT.:
**A.I** of Place, **before, in front of,** ἠγερέθοντο π. ἄστεος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.468, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.351, etc.; π. πτόλιος δεδαϊγμένον 19.292; κείνους κιχησόμεθα π. πυλάων 10.126, compare 6.80, etc.; φύλοπις αἰνὴ ἕστηκε π. νεῶν 18.172; πυρὰ φαίνετο Ἰλιόθι π. 8.561, compare 10.12, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.581, etc.; κλαγγὴ γεράνων πέλει οὐρανόθι π. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.3; π. τειχέων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.56}}; ἔμπροσθε π. (variant) τῆς ἀκροπόλιος, ὄπισθε δὲ τῶν πυλέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.53, compare 9.52}}; π. δόμων, π. δωμάτων, **in front of,** i.e. **outside** the house, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.18, 5.96, etc.; π. θυρῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 109 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}}; τὴν π. τοῦ Ἡραίου νῆσον **before** or **off** the Heraeum, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.75, compare 7.22; π. ποδός, see at {πούς} 1.4a}}]; π. χειρῶν **at** hand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1279, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 274, uncertain in “Tr.” 1207; π. τῶν ὀφθαλμῶν προφαίνεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.148.}}
**A.I.2** with Vbs. of motion, π. δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ αὐτῶν κύνες ἤϊσαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.435, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.115; π. Ἀχαιῶν ἄγγελος ᾔει 10.286, compare 13.693; π. ἕθεν κλονέοντα φάλαγγας 5.96.}}
**A.I.3** **before, in front of,** for the purpose of shielding or guarding, π. Τρώων ἑσταότ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.215}}: hence, **in defence of,** μάχεσθαι.. π. τε παίδων καὶ π. γυναικῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|8.57, compare 4.156, 373, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.74, etc.}}; ὀλέσθαι π. πόληος, Latin _pro patria mori,_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.110; π. τῆς Σπάρτης ἀποθνῄσκειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.134, compare 172, 9.72, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 18, 645, etc.; π. τοῦ θανόντος.. ἔθεσθ᾽ ἐπιστροφήν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 134; διακινδυνεῦσαι π. βασιλέως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.4; βουλεύεσθαι, πράττειν π. τινός, [prev. work] 1.6.42, 4.5.44, compare “Mem.” 2.4.7}}; π. τοξευμάτων **as a defence against** arrows, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 7.8.18}}: hence also, **for, on behalf of, instead of,** ἀγρυπνῆσαι π. τινῶν{{Refs|[prev. work] 7.6.36, compare “Leg.Gort.” 1.43}}; of an advocate, π. τῶνδε φωνεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 10, compare “OC” 811}}; ὄτι δέ κ᾽ αὐτὸς π. Ειαυτοῦ [ἀμάρτῃ] whatever offence he commits **of** his own **volition,** Kohler-Ziebarth **Stadtrecht von Gortyn** {{Refs|p.34.}}
**A.I.4** π. ὁδοῦ ἐγένοντο **further on** the road, i.e. **forwards, onward,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.382, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 3.16, 7.29 (see. φροῦδος)}}: also to denote **distance,** π. πολλοῦ τῆς πόλεως {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.35; π. τριάκοντα σταδίων}}**at a distance of** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|30 stades, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.6.24.}}
**A.I.5** π. ἠοῦς, π. ἑσπέρης τοῦ βωμοῦ, east**wards,** west**wards** of.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 7.235.45 (Orop., 4th c.BC).}}
**A.II** of Time, **before,** π. γάμοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.524; ἠῶθι π. 5.469}}; π. ὃ τοῦ ἐνόησεν one **before** the other, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.224}}; more frequently in later writers, π. τῶν Τρωικῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3, compare 1.1; π. τοῦ θανεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 883; π. τοῦ θανάτου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 57a; π. τοῦ λοιμοῦ [prev. author] “Smp.” 201d; π. δείπνου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.39; π. ἡμέρας [prev. work] 4.5.14}}; π. τοῦ χρῆσθαι **before** one uses it, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 2.6.6}}; π. μοίρας τῆς ἐμῆς **before** my doom, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1266; π. τῆς εἱμαρμένης 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.21; π. τοῦ καθήκοντος χρόνου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.126}}, compare 124; π. πολλοῦ long **before,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.130, etc.}}; π. μικροῦ, π. ὀλίγου, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 73, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.116; ὀλίγον π. τούτων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8; τὸ π. τοῦ (variant{τούτου}) [prev. work] 15}}]; π. τοῦ (sometimes written προτοῦ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1204, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.122, 5.83, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 418, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 173a; ὁ π. τοῦ χρόνος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 462, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.58, etc.}}; π. τοῦ ἤ, ={πρὶν ἤ}, {{Refs|“IG” 7.2225.22 (from Thisbe)}}; οἱ π. ἡμῶν γενόμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 13.19; οἱ π. ἐμοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.97.}}
**A.II.2** in later writers frequently with Numerals, π. τριάκοντα ἡμερῶν thirty days **before,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 5.52; π. μιᾶς ἡμέρας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 63; π. ἐνιαυτοῦ [prev. author] 2.147e; π. δυεῖν ἡμερῶν ἢ ἐτελεύτα [prev. author] “Sull.” 37: frequently with double _genitive_, π. δύο ἐτῶν τοῦ σεισμοῦ, π. δύο ὡρῶν τῆς ἐπιβολῆς, LXX.Amos.1.1, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.64}}; π. ἓξ ἡμερῶν τοῦ πάσχα, π. μιᾶς ἡμέρας τῶν γενεθλίων, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.John.12.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.717d; π. πολλοῦ τῆς ἑορτῆς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sat.” 14.}}
**A.II.2.b** in rendering Roman dates, τῇ π. μιᾶς Νωνῶν Ὀκτωβρίων, = **pridie Non. Oct.,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.203a, etc.}}
**A.III** in other relations:
**A.III.1** of Preference, **before, rather than,** κέρδος αἰνῆσαι π. δίκας to praise sleight **before** right, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.140, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361e}}; πᾶν δὴ βουλόμενοι σφίσι εἶναι π. τῆς παρεούσης λύπης anything **before, rather than,** their actual trouble, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.152}} (so, **in order to avoid,** π. τοῦ δεινοτάτου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.19); πᾶν π. τοῦ δουλεῦσαι ἐπεξελθεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.100, compare 4.59}}; ἑλέσθαι, αἱρεῖσθαι, or κρῖναί τι π. τινός to choose one **before** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.36, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 366b, “Phlb.” 57e}}; π. πολλοῦ ποιήσασθαι to esteem **above** much, i.e. very highly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.138; π. πολλῶν χρημάτων τιμήσασθαί τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.33, compare 6.10}}; π. ἄλλων **more than** others, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 249e (variant), compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1002; δυσδαίμων.. π. πασᾶν γυναικῶν [prev. work] 927 (codices, Lyric poetry)}}; π. πάντων θεῶν τῇ Ἑστίᾳ πρώτῃ προθύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 401d}}: after a _comparative_ it is redundant, ἡ τυραννὶς π. ἐλευθερίης ἀσπαστότερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.62, compare 6.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28d, “Cri.” 54b, “Phd.” 99a}}; for ἤ after ἄλλος, οὐδεὶς ἄλλος π. σεῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.85, compare 7.3.}}
**A.III.2** of Cause or Motive, **for, from,** π. φόβοιο **for** fear, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.667}}; ἀθλεύων π. ἄνακτος toiling **before the face of,** i.e. **in** his **service,** {{Refs|24.734}}; π. τῶνδε there **fore,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 495 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B** POSITION: words may be put between π. and its case, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.115}}; but it does not follow its case, except after _Epic dialect_ forms in -θι, Ἰλιόθι πρό, οὐρανόθι πρό, ἠῶθι πρό (see. above).
**C** πρό, _absolutely_ as _adverb_.:
**C.I** of Place, **before,** opposed to ἐπί (**after**), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.799, 800}}; **before, in front,** {{Refs|15.360}}; **forth, forward,** ἐκ δ᾽ ἄγαγε π. φόωσδε {{Refs|19.118}}; χωρεῖν π. δόμων to come **forth** from, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 960 (Lyric poetry); ἄγειν τινὰ π. δόμων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 59 (_anapaest meter_)}}; γῆν π. γῆς ἐλαύνομαι I am driven **on** from one land to another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 682; διώκειν γῆν π. γῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 235.}}
**C.II** of Time, **before,** πρό οἱ εἴπομεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.37}}; **earlier,** τά τ᾽ ἐσσόμενα π. τ᾽ ἐόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 32, 38.}}
**C.III** when joined with other Preps., ἀποπρό, διαπρό, ἐπιπρό, περιπρό, προπρό, it strengthens the first _preposition_, or adds to it the notion of **forward, forth.**
**D** πρό IN COMPOSITION (joined with other words),
**D.I** with _Substantives_, to denote
**D.I.1** **position before** or **in front,** πρόδομος, προάστιον, πρόθυρον, προπύλαια, etc.
**D.I.2** **priority of rank,** πρόεδρος, προεδρία, etc.: also **priority of order,** προάγων, πρόλογος, προοίμιον, προπάτωρ, etc.
**D.I.3** standing **in** another's **place,** πρόμαντις, πρόξενος.
**D.II** with _adjectives_, to denote
**D.II.1** **proximity,** πρόχειρο; and **readiness,** πρόθυμος, πρόφρων.
**D.II.2** **away** (compare 111.3 below), προθέλυμνος, πρόρριζος.
**D.II.3** **prematureness,** πρόμοιρος, πρόωρος.
**D.II.4** **intensity,** πρόπας, πρόπαρ, προπάροιθ; so also πρόκακος, πρόπαλαι.
**D.III** with Verbs,
**D.III.1** of Place, **before, forwards,** προβαίνω, προβάλλω, προτίθημι, etc.: also, **before, in defence,** προκινδυνεύω, προμάχομαι, etc.
**D.III.2** **forth,** προέλκω, προφέρω.
**D.III.2.b** **publicly,** προγράφω, προειπεῖν, πρόκειμαι.
**D.III.3** **away,** προδίδωμι, προϊάλλω, προϊάπτω, προΐημι, προλείπω, προρέω, προτέμνω, προτρέπομαι, προφεύγω, προχέω.
**D.III.4** **in preference,** προαιροῦμαι, προτιμάω, etc.
**D.III.5** **before, beforehand,** προαισθάνομαι, προγίγνομαι, προκαταλαμβάνω, etc.; of **fore**sight, προνοέω, προοράω.
**E** Etymology: cf. Latin προ?~X-, Slavonic _pro-,_ Sanskrit _pra-,_ etc., in compounds.

4254:
** προάγω** [ᾰ],
_future_ -άξω: _perfect_ _active_ προῆχα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.18, 25.8, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.11.10:}} —_middle_, see below: _perfect_ _passive_ in _middle_ sense, see below {{Refs|1.7}}:—**lead forward** or **onward,** μιν ἐς τὰ οἰκία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.148, etc.}}; **escort on their way,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.132; τοὺς πεζοὺς οὐ πολλὴν ὁδόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.23}}:—_passive_, **to be led on,** προαγομένης τῆς πόλεως ἐπὶ συμφοράς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.9.}}
**2** **carry on,** αἱμασιάν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 55.27}}; **produce,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.7.6:}} —_passive_, [τάξις] εἰς ὀξὺ προηγμένη **brought** to a point, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 16.8.}}
**2.b** **bring on** in age, etc., προῆγεν αὐτὸν ὁ χρόνος εἰς ὥραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.4:}} —_passive_, ἐπὶ πλείω προῆκται τῆς κατ᾽ ἰητρικὴν ἐπιμελείας **belong to** more **advanced** medical study, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἰητροῦ” 13.}}
**2.c** **increase, raise** a dose, ἐπὶ ἓξ κοτύλας [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “fragment” 68 (variant{προσ-}).}}]
**3** **bring forward,** νεκρόν εἰς τὸ φανερόν, τι εἰς τὸ πρόσθεν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 960a, “Plt.” 262c; τὴν φύσιν εἰς φῶς πᾶσιν [prev. author] “Ep.” 341d; βουλὴν ἀπόρρητον εἰς φῶς ἡλίου 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.552d}}; οἱ προαγαγόντες εἰς φῶς, ={οἱ γονεῖς}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.8, compare 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 7.23}}; **call up** an apparition, Thessal. in {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(3).137.}}
**3.b** **bring before** a tribunal, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 826 G 22 (2nd c.BC, _passive_)}}; π. δάνειον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1562.14 (3rd c.AD).}}
**4** **lead on, induce, persuade,** δόλῳτινὰς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.90; ὡς ἡχρεία προάγει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.59: with}}_infinitive_ added, κινδυνεύειν τινὰ π.{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. work] 45; ἐγὼ προήγαγον ὑμᾶς ἄξια τῶν προγόνων φρονεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.206: with Preps., π. θυμὸν ἐς ἀμπλακίην 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 386 (nisi to be read παράγει); τινὰς ἐς λόγους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 22a; εἰς μῖσος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.2}}; τὰς συγγενείας εἰς ἔχθραν, εἰς ἄνοιαν τὴν πόλιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.174, 8.121; εἰς ὀργὴν ἢ φθόνον ἢ ἔλεον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354a25; εἰς γέλωτα [prev. work] 1415a37}}; τινὰ ἐπ᾽ ἀρετήν, opposed to προτρέψασθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.1; πάντας ἐκ.. πολέμων ἐπὶ τὴν ὁμόνοιαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.141; πρὸς.. κακίας ὑπερβολήν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.36; ἐμαυτὸν εἰς ἀπέχθειαν [prev. author] 23.1:}} —_middle_, ἐς γέλωτα προαγαγέσθαι τινά **move** one to laughter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. δ; τὴν ὑγρότητα αὐτῶν τοῦ ἤθους εἰς ἔλεον 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 33; προαξόμεθ᾽.. εἰς ἀνάγκην 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.14}}: c._infinitive_, τοῦτο πολεμίους προάγεται ἁμαρτάνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 5.15, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.117, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1270b2}}:—frequently in _passive_, προαχθέντας εἰς φιλοποσίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.22; εἰς τοῦτ᾽ ὀργῆς προήχθησαν ὥστε.. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 20.8}}: with _infinitive_, οὐ γὰρ ἔγωγε προαχθείην ἂν εἰπεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.79, compare 18.269, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 194a31; προάγεται λαλεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 164; πολλὰ προηγμένον πρᾶξαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.23, etc.}}
**5** **carry forward, advance,** π. τὴν πόλιν **lead** it **on to power,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.18}}; π. αὐτὴν (i.e. τὴν ἀρχὴν) ἐς τόδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.75, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1274a10; λόγοισι προάγει.., ἔργοισι δ᾽ οὐδὲ κινεῖ 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 300}}; οὕτω μέχρι πόρρω προήγαγον [τὴν ἔχθραν] **carried** it so far, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.163; π. [τὰ πράγματα] ἐπὶ τὸ βέλτιον [prev. author] “Prooem.” 38, etc.}}; τὴν πραγματείαν π. εἰς τὸ πρόσθεν **promote** the study, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Fragmenta Historica” 81}}; [τὰ μαθήματα] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 985b24; τὰς τέχνας [prev. author] “SE” 183b29, compare “Po.” 1449a13}}; π. καὶ διαρθρῶσαι τὰ καλῶς ἔχοντα τῇ περιγραφῇ **carry on** and complete.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1098a22, compare “Pol.” 1282b35}}:—_middle_, ἐς τοῦτο [τὰ Περσέων πρήγματα] προηγάγοντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.50}}:—_passive_, **increase, become rife,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.266.}}
**5.b** of persons, **promote** or **prefer** to honour, δᾶμος εἰς ἀριστοκρατίαν ἄνδρας αἰ προάγοι καλῶς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Isyllus Lyricus 3, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.13.6, etc.}}; τινὰς εἰς δόξαν, ἐφ᾽ ἡγεμονίας, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 7, “Galb.” 20, etc.; ἐπὶ μέγα προαχθῆναι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 55.}}
**5.c** **prefer** in the way of choice, especially in _passive_, αἱ προηγμέναι φυλαί {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 4.8.44: προηγμένος}} **distinguished, outstanding,** ὥρα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista Jun. “Im.Praef.”}}
**6** in Stoic Philos., of things neither good nor bad but **promoted** or **advanced** above the zero point of indifference, προηγμένον.. ὃ ἀδιάφορον [ὂν] ἐκλεγόμεθα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.48, compare 1st c.BC(?): Aristo Epigrammaticus [prev. work] 83, Chrysipp.[prev. work] 3.28, etc.}}; compare ἀποπροάγω.
**7** in _perfect_ _passive_ with _middle_ sense, οὕτω προῆκται τοὺς παῖδας ὥστε.. **has had** them **brought up** in such a way that.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.23}}: also in _passive_ sense, ἐπιεικῶς τοῖς ἔθεσι προηγμένοι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1180a8.}}
**8** **pronounce** a discourse, κατὰ θεωρίαν π. πάντα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.9.3; αἱ κατὰ σχῆμα προηγμέναι τῶν ὑποθέσεων [prev. work] 2.4.2.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **lead the way, go before,** πρόαγε δή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 227c; σοῦ προάγοντος ἐγὼ ἐφεσπόμην [prev. author] “Phd.” 90b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.6, etc.: with _accusative_ added, προῆγε πολὺ πάντας uncertain in 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.1.6 (to be read πάντων)}}: of a commander, **lead an advance, push forward,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.65.1, 3.35.1, etc.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, ὁ προάγων λόγος the **preceding** discourse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 719a; αἱ π. γραφαί 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 19.6.2; ὁ π. μήν “PSI” 5.450.59 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.3** **go on, advance,** ἐπὶ πολὺ προάγει τῇ τε βίᾳ καὶ τῇ ὠμότητι Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.181; ἐκ τῶν ἀσαφεστέρων ἐπὶ τὰ σαφέστερα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 184a19; πόρρω π. ὕβρεως 4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 6 (τὸ ἔργον προῆγε}} (ν) is variant for{προσῆγε} in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.92); πᾶς ὁ προάγων καὶ μὴ μένων ἐν τῇ διδαχῇ NT.2John.9: of Time, τῆς ἡμέρας ἤδη προαγούσης 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.8.1}}; **reach, attain to,** εἰς τὰς ὀκτὼ μυριάδας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ind.Sto.” 32.}}
**II.4** **excel,** τινος [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.71 (variant{προέχει}); ἀρχαιότητι 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 2.15.}}]

4255:
** προαιρ-έω**,
_future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 108.26:}}_aorist_ προεῖλον:—**bring forth, produce from one's stores,** προαιρούσαις λαθεῖν (probable reading) ἄλφιτον, ἔλαιον κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 419; ἰσχάδας 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 68; τὸν σῖτον.. ἐντεῦθεν προαιροῦντας πωλεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.90; ἐκ τοῦ ταμιείου 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 4.6, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 15, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 17, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 1.with}}
**II** mostly _middle_, late _imperfect_ ἐπροῃρούμην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.72 codices:}}_future_ -αιρήσομαι: _aorist_ -ειλόμην: _perfect_ _passive_ (in _middle_ sense) -ῄρημαι (see. below):—**take away first for oneself,** [ἀστραγάλους] ἐκ φορμίσκων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 206e}}; **remove,** Ῥωμαίων τὰς ἀφορμάς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.29.1.}}
**II.b** **elect previously,** τινὰς ἐκ τοῦ πλήθους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1298b27 (assuming variant); προελομένου τοῦ δήμου θεωρούς “Inscription Prien.” 108.152 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.2** **prefer,** τοῦ παρόντος κινδύνου τὸν μέλλοντα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.8.6}}: but mostly followed by a _preposition_, πρὸ τοῦ κεκινημένου τὸν σώφρονα προαιρεῖσθαι φίλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 245b; ἀντὶ ἀρετῆς.. οὐδ᾽ ἂν τὰ Σύρων.. πάντα προέλοιντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.12; κριτικήν τινα [ἐπιστήμην] ἐκ τῶν ἄλλων προειλόμεθα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 292b.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ only, **choose deliberately, prefer,** οὔτε Λακεδαίμονα προῃροῦ οὔτε Κρήτην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 52e, compare “Prt.” 327a, “La.” 200e, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 15.1, etc.; βιοῖ.. οὐδεὶς ὃν προαιρεῖται βίον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 65; ἃ λυσιτελεῖ προελέσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.31; οὐ προσήκοντας.. προῃρῆσθαι λόγους [prev. author] 18.129; τῷ προαιρεῖσθαι τἀγαθὰ ἢ τὰ κακὰ ποιοί τινές ἐσμεν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1112a2, compare “Rh.” 1382a35; τοῦτον τὸν ἀγῶνα προελόμενος}}**having undertaken,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 5; πολλὰ καὶ καλὰ καὶ μεγάλ᾽ ἡ πόλις προείλετο δἰ ἐμοῦ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.285; ταύτην π. τὴν σκέψιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1324a21}}; opposed to φεύγειν τι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1172a25, “Po.” 1450b9: _absolutely_, ὁ ἀκρατὴς ἐπιθυμῶν μὲν πράττει, προαιρούμενος δὲ οὔ}} not **by preference,** not **deliberately,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1111b14, compare 1135b9, “Rh.” 1368b11.}}
**II.4** with _infinitive_, **choose deliberately** to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Demodocus” 381b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1315a26, “IG” 22.448.53; π. τὸ κατεπεῖγον μᾶλλον πράττειν ἤ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.2.}}
**II.4.b** **purpose** or **propose** to do, εὖ δρᾶν προῃρημένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 96; ὑπὲρ ἐμοῦ προῄρησαι λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 28b; εἰ προαιρησόμεθα.. τούτου μεμνῆσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.176; π. λαβεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1290b25: with}}_infinitive_ omitted, πλὴν ὧν ἐγὼ προειλόμην (i.e. πρᾶξαι) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.190, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1136b15, “Pol.” 1301a19.}}

4259:
** προαύλ-ιον**, τό,
(αὐλός) **prelude on the flute,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 417e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1414b20: _metaphorically_, π. τοῦ λόγου 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 33.367a.}}
**II** αὐλή) **place before a court, vestibule,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.77, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**II.2** **space before a cattle-pen,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.16.}}

4260:
** προβαίνω**,
_future_ -βήσομαι: _perfect_ -βέβηκα: _aorist 2_ προὔβην, _imperative_ πρόβᾱ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 262, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 872 (Lyric poetry)}}, _plural_ πρόβᾱτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 841 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1047 (Lyric poetry): 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has only}}_perfect_ and _present_ _participle_ προβιβάς (as if from βίβημἰ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.18, but προβιβῶντα (-τἰ (as if from βιβάὠ [prev. work] 807, +others codices (see. below)}}; _imperative_ προβιβάσθων {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; _participle_ προβάοντε, read by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus for προβοῶντε, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.277; προβῶντες 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 126}}:— **step forward, advance,** κραιπνά, κοῦφα ποσὶ προβιβάς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.18, 158, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.27}}; τὸν δ᾽ ὦκα προβιβάντα (-βιβῶντα codices) πόδες φέρον {{Refs|15.555}}; ὑπασπίδια προβιβάντι (-βιβῶντι codices) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.807, compare 16.609; π. εὐθέσι τοῖς σκέλεσι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 604b5: with _accusative_ _cognate_, οἵαν ὁδὸν ἁ δειλαιοτάτα π. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 263 (Lyric poetry)}}; μέγα π. **take a** big **stride forward,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 60.}}
**b** of hair, **grow,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 64.50.}}
**2** as a mark of Time, ἄστρα προβέβηκε they **are far gone** in heaven, i.e. it is past midnight. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.252}}; ἡ νὺξ π. the night **is wearing fast,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.13: hence of Time itself, τοῦ χρόνου προβαίνοντος}} as time **went on,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.53, 140; ὁ μὲν χρόνος δὴ διὰ χρόνου προὔβαινέ μοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 285}}; also τὰ μὲν προβέβηκεν the **past,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 583}}; προβαίνοντος τοῦ ἔργου, τοῦ πολέμου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.23, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.47.3; τοῦ κώθωνος εὖ μάλα προβεβηκότος Hegesander Historicus 21}}; ἐκ τοῦ προβεβηκότος, **e re nata, on the spur of the moment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.12.2: of Age, προβήσεται ἡ ἡλικία 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 6}}; of persons, οἱ προβεβηκότες τῇ ἡλικίᾳ **advanced** in age, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.16, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.18}}; π. τῶν ἡμερῶν, ταῖς ἡμέραις, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Josh.13.1, 23.1: _absolutely_, οἱ π. 3rd c.BC: Bato Comicus 7.9, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 24; ἐπεὶ προέβη τοῖς ἔτεσιν 3rd c.BC: Macho Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.580c; προβεβηκότες ἐν ταῖς ἡμέραις αὐτῶν NT.Luke.1.7, compare 18; ἡλικίας εἰς τὸ πρόσθε π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 325c; π. εἰς πεντήκοντα ἔτη 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 68.4 (nisi to be read προεβεβιώκεἰ.}}
**3** _metaphorically_ of narrative, argument, action, events, μὴ πέρα προβῇς λόγου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 66; προβήσομαι ἐς τὸ πρόσω τοῦ λόγου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5}}; προβάς φησιν.. **further on,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1012.12, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.87 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; π. ἐκ τῶν κνημέων ἐς τοὺς μηρούς **went on..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.75}}; προέβαινε τὸ ἔθνος ἄρχον καὶ ἐπιτροπεῦον the nation **was organized in a series** of overlordships and mandates, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.134; προὔβης τῶνδε καὶ περαιτέρω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 249; π. ἐπ᾽ ἔσχατον θράσους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 853 (Lyric poetry); οἷ προβαίνει τὸ πρᾶγμα τοῦ βουλεύματος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 836; ποῖ προβήσεται λόγο; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 342; πέρας δὴ ποῖ κακῶν προβήσετα; [prev. author] “Or.” 511, compare 749; τὸ τῆς τύχης ἀφανὲς οἷ προβήσεται [prev. author] Alcaeus785; μὴ προβαίη μεῖζον ἢ τὸ νῦν κακόν [prev. author] “Med.” 907; τὸ ἔθος ἐπὶ πολὺ προβαίνει 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.179: _impersonal_, εἰς τοῦτο προβέβηκε ὥστε..}} it **has gone** so **far** that..,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 839c}}; π. πόρρω μοχθηρίας **to be** far **gone** in knavery, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 30; π. εἰς τοῦτο ἔχθρας ὥστε.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.16; εἰς ἀταξίαν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.38; μέχρι τίνος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.1.3; ἐπὶ τὸ χεῖρον π. τὰ πράγματα [prev. author] 5.30.6}}: in good sense, **make progress,** τοσοῦτον προβεβήκαμεν ὥστε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 187a}}; of an enterprise, **prosper, succeed**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1209.10 (1st c.BC), etc.}}
**II** **go before,** i.e. **be superior to,** another, πολὺ προβέβηκας ἁπάντων σῷ θάρσει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.125; κράτεϊ 16.54, compare 23.890}}; δυνάμει τε καὶ αἰδοῖ Τρηχῖνος προβέβηκε by might and awe **he is over,** i.e. **rules,** Trachis, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 355, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 1.5.}}
**III** with _accusative_ _of things_, **overstep,** τέρμα προβάς {{Refs|Pi “N.” 7.71.}}
**IV** with _accusative_ of the instrum. of motion, πόδα π. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 283; οὐκ ἂν προβαίην τὸν πόδα τὸν ἕτερον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 161, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist. Conscr.” 29; προβὰς δὲ κῶλον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1412; ἀρβύλαν προβάς [prev. author] “Or.” 1470 (Lyric poetry)}}; προβεβήκασι τὰ ἀριστερά **have** their **left legs foremost** (variant{προβεβλήκασι}, see at {προβάλλω} {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 706a7; προβὰς τὸν πόδα τὸν ἀριστερὸν καὶ τὸν δεξιὸν ὑποβάς 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 5.23.}}
**V** Causal, in _future_ _active_, **move forward, advance,** τίς τρόπος ἄνδρα προβάσει [ᾱ]; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.63.}}

4261:
** προβάλλω**,
_future_ -βᾰλῶ: _perfect_ -βέβληκα: _Epic dialect_ _aorist 2_ προβάλεσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.331: 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} has only _aorist_ _active_ and _middle_ without augment:—**throw** or **lay before, throw to,** Νότος Βορέῃ προβάλεσκε [σχεδίην] φέρεσθαι [same place]; τοὺς μαζοὺς κυσὶ προέβαλε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.112; τρωγάλια τοῖς θεωμένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 798; πυροὺς ὀλίγους π. [prev. author] “Av.” 626; π. τινὰ ταῖς Νύμφαις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e}}; ἀνδρὶ δέμας, of a woman, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cretan dialect” 6: without _dative_, π. ἀκήδευτα τὰ σώματα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 28.}}
**II** **put forward,** π. πρόβλημα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 261a}}; ἄμφω τὰ δεξιὰ προβεβληκώς, of a horse, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1460b19}} (also _middle_, τὰ ἀριστερὰ προβάλλονται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “IA” 706a6); χλαμύδα ἀλώπεκι 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.18.6; π. αὐτὸν ἐς τὸ μέσον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 25: _metaphorically_, ἀγαθὴν ἐλπίδα π. σαυτῷ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 572}}:—_passive_, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111.1.}}
**II.b** in obstetrics, **present,** _active_ and _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.69, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.60, +others}}
**II.2** ἔριδα προβαλόντες **putting forth** strife, i.e. striving, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.529.}}
**II.3** **put forward** as an argument or plea, ἐπεί μοι τὴν θέμιν προὔβαλες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 810; Κύπριν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 825; τοὔνομα τὸ τῆς εἰρήνης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.8}}:—_passive_, τὸν ὑφ᾽ ἁπάντων προβαλλόμενον λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.92; ἐς ἐνθυμίαν αἰεὶ προβαλλόμενος [prev. author] 5.16.}}
**II.4** _middle_, **put forward, propose for an office,** λῃτουργεῖν π. γυμνασίαρχον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.132}}:—_passive_, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.4.}}
**II.5** **propound** a question, task, problem, riddle (compare πρόβλημα IV), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 757, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 536d}}; αἴνιγμα, γρῖφον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Chrm.” 162b, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 74.5; χαλεπὴν π. ᾱἵρεσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 245b; εὔσκεπτον σκέψιν π. [prev. author] “Phlb.” 65d}}; ὰπορίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1283b35: later followed by _interrogative_ clause, πρόβαλε σαυτῷ τί ἂν ἐποίησεν ἐν τούτῳ Σωκράτης 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 33.12; θεοῦ προβαλόντος πότερον.. 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.41 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus:}} —_passive_, προβάλλεται τάδε θεωρῆσαι, περὶ τοῦ κώνου προβεβλημένα ἐστὶ τάδε, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “Con.Sph.Praef., Spir.Praef.”}}
**II.6** **put forth beyond,** κάρα.. ὀχημάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 740; τῶν ὀδόντων τὴν γλῶσσαν 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.7; φλέγμα καὶ ἀφρῶδες ἐκ τοῦ στόματος 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 1.2.}}
**III** **expose, give up,** π. σφέας αὐτοὺς ὑπὸ τοῦ κακοῦ **give** themselves **up for lost,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.141; ἐμαυτὸν εἰς ἀρὰς δεινάς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 745; ψυχὴν π. ἐν κύβοισι δαίμονος}}**hazard, venture,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 183.}}
**IV** **send forth, emit,** τράγου ὀσμήν variant (for προσ-) in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.50; τὴν φωνὴν ὀξεῖαν π. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.8; ἦχον τραχύν [prev. author] 5.30, etc.}}; **produce,** καρπόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 4.8.19; ἄνθος 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 12.1}}:—_passive_, with _genitive_, **to be emitted from,** αἱ τῶν θεῶν δυνάμεις προβεβλημέναι τῶν πρώτων {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.552S.}}
**V** _intransitive_, **stick out,** of the tongue, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 660a24.}}
**V.2** **fall forward,** εἰς τὸ μέτωπον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 487.}}
**B** _middle_ with _perfect_ _passive_ (used also in _passive_ sense, see below):— **throw** or **toss before one,** οὐλοχύτας προβάλοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.458, +others}}: hence, **throw away, expose,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1017.}}
**B.2** **set before** or **in front,** θεμείλιά τε προβάλοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.255.}}
**B.3** **set before oneself, propose to oneself,** ἔργον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 779.}}
**B.4** **put forward, propose for election,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.98, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 755cf, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.25, “IG” 22.1343.29, etc.; προβαλλόμενος ἑαυτόν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.15}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same place], Pl.[same place], etc.; προβληθεὶς πυλάγορος οὗτος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.149, compare285.}}
**B.5** with _dative_ et _infinitive_, **challenge** a person to..,π. μοι [ὀμόσαι] Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Chr.” 32i14, compare ii 13(2nd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, of the oath, **to be proposed as a challenge,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work]ii 25, “Sammelb.” 5231.9 (1st c.AD).}}
**B.II** **throw beyond, beat in throwing**: hence, **surpass, excel,** with _genitive_ _person_ et _dative_ _of things_, ἐγὼ δέ κε σεῖο νοήματί γε προβαλοίμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.218.}}
**B.III** **hold before oneself** so as to protect, λαιᾷ ἴτυν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 15.3; Πηλεΐδᾳ κατ᾽ ὄμμα πέλταν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 370 (Lyric poetry); τὼ χεῖρε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 201}}; π. τὰ ὅπλα **level** arms, opposed to μεταβάλλεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (προβολή 1), τὴν φάλαγγα ἐκέλευσε προβαλέσθαι τὰ ὅπλα καὶ ἐπιχωρῆσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.17, compare 6.5.16, “Mem.” 3.8.4: in}}_perfect_ _passive_, σάρισαν προβεβλημένος **having** his pike **advanced,** with levelled pike, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.100; τοὺς θυρεοὺς πρὸ τῶν νώτων.. -βεβλημένοι 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 36.1; εἰκοσάπηχύν τινα προβεβλ. κοντόν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 27.4}}; also προβεβλημένοι τοὺς θωρακοφόρους **having** them **to cover** one **in front,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.24}}; π. τάφρον, ποταμόν, of a general, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.18.3, 2.5.5; π. τῆς.. στρατοπεδείας τεῖχος [prev. author] 1.48.10, etc.; πόλις -βεβλημένη ποταμόν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 11.2.17}}; π. τὰ θηρία πρὸ τῶν κεράτων, λογχοφόρους τῆς δυνάμεως, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.72.9, 3.113.6}}: _absolutely_, **stand in front,** πρὸ ἀμφοῖν προβεβλημένος **standing so as to cover** both, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.21, compare “Cyr.” 2.3.10: with _genitive_, τούτου προβέβληται Πολύευκτος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.139; προβάλλεσθαι ἢ ἐναντίον βλέπειν οὔτ᾽ οἶδεν οὔτ᾽ ἐθέλει [prev. author] 4.40}}; προαίρεσις τῆς πολιτείας προβεβλημένη a **guarded** policy, {{Refs|[prev. author] 19.27}}; πρὸς ἅπαντας -βεβλημένος **on one's guard** against, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Dio” 9}}:—_passive_, ἱππῆς προβέβληνται πρὸ τοῦ δεξιοῦ κέρως {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 36.2; κράνη πρὸ τῆς κεφαλῆς π. [prev. work] 34.3.}}
**B.III.2** _metaphorically_, **put forward,** τὴν ἀγαθὴν προβαλλόμενος ἐλπίδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.97; ταύτην τὴν συμμαχίαν [prev. work]195; τὴν Εὔβοιαν προβαλέσθαι πρὸ τῆς Ἀττικῆς [prev. work]301, compare 300, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.122; τι πρὸ τῆς αἰσχύνης 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.11.}}
**B.III.2.b** **bring forward, cite on one's own part, in defence,** τὸν Ὅμηρον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 201b; π. μάρτυρας 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.3, etc.}}; ὁ προβαλόμενος **one who has brought evidence,** Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 46.10}}; **cite as an example,** ἔθνος οὐδὲν ἔχομεν προβαλέσθαι σοφίης πέρι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.46}}; **use as an excuse** or **pretext,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.87, etc.; τὸ εὐπρεπὲς ἄσπονδον προβέβληνται [prev. author] 1.37}}; π. σκῆψιν, πρόφασιν, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.56.7, 15.20.3.}}
**B.IV** in _Attic dialect_ law, **accuse** a person by προβολή (see. προβολή see), **present him as guilty** of the offence, προὐβαλόμην ἀδικεῖν τοῦτον περὶ τὴν ἑορτήν [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.1 (compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus see at {προβαλλομένους}); π. τινά τι [prev. work] 28}}]; τινα alone, {{Refs|[prev. work] 175}}; ὁ προβαλλόμενος the **prosecutor** in a προβολή, {{Refs|[prev. work] 179}}:—_passive_, **to be accused** or **presented,** προὐβλήθησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.35}}: generally, **attack, censure,** τὸ ἔθος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.24, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.137; τοὺς ψευδομένους 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.8.7 (assuming variant), compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “C. Gracchus” 14}}; opposed to **ἐπαινεῖν**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.18d.}}

4263:
** πρόβᾰτον**, τό,
frequently in _plural_ πρόβατα (but also in _singular_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 43, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 302a, etc.)}}; irregular _dative_ πρόβασι{{Refs 5th c.AD+|Hdn.Gr. 1.414, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus:}} — used (among the Ionians and Dorians) of all four-footed **cattle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.41, etc.; πάντων τῶν π. βόες μάλιστα ἀτονέουσι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 8; τὰ ἄλλα π. καὶ ἵππους μάλιστα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.61, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 316, “IG” 12(1).677.31 (Rhodes, 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; of Europa's bull, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 28: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} generally of **cattle, flocks** and **herds,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.124, 23.550, “hymnus ad Mercurium” 571, compare “IG” 12(7).62.35 (Amorgos, 4th c.BC)}}; τὰ π. καὶ καρταίποδα {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 4.35}}; opposed to ἄνθρωποι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 558, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.203}}; τὰ λεπτὰ τῶν π. small **cattle,** i.e. sheep and goats, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 133, 8.137; τὸ μὲν μέζον π..., τὸ δὲ μεῖον “IG” 5 (2).3.14 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}; so later, π. ἀπὸ τῶν ἀρνῶν καὶ τῶν ἐρίφων λήψεσθε {{LXX|LXX.Exo.12.5: but in _Attic dialect_ Prose and Comedy texts (never in Trag.)}} almost invariably of **sheep,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 714, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.14, “IG” 22.1672.289, etc.; ὥσπερ π. βῆ βῆ λέγων βαδίζει 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 43}}; so in later _Boeotian dialect_, {{Refs|“IG” 7.3171.39,44 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_)}}: generally, **animals for slaughter,** whether for sacrifices, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.56}}; or for food, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.207; compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.29.}}
**2** proverbial of stupid, lazy people, ἀριθμός, πρόβατ᾽ ἄλλως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1203, compare “V.” 32: Comedy texts _comparative_, προβάτου προβάτερον}} **more sheepish** than **a sheep,** uncertain conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 122}}; χρυσοῦν π., = Latin _pecus aurea,_ as nickname, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 59.8}}: in other proverbs, τοὺς γευομένους κύνας τῶν π. κατακόπτειν φασὶ δεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.40; λέων ἐν προβάτοις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cleomenes” 33, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.35.13.}}
**II** name of a sea-fish, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.146, 3.139, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 9.38}}. (Orig. of small cattle, sheep and goats, which in primitive mixed herds **walk in front** (προβαίνει) of the larger animals.)

4264:
** προβῐβ-άζω**,
_future_ **-άσω**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 58.23}}, _Attic dialect_ προβῐβῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1570:}} — causal of προβαίνω, **cause to step forward, lead on,** τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 180 (Lyric poetry)}}; ποῖ προβιβᾷς ἡμᾶς ποτ; to what point, how far **do you mean to carry** us ? Ar.[same place]; τινὰ εἰς ἀρετήν, εἰς ἐγκράτειαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.1}}; ἕως Μακεδονίας τὴν ἀρχήν **extend** it.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.3}}; **push on,** οὐδὲν ἠδύνατο π. τῶν ἔργων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.100.1}}:—_passive_, **to be developed, improved,** of machines, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 74.4.}}
**2** **push forward, advance,** τὸ ὑπερκείμενον τοῦ κρημνοῦ (by building a wall) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.78}}; **exalt,** τὴν πατρίδα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.10.4}}; τινὰ ἐς τὰς ἀρχάς **promote** him, D.C.[same place]; δύναμιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.40 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**3** **teach,** τινάς τι {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Deu.6.7, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 36 (uncertain)}}; **put forward** as a representative, {{NT|NT.Act.19.33 (variant)}}:— _passive_, **to be instructed** or **egged on,** ὑπὸ τῆς μητρός {{NT|NT.Matt.14.8.}}
**II** _intransitive_, ={προβαίνω}, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.44.1, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.231 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**III** of a male, **mount before,** ἄλλην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 546a10.}}

4266:
** προγίγνομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ and later **προγεωργ-γίνομαι** [ῑ]: _future_ -γενήσομαι: _aorist_ προὐγενόμην: _perfect_ προγέγονα and -γεγένημαι:— **come forward,** οἱ δὲ τάχα προγένοντο quickly they **came in sight,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.525, compare “Hymni Homerici” 7.7; ἄμυδις προγένοντο 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 345; εἴσω π. 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 2.103; κόπρον ἔπι π. 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 178, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.134: with _genitive_, ὠκεανοῖο.. ὁπότε προγένωνται Ἰχθύες 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 706; ἀστὴρ ὑπὲρ τὸν ὁρίζοντα πρὸ ἁλίου προγενόμενος 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 97a.}}
**II** **to be born before, exist before,** ἢν.. προγεγονότες ἔωσι πρὶν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.3; οἱ προγεγονότες θεοί [prev. author] 2.146}}; οἱ π. ἄνθρωποι **former** men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.8.10; οἱ προγεγενημένοι [prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.7.24, etc.}}; οἱ προγενόμενοι the **previous** crews, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.17.12.}}
**II.2** of events, etc., ταῦτά μοι προὐγεγόνει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 219e}}; αἱ ἀκοαὶ τῶν προγεγενημένων reports **of things of old time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.20, etc.; τὰ προγεγονότα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 1, etc.}}; προγεγενημένοι [πόλεμοι], καιροί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, Decrees Byzantine cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.90; οἱ προγεγονότες ἡμῖν ἔμπροσθεν λόγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 699e, compare “PHib.” 1.96.8 (3rd c.BC)}}; αἱ διὰ τῆς ψυχῆς ἡδοναὶ πρὸ τῶν διὰ τοῦ σώματος προγίγνοιντ᾽ ἄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 39d.}}
**III** simply, **to be born,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 6.255, 336.}}

4267:
** προγιγνώσκω**,
_Ionic dialect_ and later **προγεωργ-γῑνώσκω**: _future_ -γνώσομαι: _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ _infinitive_ προγνώμεναι {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 257}}:—**know, perceive, learn,** or **understand beforehand,** τι [same place]; τὰ στοιχεῖα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 203d: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1072; π. ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 8.12}}:—in Medicine texts, **know before being told, declare unaided,** τά τε παρεόντα καὶ τὰ προγεγονότα καὶ τὰ μέλλοντα ἔσεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 1; τοὺς καιρούς [prev. author] “Aër.” 2.}}
**2** **prognosticate,** π. χειμῶνα αἱ μέλιτται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 627b10.}}
**3** **foreknow,** λαόν {{NT|NT.Rom.11.2, +others}}
**4** with _genitive_, π. τῶν θεῶν **learn** things **in advance of..,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 8.7.}}
**II** **judge beforehand,** ἐς τὸ μέλλον καλὸν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64}}; **provide,** τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.11:}} —_passive_, παρὰ τῷ διαιτητῇ προεγνωσμένος ἀδικεῖν **judged beforehand** to have done wrong, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.58, compare “Inscription Délos” 502 A 12(3rd c.BC).}}

4268:
** πρό-γνωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**foreknowledge,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Jdt.9.6, NT.Act.2.23, NT.1Pet.1.2.}}
**II** **perceiving beforehand,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.399d, 982c, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 8, etc.}}
**II.b** in Medicine texts, **prognosis** of diseases, Κῳακαὶ π. title of work by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.490, 18(2).11, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.382 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}; title of work by Democritus.
**III** **prediction,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 17.13, +others}}

4269:
** πρόγον-ος**, ον,
(γίγνομαι, γέγονα) **early-born,** [ἄρνες], opposed to μέτασσαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.221, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Hermesianax Elegiacus 7.74, “SIG” 1038.9 (Eleusis, 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; **first-born,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 2.1301 (uncertain).}}
**2** ={ἀπόγονος} 1, uncertain in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.50.}}
**II** **forefather, ancestor,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.59; πατρός σου π. πατήρ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 267, compare “Hel.” 15, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 11b: frequently in _plural_, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.105, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 405, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.150, etc.; οἱ ἄνωθεν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 236e; οἱ πάλαι π. [prev. author] “Ep.” 359d; ἐκ προγόνων [prev. author] “Tht.” 173d}}; also of gods or heroes who are the **authors** or **founders** of a race, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 273, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.127, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 186e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.14, etc.; Ζεῦ πρόγονε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1242; θεοὶ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 302d}}: also as _feminine_, π. γυνή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 533 (Lyric poetry), compare 43 (Lyric poetry): _metaphorically_, οἱ π.}} the **fathers** or **founders** of a school, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 15; τὸν π. τῆς ἐμαυτοῦ σοφίας 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 8.7}}; ἰὼ πόνοι π. πόνων troubles **parents** of troubles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1197 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** **child by a former marriage, step-son,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1329, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isocrate” 18, “Mon.Anc.Gr.” 16.9, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cal.” 26, “Supp.epigram” 6.667 (from Attalia), “PFay.” 48.3 (1st c.AD)}}, etc.: _feminine_, **step-daughter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 79, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 12.5, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 10, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.43, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 9}}; rarely προγόνη (which see): irregular _superlative_ προγονέστατος **eldest step-son,** uncertain in {{Refs|“TAM” 2(1).246.18 (from Sidyma).}}

4270:
** προγράφω** [ᾰ],
**write before** or **first,** τὰς αἰτίας προὔγραψα πρῶτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23}}; **write before** or **above,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|NT.Eph.3.3; αἱ προγεγραμμέναι λέξεις 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.7.5; κατὰ τὰ -γεγραμμένα “PPetr.” 3p.179 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὁ προγεγραμμένος ἀριθμός **before-mentioned,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1018c.}}
**2** **write as a copy,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.18.}}
**II** **set forth as a public notice,** π. τι ἐν πινακίοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 450}}; π. κρίσιν, δίκην τινί, **give notice of** a trial, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.42, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 11 (_passive_)}}; **appoint** or **summon by public notice,** ἐκκλησίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.60,61}}; χορηγοὺς π. **appoint** as choregi, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1352a1; π. τινὰ [κληρωθησόμενον τ]ῆς φυλῆς ἣν ἂν βούληται “Supp.epigram” 4.183.15 (Halic., 3rd c.BC)}}; π. τοὺς λειτουργήσοντας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.73, compare 74 (_passive_, Andania, 1st c.BC)}}; στρατιᾶς κατάλογον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 39; φρουρᾶς ἡμῖν προγραφείσης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.3; π. ὅσα δεῖ χρηματίζειν τὴν βουλήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 43.3; ἀπὸ τίνος ἄρχοντος καὶ ἐπωνύμου μέχρι τίνων δεῖ στρατεύεσθαι [prev. work] 53.7}}; οἷς κατ᾽ ὀφθαλμοὺς.. Χριστὸς προεγράφη **was proclaimed** or **set forth publicly,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Gal.3.1, compare “Supp.epigram” 4.263.13, 15 (Panamara, 1st c.AD)}}:—_middle_, περὶ ὧν προεγράψατο εἰς τὴν βουλήν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Milet.” 6.43 (3rd c.BC),compare “SIG” 562.3(Paros, 3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**II.2** **give written notice of sale,** παρὰ τῇ ἀρχῇ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 97.1, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.205c}}; **sell by auction,** ἐν τῷ πραιτωρίῳ τὰ κτήματα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 51.4.}}
**II.3** = Latin _proscribere,_ π. τινὰς φυγάδας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 32.5.12}}; οἱ προγεγραμμένοι **the proscribed,**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 6.1; οἱ π. ὑπὸ Σύλλα 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.2.6; οἱ προγραφέντες 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 47.13; οἱ προγραφέντες ἐπὶ θανάτῳ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 27: _metaphorically_, οἱ προγεγρ. εἰς τοῦτο τὸ κρίμα}} **those whose names have been registered** for condemnation, {{NT|NT.Jude.4.}}
**III** **write** a name **at the head of a list,** π. τινὰ ἐπὶ τῶν ψηφισμάτων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 10}}; τῆς βουλῆς π... Μάρκον, of the censor, **name** Marcus **princeps senatus,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aem.” 38, compare “Flam.” 18}}:—_passive_, προγράφεσθαι τοῦ συνεδρίου {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.318c; προγεγραμμένος τῆς βουλῆς [prev. author] “TG” 4.}}

4271:
** πρόδηλ-ος**, ον,
**clear** or **manifest in front** or **beforehand,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Oxy.” 1789 “fragment” 1 ii4, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.30, etc.; ἐμβολὴ π. ἥτις γίνοιτ᾽ ἂν ἁρμόζουσα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 30, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 190 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Epitaphius” 8}}; οἱ π. [φόβοι] **foreseen,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1117a19, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.19, +others; τοῦ μὲν ὄντος π. τοῦ δὲ ἀγνοουμένου 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.37; εἰ μὲν ἦν π. τὰ μέλλοντα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.196; π. εἵλοντο θανάτους 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.54.4; πρόδηλον ἥδη ἦν, ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 238b, etc.}}; **evident,** καὶ τυφλῷ, φασι, pro/dhlon {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Polystr. p.8 W.; πρόδηλα γάρ [ἐστι], ὅτι.. μέλλουσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 3.3}}; ἐκ προδήλου from **a place in sight, in full view,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1429 (Lyric poetry)}}. _adverb_ **-λως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.182, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Otho” 9; θανεῖν π. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1311.}}
**2** ={προδηλωτικός}, with _genitive_, {{Refs|Vett. Val. 92.22, +others}}

4272:
** προδίδωμι**,
**give beforehand, pay in advance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.7, “IG” 22.1304.34}}; προεδίδου conjecture for προσ- in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.15.7}}; προδιδούς, opposed to ἐπιδιδούς, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.174}}; **give first,** {{NT|NT.Rom.11.35}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1350a36; τῶν -δεδομένων τιμῶν “Inscription Prien.” 107.17, compare “GDI” 5181.34 (from Crete)}}; of a menu-tablet, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.49d.}}
**II** **give up,** [κλῆρον] {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.96 (3rd c.BC)}}; **deliver up,** τοὺς ὁμοκωμήτας ἡμῖν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PThead.” 17.16 (4th c.AD)}}: most frequently, **give up to the enemy, betray,** τοὺς λοιποὺς τοῖσι Σαμίοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.23; τὸ σὸν θνητοῖσι π. γέρας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 38, etc.; π. τὴν Ποτείδαιαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.128; τὰν φυγάδα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 420 (Lyric poetry); ἱκέτας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 246; πυργώματα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 251}}; τὰς πύλας, φρούριον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 766, “Ra.” 362}}; of a woman, π. τὸ σῶμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 90}}: with _infinitive_, ὃν σὺ προὔδωκας θανεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1588, cf. Alcaeus659}}:—_passive_, προδοθέντες ὑπὸ Σιτάλκεω ἥλωσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.137; ἀπόλωλα τλήμων, προδέδομαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 923.}}
**II.2** **forsake, abandon,** οἵ με φίλοι προὔδωκαν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 813; π. τὴν Ἑλλάδα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.7.β, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 408; μηδαμῶς.. προδῷς με [prev. author] “Th.” 229; τὴν μητέρα π. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.5; τὴν πολιτείαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 762c; σαυτόν [prev. author] “Cri.” 45c}}:—_passive_, προδεδόμεθα ὑπὸ τῶν συμμάχων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.60, compare 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 78.19.}}
**II.3** _absolutely_, **play false, desert,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.113, 6.15, etc.}}; οὔτοι προδώσει χρησμός **will** not **prove traitor,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 269; χάρις.. προδοῦσ᾽ ἁλίσκεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1267}}; ἢν προδιδῶσι πρὸς τοὺς κατιόντας **treat treasonably** with them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.45: with _accusative_ _cognate_, προδοσίαν π.}}**to be guilty of treachery,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.10.}}
**II.4** with a thing as subject, **betray, fail** one, [αἱ κάτω πλίνθοι] π. τὰς ἄνω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.5; ὁ ὀφθαλμὸς π. τινά 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 52.13}}: _intransitive_,**fail,** of wine, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 1.5}}; of a river, **run dry,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.187}}; of a barricade that has proved useless, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.52.}}
**II.5** with a thing as object, **surrender, give up,** προδέδοται τὰ κρυπτά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1140}}; χάριν π. to be thankless, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Heracl.” 1036; τὰ πράγματα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 241; τὸ δοκοῦν ἀληθὲς οὐχ ὅσιον προδιδόναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 607c; τὸ δίκαιον [prev. author] “Lg.” 907a}}; ἑτέροισι τὴν νίκην{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 906e; καιρὸν τοῖς ἐναντίοις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.6}}; **to be false to, fail to uphold,** ὅρκους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.22; τὴν καταχειροτονίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.120}}; **give up as lost, bid adieu to,** ἡδονάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1166; τὰς ἐλπίδας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1500; τὴν ἐκείνου προαίρεσιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.28; τὸν ἀγῶνα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.115.}}

4273:
** προ-δότης**, ου,
_Doric dialect_ **-ας**, ὁ, **betrayer, traitor,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.30, 144, 5th c.BC: Timocreon Lyricus 1.5, etc.}}; π. πατρός, πατρίδος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1057, “Ph.” 996, etc.; ὁ ἐν λέχει π. [prev. author] “Med.” 206 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. τῶν ὅρκων **traitor** to his oaths, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 71: _metaphorically_, τῆς ὑγιείης 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 234.}}
**2** **one who abandons in danger,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1068 (_anapaest meter_); π. τινὸς καταστῆναι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.26.}}
**3** as _adjective_, irregular _comparative_ **-ίστερον**{{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}

4274:
** πρόδρομ-ος**, ον,
**running forward with headlong speed,** π. ἦλθον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 211 (Lyric poetry); φυγάδα πρόδρομον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 108 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; μόλε π. variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 296 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** **running before, going in advance,** π. στρατιή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.14; κήρυκας π. προπέμπειν [prev. author] 1.60; π. ἥκω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 424}}; frequently of horsemen **in advance of** an army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.121, 122; π. τῶν ἄλλων ἥκειν [prev. author] 7.203, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.22; λεὼς π. ἱππότας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 80 (Lyric poetry)}}; **mounted skirmishers,** οἱ π. **'guides',** a special corps in the {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Macedonius Epigrammaticus army, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.12.7, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.17; οἱ ἀμφὶ [τὸν ἵππαρχον] πρόδρομοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 1.25; at Athens, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 49.1}}; also, of light ships, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.11.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **precursor,** ἀστέρα.. ἀελίου π.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Ion Lyric poetry 10; ἠπίαλος πυρετοῦ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 332 (_anapaest meter_); δείπνου π. ἄριστον 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 75.13; π. τοῦ δοκοῦντος καλλίστου εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 154a, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.7.}}
**4** π. (i.e. οἶνος), see at {πρότροπος}.
**II** as _substantive_, πρόδρομοι, οἱ,
**II.1** see above {{Refs|1.2.}}
**II.2** **northerly winds, preceding the etesian winds,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 361b24, “Pr.” 941b7, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Ventis” 11.}}
**II.3** **early figs,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “CP” 5.1.5f, compare 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 16.113.}}

4275:
** προεῖδον**,
_aorist_ with no _present_ in use, προοράω being used instead, _participle_ προϊδών, _infinitive_ προϊδεῖν:— **look forward,** ὀξὺ μάλα προϊδών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.393}}; **see beforehand, catch sight of,** μή πώς με προϊδὼν.. ἀλέηται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.396; ὅτε προΐδωσιν ἰόντα κίρκον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.756, compare 18.527, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14}}:—_middle_, προΐδωνται [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.155; χαλεπὸς προϊδέσθαι καπρός 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 386 (variant{προσιδ-}).}}]
**2** **foresee, portend,** κακότητος ἀνάγκας Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.140; ἐσσόμενον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.27: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 691b}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.8, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.68, etc.}}
**II** **take thought for,** ἡμέων οἰκοφθορημένων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.144}}; καθ᾽ ἡσυχίαν τι αὐτῶν (i.e. τῶν ἀποβαινόντων) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.83:}} —mostly in _middle_, προϊδόμενος (προειδομένους codices) αὐτῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.64; τοῦ μέλλοντος προϊδέσθαι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 45.19; ὅπως μὴ.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.17; προϊδέσθαι ὑπέρ τινος [prev. author] 23.134}}; οὐδὲν τοῦ χωρίου προείδετο **did** not **worry about..,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 56.13.}}

4277:
** προεῖπον**
(also -εῖπα{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.114.8)}}_aorist_ with no _present_ in use, προλέγω and προαγορεύω being used, _participle_ προειπών, _infinitive_ προειπεῖν:— **foretell,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3c, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.601}}; **premise,** τοῦτο προειπόντα ἐπειπεῖν τὰ ἔμπροσθεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1394b31.}}
**II** **proclaim** or **declare publicly,** ἀλλήλοισι πόλεμον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9. ; ξεινίην τοῖσι Ἀκανθίοισι π. [prev. author] 7.116; ἀγῶνας ἑκάστοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.18}}; νικητήρια ταῖς τάξεσι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 2.1.24; θάνατον αὐτῷ π. μὴ πράξαντι ταῦτα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 698c}}; π. τινὶ φόνου **make proclamation** of murder against him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.9, cf. Legal cited in [same work] 43.57; π. τοῖς θεοῖς ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 401a}}; ὀνυμάξει αὐτὸν προειπὼν τρῖς ἁμέρας **giving notice of** three days (within which he must answer the call), {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.167 (from Cyrene)}}; ἐν ἡμέραις πέντε ἀφ᾽ ἧς ἂν ἀλλήλοις προείπωσιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 1050.27 (1st c.AD).}}
**III** with _infinitive_, **order** or **command before,** πρό οἱ εἴπομεν.., μήτ᾽ αὐτὸν κτείνειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.37, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.21, 155, 7.12, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 351; οἱ νόμοι προεῖπον αὐτῷ μὴ δημηγορεῖν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.3; π. τοῖς καδεσταῖς ἀλλύεθθαι “Leg.Gort.” 2.28}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, π. σῖτον ἐσάγειν τὸν βουλόμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26}}; π. αὐτῷ δήσειν **threatened** him that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.17.}}
**III.2** **enjoin,** with _accusative_, π. Λυδοῖσι τὰ ὁ Κροῖσος ὑπετίθετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.156.}}

4279:
** προεπ-αγγέλλω**,
**announce before,** ὡς μαντευσόμενοι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 38.13; π. σφίσιν αὐτὸ τοῦθ᾽ ὅπως.. [prev. author] 40.32}}:—_passive_, εὐλογία -ηγγελμένη {{NT|NT.2Cor.9.5; τὰ -ηγγελμένα}}**matters on which orders had been issued,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 6.27.1.}}
**II** **canvass for an office before,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 39.31.}}
**III** _middle_, **announce before,** εὐαγγέλιον {{NT|NT.Rom.1.2}}:—_passive_, **to be promised before,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 42.32, 46.40.}}

4280:
** προερέω**,
_Attic dialect_ _contraction_ **προερῶ**, serving as _future_ to προεῖπον(which see): also _perfect_ προείρηκα, _passive_ -ημαι: _aorist_ _passive_ προερρήθην, _contraction_ προὐρρήθην:— **say beforehand,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 292d, etc.}}:—_passive_, ἐκ τῶν προειρημένων {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 75b; κατὰ τὰ π. [prev. author] “R.” 408c; τοῖς π. συμφωνεῖν [prev. work] 398c}}; τὰ προρρηθέντα{{Refs|[prev. work] 619c; ταῦτά μοι προειρήσθω}}**be said by way of preface,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.14, compare 5.29.}}
**II** **order beforehand** or **publicly,** συλλέγεσθαι ἐς Σάρδις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.77, 81; π. τῷ στρατῷ ὡς.. ἀκουστέα εἴη [prev. author] 3.61}}:— _passive_, προὐρρήθη ὅπως.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 198e; προείρητο αὐτοῖς μὴ ἐπιχειρεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.40}}; ἔχοντες τὸ προειρημένον the **prescribed** implement, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126; ἀπικέσθαι ἐς τὴν π. ἡμέρην [prev. author] 6.128}}; δεῖπνον.. ἐκ πολλοῦ χρόνου π. **ordered beforehand,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.119}}; πόλεμος προερρήθη **was declared,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.17.}}
**II.2** ὀνυμαστὶ προερεῖ **will call** him **publicly** by name, {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.167 (from Cyrene).}}

4281:
** προέρχομαι**
(πρόειμι serves as _future_), _aorist_ προῆλθον: _perfect_ προελήλῠθα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 113.2}}:—**go forward, advance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.207, 9.14; ἐς τὸ ὁμαλόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.65; ἐς τὸ πλέον [prev. author] 2.21; ἐκ τοῦ χωρίου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.25; ἐπὶ τὸ βῆμα 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.58:}} _absolutely_, προελθών, = _Attic dialect_ παρελθών, **having come forward** to speak, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.14.7; προελθὼν ὁ κῆρυξ ἐκήρυττε.. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.154; π. εἰς τὸν δῆμον “SIG” 742.49 (Ephesus, 1st c.BC)}}: with _accusative_ _cognate_, π. ἡμερησίαν ὁδόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 616b; κατὰ τὴν ὁδόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.16.}}
**b** **come forth,** πλάγια π. τὰ ἔμβρυα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 576a24; π. μητρός}}**to be born,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “Vita Platonis;” p.1 W.: generally, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 25, +others}}; **appear, be published,** of a book, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.1.54.}}
**c** **go away from, leave,** ἀπὸ τῆς ἑαυτοῦ [οἰκίας] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 472.5 (2nd c.AD),compare “Stud.Pal.” 1.8.10 (5th c.AD)}}; οὐδεπώποτε ἐξ Αἰθιοπίας τὸν ἕτερον πόδα προελθών {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 32.}}
**2** of Time, προελθόντος πολλοῦ χρόνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 273a; π. κατὰ χρόνον [prev. author] “Prm.” 152a}}; of persons, προεληλυθότες ταῖς ἡλικίαις **advanced** in years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.5.}}
**3** **go on, proceed,** in a story or argument, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 237c; εἰς τὸ πρόσθεν π. [prev. author] “Lg.” 682a, compare “Prt.” 339d.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, [τὰ Περσέων πρήγματα] ἐς τοῦτο προελθόντα the power of the Persians **having advanced** to this height, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.50; ὥσπερ μαθητὴν εἰς τοὔμπροσθε π.}}**make progress,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 4.10; ἐνταῦθα π. ὥστε.. [prev. author] 15.82}}: frequently in bad sense, εἰς πᾶν π. μοχθηρίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.3; οὕτως αἰσχρῶς π. [prev. author] 23.204; οἷ προελήλυθ᾽ ἀσελγείας ἅνθρωπος [prev. author] 4.9; εἰς τοῦτ᾽ ἀναισθησίας καὶ τόλμης προεληλύθασιν [prev. author] 24.182}}; πόρρω προεληλύθασι φυλακῆς they **are far gone** in cautiousness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 4.4.}}
**5** **go before** or **first,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.3.9, etc.}}; π. τινός **go before** him,{{NT|[prev. work] 2.2.7; π. τινάς NT.Mark.6.33.}}
**5.b** **arrive first,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.100}}: _perfect_, **have travelled first,** ὁδόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328e.}}
**II** **take legal proceedings, appear in court,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PGiss. 8.12(2nd c.AD), etc.}}

4284:
** προέχω**,
_contraction_ **προὔχω**, as always in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, except 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.11 (see. infr. B), also in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus and 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus:}}_future_ προέξω: _aorist_ προέσχον, _middle_ προεσχόμην, προὐσχόμην, compare προΐσχω:— **hold before,** τὴν ἀσπίδα τῆς κωλῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 989}}; especially so as to protect another, τὼ χεῖρε π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.10}}:—_middle_, **hold before oneself,** προὔχοντο ἑκάστοθι ἐννέα ταύρους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.8}}; **hold out before one,** πρὸ δούρατ᾽ ἔχοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.355, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.42}}; προὐσχόμην σε **held** you **out** as a child (to do your needs), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1385.}}
**2** _metaphorically_ in _middle_, **put forward as a pretext,** τάδ᾽ ἂν προὔχοιο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 80}}; ὅπερ μάλιστα προὔχονται εἰ καθαιρεθείη, μὴ ἂν γίγνεσθαι τὸν πόλεμον the cancellation of which is the chief consideration in return for which they **pretend** that war would not occur, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140.}}
**2.b** **hold forth, offer,** ἃ προείχοντο αὐτοῖς {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.68: προΐσχομαι (which see) is more usual in this sense.}}
**II** **to be possessed** or **informed of** a thing **beforehand,** π. τῶν Ἀθηναίων οὐ φιλίας γνώμας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.4, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 31.27, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 43.3.}}
**II.2** **have before,** ἃ προεῖχεν μισθῷ ὁ.. Πτολεμαῖος **had hitherto** on lease, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 889.9 (2nd c.AD),compare “Sammelb.” 5672.5 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; εἰς τοῦτο ὑπολόγησον ὃ προέχουσι what they **have already received,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 2p.32(3rd c.BC): _absolutely_, ὁ προέχων}} the **first recipient,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1164b9.}}
**B** _intransitive_, **jut out, project,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} in a local sense, of headlands, towers, hills, ὅθ᾽ ἀκρότατος πρόεχ᾽ ἀκτή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.11, compare 10.90; ἐπ᾽ ἠϊόνας προὐχούσας 6.138; πύργῳ ἐπὶ προὔχοντι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.97; ἐπὶ προὔχοντι μελάθρῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.544; ἀκτὴ προέχουσα ἐς τὸν πόντον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.177, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.109, 6.97; τὸ προὔχον τῆς ἐμβολῆς [prev. author] 2.76}}: generally, **project,** with _genitive_, προέχουσα κάρης εὐρεῖα καλύπτρη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 125.}}
**B.II** in running, **to be the first, have the start,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.325: with _genitive_, ἡμέρης ὁδῷ π. τῶν Περσέων}} **keep ahead** of them by a day's march, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.120}}; προέχων τῶν ἄλλων [ὁ ἵππος] **getting before** the rest, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.22}}; τῇ κεφαλῇ π. **beat** by a head, in racing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.16: of Time, προεῖχε [ἡ τριήρης] ἡμέρᾳ καὶ νυκτί}} **started first** by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.49; π. εἴκοσιν ἔτεσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 879c}}: _metaphorically_, **have the advantage of,** τινὸς τῷ διπλασίῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.2.}}
**B.II.2** of rank, with _genitive_, δήμου προὔχουσιν **they are the first** or **chief** of the people, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 151}}; τοῦ Δωρικοῦ, τοῦ Ἰωνικοῦ [γένεος], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.56}}: _absolutely_, **to be superior,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.39, 3.82; ἀνθρωπεία φύσις πολεμία τοῦ προὔχοντος}}**to all that is eminent,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.84}}; οἱ προὔχοντες the **chief men,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.17}}; οἱ π. [βίοι] the **principal** kinds of lives, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1095b18.}}
**B.II.3** **surpass, excel,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.66: frequently with _genitive_, τὸ Ἄργος π. ἅπασι τῶν ἐν τῇ.. χώρῃ in all things, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1, compare 32; τέχνα γὰρ τέχνας ἑτέρας προὔχει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 138 (Lyric poetry); πολὺ προὔχουσα θεάων 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 218; π. αὐτέων τοσοῦτον ὅσον.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.136; πολλῷ π. [prev. author] 3.82}}; π. δυνάμει, πλήθει καὶ ἐμπειρίᾳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.18, 121; τοσοῦτον ἐκείνων μεγέθει π. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Musc.Enc.” 1: also π. τινὸς τιμήν}} **to be preferred** to him in honour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 208; π. ἔν τινος λαμπρότητι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16; μικρὸν π. ἐν τοῖς μεγάλοις μᾶλλον ἢ πολὺ διαφέρειν ἐν τοῖς μικροῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.5; κατά τι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 30.}}
**B.II.3.b** rarely with _accusative_ _person_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.19 (nisi secl. [ἡμᾶς])}}:—_passive_, **to be excelled,** οὐθὲν π. ὑπὸ τοῦ Διός {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1038d}}; **to be in worse case,** {{NT|NT.Rom.3.9.}}
**B.III** _impersonal_, οὔ τι προέχει **it is of** no **advantage,** with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.27.}}

4285:
** προηγ-έομαι**,
**go first and lead the way,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.48, 7.40, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 13.2, etc.}}; τινι **for** a person, i.e. **guide** him, τῷ θεῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1195, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.1}}; π. τὴν [ὁδόν] {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 6.5.10}}; of troops, **form the van,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.2.27}}; π. πᾶσι [τοῖς ποσίν] **to have** all **in front,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 714a4.}}
**b** **precede logically,** τινος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.210.}}
**2** with _genitive_, **take the lead of,** τῶν προόδων ἄλλους προόδους.. προηγεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 4.5; π. τῆς πομπῆς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.13.11}}; of the planets in retrograde motion, **get ahead of,** ἀπλανῶν ἀστέρων {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 12.22: later with _accusative_, ἀλλήλους π. τῇ τιμῇ NT.Rom.12.10:—with}}_infinitive_, προήγημαι τὴν τούτων ἐπίδοσιν ποήσεσθαι **have taken the initiative** in.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1193.11 (1st c.BC).}}
**3** of things, τὸ πῦρ μὲν ἀπὸ τούτων τῶν ἱερῶν προηγεῖται **goes before, precedes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 13.3; ῥάβδοι π. ἑκάστῳ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.53.8.}}
**4** _present_ _participle_ προηγούμενος, η, ον, **going first,** τὸ π. στράτευμα the **van,** opposed to οὐρά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.2}}; **preceding, {προηγεμών},** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.94 W.; γράμματα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 45.}}
**4.b** in Mathematics texts, τὰ π. **forward** points, i.e. those lying on the same side of the radius vector of a spiral as the direction of its motion, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἑλίκων” 11 “Def.” 6; ἁ π. εὐθεῖα [prev. author] “Spir.” 21,23.}}
**4.c** Astron., τὰ π. ζῴδια signs **leading** in the daily movement of the heavens, i.e. **westerly** signs, opposed to ἑπόμενα, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 1.5, Theo Sm. p.147 H., etc.}}
**4.d** τὰ π. **initial data, premisses,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.16.6, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.20.1; σημεῖα 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 36; φαντασίαι 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 8.49}}; π. οὐσία τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ **given,** i.e. external to the soul, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.7.6; τὸ π., opposed to τὸ ἐπιγέννημα, [prev. work] 7}}; τὰ π. **originals** of paintings, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 396b14.}}
**4.e** **leading, principal,** κατὰ π. λόγον according to a **guiding** principle, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.48}}; ὁ π. λόγος, τὸ π. ἔργον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.20.14, 2.5.4}}; σύν τινι προηγουμένῳ in conjunction with a **purpose,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 27.131, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.4.8; χειρὸς οὐσία μὲν ἡ σάρξ, προηγούμενα δὲ τὰ χειρὸς ἔργα 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.7.24, compare 3.22.76; ὑπηρετικὰ ἄλλοις, οὐκ αὐτὰ π. [prev. work] 2.8.6}}; so in in Mathematics texts, π. θεώρημα **leading** theorem, opposed to ἀντίστροφον, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” p.254F.}}
**4.f** in Medicine texts, π. αἴτιον **predisposing** cause, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Athenaeus Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.112, compare 7.10, +others}}; π. αἰτίαι **antecedent** causes, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.264.}}
**5** _aorist_ _participle_, ὁ -ησάμενος the **former** ἡγεμών, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLips.” 63.6 (4th c.AD), etc.}}

4286:
** πρόθεσις**, εως, ἡ,
(προτίθημι) **placing in public;** of a corpse, **laying** it **out** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (προτίθημι 11), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 947b, 959a, 959e, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.64.}}
**2** **public notice,** αἱ π. τῶν ἀναγεγραμμένων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1322a9.}}
**3** **statement** of a case, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1414b8}}; ὑπέρ τινος τὴν πρόθεσιν ποιήσασθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cat.”}}; **theme, thesis,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.36, +others 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}: generally, **proposition, statement,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Amm.” 2.2.}}
**4** πρόθεσιν ποιεῖσθαι ἐπὶ ταῖς.. προσόδοις to make **payment in advance**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IPE” 12.32 A 41 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC).}}
**5** **offering,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 328.75 (3rd c.BC)}}, Gauthier et Sottas **Decret trilingue** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|(3rd c.BC), 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 1.13 P., “OGI” 90.48 (2nd c.BC), “UPZ” 149.21, 31 (2nd c.BC): especially in “VT”}} and **NT,** οἱ ἄρτοι τῆς π. the loaves **laid before, shew**-bread, {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.1Ki.21.6(7), NT.Matt.12.4; ἡ π. τῶν ἄρτων NT.Heb.9.2, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.294; ἡ τράπεζα τῆς π. LXX.2Ch.29.18.}}
**II** **purpose, end proposed,** ἐπαινῶ σὴν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 22.14 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., Epist. Darei), cf. Philipp. cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.167, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 47a5, 4th-3rd c.BC: Cleanthes Stoicus 1.131, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.35.2, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.21.2, etc.; π. βίων 4th c.AD: Adamantius Physiognomonicus “Phgn.” 1.2}}; defined as σημείωσις ἐπιτελέσεως, {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.41; κατὰ πρόθεσιν}}**purposely,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.10.6; τὰ κατὰ π. φύντα 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.144; τὰ κατὰ τὴν π. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.54.1, compare “PTeb.” 27.81 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.2** **goodwill,** π. ἔχειν πρὸς ἔθνος, ὑπὲρ τῶν πολιτῶν, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.73.2, “OGI” 765.44 (probable reading, Priene).}}
**III** **supposition, calculation,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.32.1, 12.21.6.}}
**IV** Grammars, **preposition,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.45, 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 634.5, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 2, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 305.24, “Pron.” 64.5.}}
**IV.2** **prefixing, placing first,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Synt.” 311.1, “Pron.” 58.16.}}
**IV.3** π. καὶ πτῶσις perhaps **stem** (or **root**) and ending, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2.18.}}
**V** ={προθεσμία}, interpolated in {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}

4287:
** προθεσμ-ία**
(i.e. ἡμέρα), ἡ, **day appointed beforehand, a fixed** or **limited time,** within which money was to be paid, actions brought, claims made, elections held, etc., and if this period was allowed to expire, no further proceedings were allowed, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.25, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.39}}; ἐὰν ἡ π. ἐξήκῃ is past, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.41.9; τριετὴς π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 954d, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.27, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.5.10.}}
**2** generally, **fixed** or **appointed time,** προθεσμίας οὔσης τῷ κινδύνῳ {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.17; π. ἀδικημάτων [prev. author] 13.83; μηδεμίαν εἶναι π. τῆς ἐπιλήψεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 954e; τῆς προθεσμίας ὀλίγης εἰς τὴν χειροτονίαν οὔσης 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.14, cf. NT.Gal.4.2, “OGI” 509.21 (Aphrodisias, 2nd c.AD)}}; οὐκ ἐτήρησε τὴν π. τῆς θεοῦ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Supp.epigram” 4.649 (Lydia, 2nd c.AD)}}; π. φυσικὴ [νόσου] natural **period,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.289; ἡ π. τῆς καθάρσεως 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.10, compare 1.21, +others: _plural_, τρεῖς τοῦ μηνὸς ἀρχαὶ καὶ π. (Kalends, Nones and Ides) 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.269b; προθεσμίας ὁριζομένους ἑορτάς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 27.}}
**3** **occasion of delay,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 15.5.1.}}
**II** προθέσμιος, α, ον, _adjective_ **foreappointed,** Ἔφεσος, ἡ π. τῶν γάμων (i.e. πόλις) {{Refs|Ach. Tat. 5.21.}}

4288:
** προθῡμ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **readiness, willingness, eagerness,** ᾗσι προθυμίῃσι [ῑ] pepoiqw/s {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.588}}; opposed to ἀθυμία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.13; τῶν πέρι καί τινα ἐνάγει π. μαχόμενον ἀποθνῄσκειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49; προθυμίας οὐδὲν ἐλλείπεις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 343; μηδὲν ἀπολείπειν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 961c; οὐ μὴν ἀνήσω νῦν π. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 285; π. ἐμβαλεῖν τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.13, etc.}}; πάσῃ π. with all **zeal,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 412e; διὰ τὴν π. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.20.15}}; ὑπὸ προθυμίας by my **eagerness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 91c: _plural_, τὰς ἄγαν π. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 708.}}
**2** with _genitive_ _person_, ἐκ τῆς Κλεομένεος π. at his **desire,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.65, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1329}}; κατὰ τὴν τούτου π. as far as his **desire** goes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.124}}; τοῦ θεοῦ προθυμίᾳ **by the will** of the god, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1385; also ἡ ἐμὴ π. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.99.}}
**3** with _genitive_ objecti, πᾶσαν π. σωτηρίης.. παρεχόμενοι showing the utmost **zeal** to save it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.98}}; π. ἔργου **readiness** for action, **will** or **purpose** to act, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 669, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 616; π. τοῦ ἐθέλειν κινδυνεύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 697d, compare 935d, etc.}}
**4** π. ἔχειν, ={προθυμεῖσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.19, 53:c}}. _infinitive_, ἔσχε π. στρατεύσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.204, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 689; πᾶσαν π. ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327b, compare 361c}}: also with _participle_, ἔφη πᾶσαν π. σχεῖν δεόμενος {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 23d; also π. ἔχειν ὅπως.. [prev. author] “Mx.” 247a.}}
**II** **goodwill, ready kindness,** Ἑλλήνων εἵνεκα ἔργον.. ἔργασμαι ὑπὸ προθυμίης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.45; εἴς τινας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.43}}; πλείστην π. περὶ ὑμᾶς, π. πολλὴν περί σε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 7.6.11, 7.7.45; ὑπέρ τινων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.8; π. ἐδείξαμεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.74.}}
**III** **desire** of natural functions, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.66, 2.20, 45; πρὸς τὸ κνᾶσθαι 1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 5.129.}}

4289:
** πρόθῡμος**, ον,
**ready, willing, eager,** π. εἰμι, with _infinitive_, ={προθυμέομαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.3, 6.5, +others, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 720, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.18 (_comparative_), etc.}}; π. ἔα πυθέσθαι I was **eager** to learn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.19; εἶναι ὡς -οτάτοισι συνεξελεῖν [prev. author] 1.36; -ότερος ἐγένου ἐμὲ λαβεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220e: with _Article_ inserted, τὸ προσταλαιπωρεῖν.. οὐδεὶς π. ἦν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53.}}
**2** with _genitive_ objecti, **eager for,** ὧν π. ἦσθ᾽ ἀεί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 3; χάριν.. ὧν πρόθυμοι γεγενήμεθα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.67.}}
**3** with Preps., ἐὰν γένῃ π. ἐς τὰ πράγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 209; παρέσχεν ἑαυτὸν.. -ότατον ἐς τὴν ὀλιγαρχίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.68, compare 74; προθυμότεροι ἐς τὸ διώκειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.22; ἐπί τι [prev. author] “HG” 1.1.34}}; πρὸς τὸν πόλεμον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.5.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 468c, etc.}}
**4** _absolutely_, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.91, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 829, “Hec.” 307, etc.: τὸ πρόθυμον, ={προθυμία}, [prev. author] “Med.” 178 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 859b.}}]
**II** **bearing goodwill, wishing well, devoted,** φύλαξ.. τῇ σῇ π. εἰς ὁδὸν κυναγία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 37; π. εἶχ᾽ ὀφθαλμὸν εἰς Ἰάσονα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1146; π. τῇ πόλει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.40}}; εἴς τινας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 6.5.42, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.31.}}
**III** _adverb_ **-μως** **readily, zealously, actively,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.111, 5.13, etc.}}; π. υᾶλλον ἢ φίλως **with** more **zeal** than kindness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1591; π. λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327b; ἐρωτᾶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.38; μανθάνειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 201b (_superlative_)}}; μάχεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.8 (_superlative_)}}; π. ἔχειν πρός τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 176c}}: _comparative_ **-ότερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.80, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.9, etc.}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.59, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.68, etc.}}

4290:
Included with: ** πρόθῡμος**, ον,
**ready, willing, eager,** π. εἰμι, with _infinitive_, ={προθυμέομαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.3, 6.5, +others, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 720, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.18 (_comparative_), etc.}}; π. ἔα πυθέσθαι I was **eager** to learn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.19; εἶναι ὡς -οτάτοισι συνεξελεῖν [prev. author] 1.36; -ότερος ἐγένου ἐμὲ λαβεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220e: with _Article_ inserted, τὸ προσταλαιπωρεῖν.. οὐδεὶς π. ἦν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53.}}
