4291:
** προΐστημι**,
_future_ -στήσω: _aorist 1_ προὔστησα, _participle_ προστήσας, _infinitive_ προστῆσαι. Causal in these tenses, as also in _present_ and _aorist 1_ _middle_, **set before,** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, προστήσας [σε] πρὸ Ἀχαιῶν Τρωσὶ μάχεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.156: with _genitive_, π. τὸ σῶμα τοῦ σκοποῦ}} **put** his body **in the way,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.4 (uncertain reading), compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.33.7.}}
**2** **set over,** ὃν ἡ πόλις ἀξιοῖ αὑτῆς προϊστάναι, variant for{-εστάναι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 197d.}}
**3** **exhibit publicly, prostitute,** π. ἐπ᾽ οἰκημάτων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 7.133.}}
**II** _middle_, mostly _aorist_ 1, **put** another **before oneself, choose as one's leader,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123, 4.80: with _genitive_, προΐστασθαι τουτονὶ ἑαυτοῦ}}**take as one's guardian,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 565c, compare 442a (conjecture), 599a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.37; σφῶν αὐτῶν προὐστήσαντο τιμωρὸν γενέσθαι Κηφίσιον 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.139; στρατηγόν τινα τοῦ πολέμου π. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 21.}}
**II.2** **put before one, put in front,** σκίπωνα προστήσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.172; τὰ ἅρματα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.18}}; τὴν χεῖρα, so as to shade the eyes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 960a21.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, **put forward as an excuse** or **pretence, use as a screen,** τί τάδε προὐστήσω λόγ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 319; τὰ τῶν Ἀμφικτυόνωνδόγματα προστήσασθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.19, etc.: with _genitive_, [τὴν ἀτυχίαν] τῆς κακουργίας προϊστάμενος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.1; τοῦ ἀγῶνος τὴν πρὸς ἔμ᾽ ἔχθραν προΐσταται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.15.}}
**II.4** προστησώμεθα Τύρταιον **put** him **forward, cite** him **as an authority,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 629a.}}
**II.5** **prefer, value above,** ὦτα τοῦ νοῦ προστησάμενοι {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 531b.}}
**II.6** **establish** a thing **before** another, τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς τῆς ὑποστάσεως αὐτῶν (i.e. τῶν ὄντων) {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.6.15, compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 133.}}
**II.7** **manifest,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 195, +others}}
**B** _passive_, with _aorist 2_ _active_ προὔστην: _perfect_ προέστηκα, _2nd pers._ _plural_ προέστατε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49}}; _infinitive_ προεστάναι, _participle_ προεστώς (see. below): _future_ _perfect_ προεστήξομαι, see below {{Refs|11.2}}:—_aorist_ _passive_ προεστάθην, see below{{Refs|11.3}}:—**come forward,** variant for{προσ-} in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.15.}}
**B.2** c._accusative_, **approach** as a suppliant, ἥ σε.. λιπαρεῖ προὔστην χερί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1378}}; προστῆναι μέσην τράπεζαν uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 660.1 (perhaps προσβῆναι):—in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86, προσστῆναι is restored.}}
**B.3** with _dative_, **stand so as to face** another, σοὶ γὰρ αἴας πολέμιος προὔστη ποτέ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1133:—in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.129, προσστάς is restored.}}
**B.4** **stand in public, be a prostitute,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator “Epistulae” 7.3, Vett. Val. 16.7.}}
**B.II** with _genitive_, **to be set over, be at the head of,** τῆς Ἑλλάδος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.69, 5.49; τῶν Ἀρκάδων τοὺς προεστεῶτας [prev. author] 6.74}}; especially **to be chief** or **leader of a party,** τῶν παράλων, τῶν ἐκ τοῦ πεδίου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.59; τοῦ δήμου [prev. author] 3.82, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.7; ἡμῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 419; τῆς πόλεως 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65}}; π. αὐτῶν **to be** their **ringleader,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.2.9}}; π. χοροῦ, στρατεύματος, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 3.4.3}}; π. τῶν πολιτειῶν **head** the respective parties in the state, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.9, etc.:}} _absolutely_, οἱ προεστῶτες, _Ionic dialect_ -εῶτες, **the leading men,** τῶν σκυθέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.79, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.11, etc.; οἱ προεστηκότες ἐν ταῖς πόλεσι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.1}}; οἱ ἐν ταῖς πόλεσι προστάντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82; τῷ προεστῶτι καὶ ἄρχοντι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 428e.}}
**B.II.2** in various relations, **govern, direct,** οὐκ ὀρθῶς σεωυτοῦ προέστηκας **you do** not **manage** yourself well, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.173; π. τῆς μεταβολῆς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.75; τοῦ ἱεροῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.31; τοῦ ἑαυτοῦ βίου [prev. author] “Mem.” 3.2.2; τοῦ πράγματος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.18; προεστήξομαι τῆς χωνεύσεως “PCair.Zen.” 481.9 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐργασίας, τέχνης, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 24, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.612a; π. ἐνδόξου καὶ καλῆς αἱρέσεως “OGI” 219.3 (Ilium, 3rd c.BC).}}
**B.II.3** **stand before** so as **to guard,** οἱ δορυφόροι Μασίστεω προέστησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.107, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 306, etc.}}: hence, **support, succour,** πρόστητ᾽ ἀναγκαίας τύχης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 803}}; ὁ προστὰς τῆς εἰρήνης **the champion** of peace, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.161}}; πάντων προστᾶσα [δύναμις] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 25b}}; π. τινός **to be** his **protector**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“GDI” 1726.6 (Delph., 2nd c.BC), “PFay.” 13.5 (2nd c.BC)}}; τῆς ἐναντίας π. γνώμης {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.5.8; τοῖσιν ἐχθροῖς προὐστήτην φόνου}}**were the authors of..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 980}}; π. [νόσου] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 221: _absolutely_, βέλεα.. ἀρωγὰ προσταθέντα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 206 (Lyric poetry).}}

4292:
** προκαλ-έω**,
**call forth,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 44.34}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 22.9.2}}; **to be evoked,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 411.}}
**B** mostly _middle_, **call out to fight, challenge,** Αἴας δὲ πρῶτος προκαλέσσατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.809, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.142; ἴθι νῦν προκάλεσσαι.. Μενέλαον ἐξαῦτις μαχέσασθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.432, compare 7.39; προκαλέσσατο χάρμῃ [prev. work] 218}}; so, later, π. εἰς ἀγῶνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.17, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Symp.” 20; εἰς μονομαχίαν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 1.24; μάχῃ “Anacreontea” 12.7}}; ταῦτα π. τοὺς συνόντας thus.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.4}}; **challenge** to drink, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus “fragment” 6.7 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; π. τινὰ συμπαίζει; συγγυμνάζεσθαι, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 14.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217c: proverbial, ἱππέας εἰς πεδίον προκαλῇ, Σωκράτη εἰς λόγους προκαλούμενος}} of one who **challenges** another in his own department, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 183d, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 268.}}
**B.2** **invite** or **summon,** τινὰ ἐς λόγους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.201, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.34; ἐς σπονδὰς καὶ διάλυσιν πολέμου [prev. author] 4.19; ἐπὶ ξυμμαχίαν [prev. author] 5.43; ἐπὶ τιμωρίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.226; πρὸς τὸ συνδειπνεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217c}}; [ἰχθῦς] πρὸς τὴν θήραν π. **entice** them out, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 534a17}}; πρὸς αὑτόν τινας **endeavour to attach** them to oneself, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.77.7.}}
**B.3** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **invite** one to do.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Tragica Adespota” 165 (= “Comica Adespota” 1295), etc.; π. τινὰ ἐς λόγον ἐλθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.91; εἰρήνην ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.15, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 294b, etc.; προκαλούμεθα ὑμᾶς φίλοι εἶναι καὶ ἐκ τῆς γῆς ἡμῶν ἀναχωρῆσαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.112}}; of things, αὐτὰ (i.e. τὰ πράγματα) προκαλεῖται παρασκευάζειν τι **invite, admonish,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1331a22: also π. εἰ βούλοιντο..}} with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.30.}}
**B.4** _absolutely_, αὐτῶν προκαλεσαμένων **at** or **after** their **invitation,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 20, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 451c}}; **appeal,** προκαλεῖσθαι περί τινος ἐπὶ Ῥωμαίους {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 24.9.13.}}
**B.II** with _accusative_ _of things_, **offer, propose,** δίκην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.39, compare 2.72, 73, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 984, etc.; τὰ εἰρημένα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.37; τὰς σπονδάς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 796: with _accusative_ _person_ added, προκαλεῖσθαί τινας τὴν εἰρήνην}} **offer** them peace, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 652, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 5a, “Chrm.” 169c.}}
**B.II.2** law-term, **make an offer** or **challenge** to the opponent for bringing about a decision, e.g. for submitting the case to arbitration, letting slaves be put to the torture, etc., προκαλοῦνται πρόκλησιν ἡμᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.12, compare 40.44, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.6}}: with _accusative_ _person_, **challenge** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.23; π. εἰς πάντα τινάς [prev. work]26; εἰς ἀντίδοσιν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.9; εἰς ὅρκον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 52.17; εἰς ἐμφανῶν κατάστασιν 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.31 (to be read προσ-)}}; π. τινά τι **make** one **an offer,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.4, compare 37.42: with _accusative_ et}}_infinitive_, π. τὴν μητέρα ὀμόσαι **offer** that she should take an oath, {{Refs|[prev. author] 55.27}}: with _infinitive_ only, π. ἐθέλειν ἐπιδεῖξαι {{Refs|[prev. author] 27.50, compare 54.27; also π. κατά τινος εἰς μαρτυρίαν [prev. author] 29.20 (προσκ- codices):}} —_passive_, π. περὶ Ἐπιδάμνου ἐς κρίσιν Th.l.34.
**B.III** **call up** or **forth,** εὐγένειαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 308; τὸν Θησαυρὸν ἐς τουμφανές 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 41; τρίχας 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.151.}}

4295:
** πρόκειμαι**,
used as _passive_ of προτίθημι, **to be set before one,** ὀνείαθ᾽ ἑτοῖμα προκείμενα the meats **laid** ready, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.91, +others}}; π. δαίς, δεῖπνον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.211, 5.105; τὰ π. ἀγαθά [prev. author] 9.82}}; ἄρτοι προκείμενοι **shew**-bread, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.39.18 (36); τράπεζα π. [prev. work] 38.9 (37.10).}}
**2** **lie exposed,** ὁρέω παιδίον προκείμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.111}}; of a tuft of wool, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 702}}; ἄτιμος ὧδε πρόκειμαι, says Ajax of himself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 427 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1179; νομίζετε τὸν παῖδα τουτονὶ ἱκετηρίαν ὑμῖν προκεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.83}}; especially **lie dead,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 964 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1059}}; προκείμενον νέκυν **laid out** for burial, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1012, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1101, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 537, “Av.” 474, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.34, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Luct.” 12}}; opposed to ἐξενεχθείς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 23}} (also, **to be buried first,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae ad res Romanas pertinentes 4.735 (from Eumenia), “MAMA” 4.357 ([prev. passage], 3rd c.AD)): _metaphorically_, πρὸς ὕβριν π.}} **to be exposed** to.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 33.15 (uncertain reading).}}
**3** **to be set before** competitors, as the prize of a contest, τοῖσι.. προὔκειτο μέγας τρίπος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 312: hence}}
**3.b** _metaphorically_, **to be set before** one, **proposed,** γνῶμαι τρεῖς προεκέατο three opinions **were set forth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.83, compare 7.16. α; τοσούτων πέρι σκέψις πρόκειτα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 533e, compare “Phdr.” 237c}}; π. τῷ συμβουλεύοντι σκοπὸς τὸ συμφέρον **is proposed** as a mark, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1362a17}}; ἡ προκειμένη ξυμμαγία the alliance **which naturally offers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.35}}; frequently of contests, πόνος τε καὶ ἀγὼν ἔσχατος ψυχῇ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 247b, compare “La.” 182a; καταγέλαστον.., ὃ πάλαι πρόκειται, τοῦτο πάλιν προτιθέναι [prev. author] “Euthd.” 279d}}; **to be extant,** προοίμια π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 722e}}; frequently in _participle_, ὁ προκείμενος ἄεθλος the task **set,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 4.10, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 259, 755; ἀγῶνος μεγίστου π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.60; ἆθλα π. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.47, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.2, etc.; τὸν π. πόνον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1149; ἔχειν ἔργον π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407a}}; τὰ προκείμενα, opposed to μέλλοντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1334, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 984; so ξυμφορᾶς προκειμένης [prev. author] Alcaeus551}}; τὸ π. ἐν τῷ λόγῳ or τὸ π., **the question** under discussion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 457d, “La.” 184c, etc.}}; τὸ π. πρῆγμα the matter **in hand,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.207: _impersonal_, περὶ σωτηρίας προκειμένου}} **when the question is** concerning safety, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 401; πρόκειται ἡμῖν ζητεῖν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 54, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 7.5.}}
**4** **to be set forth, settled, prescribed, appointed,** ἔργων ὧν νόμοι πρόκεινται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 865 (Lyric poetry); π. σημήϊα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.38}}; αἱ προκείμεναι ἡμέραι the **prescribed** days,{{Refs|[prev. work] 87; ὀγδώκοντα ἔτεα ξόης πλήρωμα ἀνδρὶ προκεῖσθαι [prev. author] 3.22; ἀναγκαίη π. [prev. author] 1.11}}; τὸ θανεῖν.. πᾶσι πρόκειται probably in {{Refs|“IG” 12(1).146 (from Rhodes)}}; of laws, νόμους ὑπερβαίνουσα τοὺς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 481}}; of punishments, στέρεσθαι κρατὸς ἦν προκείμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 371; φόνον π. δημόλευστον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 36; πολλῶν [ἁμαρτημάτων] θανάτου ζημίαι π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45.}}
**5** **to be first stated,** οὐ πρόκειται τοῦ λόγου τὸ τί ἐστιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 142b24.}}
**II** **lie before, lie in front of,** with _genitive_, Αἴγυπτος προκειμένη τῆς ἐχομένης γῆς **projecting further than,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.12, compare 4.99}}; ᾗ (ᾧ codices) προὔκειτο μαστῶν περονίς where **was set** a brooch **before** her breasts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 925; πρὸ τῶν ἀνθρώπων π. τὰ παραφράγματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 514b; Ἐφέσου τεὰ τόξα πρόκειται 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 258; οἱ προκείμενοι τῶν στοῶν πύργοι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.48.2}}: _absolutely_, of a cape, island, etc., ἐν τῇ θαλάττῃ π. χωρίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.3; τὰ προκείμενα τῆς χώρας ὄρη [prev. author] “Mem.” 3.5.27; παρὰ ἤπειρον νῆσος π. [prev. author] “Ath.” 2.13, etc.}}
**II.2** **bulge, project,** ἡ γαστὴρ πρόκειται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.3.}}
**III** **precede,** γράμμα π. an **initial** letter, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.426}}; ἐν τοῖς π. in the **preceding pages,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 138.4; ὡς πρόκειται [prev. work] 32.17}}, frequently in Papyrus, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 271.15 (1st c.AD), etc.}}; προκείμενον a **preceding** word, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 39.25, +others}}; χρόνος ὁ προκείμενος date **as above,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PTeb. 397.34 (2nd c.AD)}}; τοῦ π. ἔτους in the **aforesaid** year, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PAmh.” 50.11 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἡ π. βοτάνη **above-mentioned,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PMag.Par. 1.779, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.455 (but οἱ π. θεοί}} **represented** on this monument, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 663.2 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)).}}
**III.2** τὸ π. αὐτοῦ μόριον **from which it is derived** (ὥς from ὅς), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 171.8.}}

4297:
** προ-κοπή**, ἡ,
**progress on a journey,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.76d.}}
**2** generally, **progress, advance,** τὴν οἴησιν ἔλεγε προκοπῆς ἐγκοπήν that opinionforming was the stoppage of **progress,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 4.50}}; π. σχεῖν, ποιεῖσθαι, λαμβάνειν, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.37.10, 2.13.1, 8.15.6; ἡ ἐπὶ τὸ βέλτιον π. [prev. author] 1.12.7; opposed to ἡ ἐπὶ τὸ χεῖρον π. 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 4.4.1}}; frequently of **moral progress,** {{Refs|Stoic. 3.31, +others}}; παλίντροπος π. **progress** in a contrary direction, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.16.9; ἐν παιδείᾳ π. LXX.Sir.51.17}}; **proficiency,** ἐν τοῖς λόγοις {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 107; ἐν φιλοσοφίᾳ 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.6, compare 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 15.16; π. τοῦ εὐαγγελίου NT.Phil.1.12; τὸ ἐπιστρέφειν προϊέναι ἐστί, π. γάρ τις, ἀλλ᾽ οὐχὶ ἀπὸ τοῦ αἰτίου 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 77}}; **improvement** in health, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.8.17: _plural_, προκοπὰς λαμβάνειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.47.12, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.54 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.83, +others, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.2.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.75b, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 22; ἐν προκοπαῖς “epigram Gr.” 321.6, “Arch.Papyrus” 1.220 (from Egypt), compare “IG” 14.1976 (from Rome)}}; ἐν μείζοσι προκοπαῖς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 233.16 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2.b** **success, prosperity,** δόζα καὶ π. παρά τισιν ὑπάρξει{{Refs 4th c.AD+|Aristeas 242, compare “OGI” 627.2 (from Bostra), 4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.1.}}
**3** **military promotion,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.2.6.}}
**4** **process,** κατὰ προκοπήν by **process of time** or **growth,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.43.}}
**5** in Mathematics texts, **progression** of numbers, μέχρις ἂν εἰς τετράδα ἡ π. ἔλθῃ {{Refs|“Theologumena Arithmeticae” 21.}}

4298:
** προκόπτω**,
**cut** one's way **forward,** only _metaphorically_, π. διὰ τῆς λεωφόρου **advance** by the high-road, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Anonymus cited in 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus: with _accusative_ _cognate_, τὴν ὁδὸν προκεκοφέναι 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 2.6.7: without ὁδόν, ἐπὶ πολὺ προκεκοφότες [prev. work] 2.16.3; π. τριάκοντα σταδίους 4th c.BC: Chio Epistolographus “Ep.” 4.2}}:—_passive_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}, **advance, prosper,** ἀνωτέρω ὀδὲν τῶν πρηγμάτων προκοπτομένων {{Refs|1.190; ἐς τὸ πρόσω οὐδὲν προεκόπτετο τῶν πρηγμάτων 3.56.}}
**II** with _neuter_ _adjectives_, προκόψομεν οὐδέν **shall make** no **progress, advance** not at all. {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 35}}; τὰ πολλὰ προκόψασ᾽ **having prepared** most of **the way,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 23}}; τί ἂν προκόπτοι; what **good would you get?** {{Refs|[prev. author] Alcaeus1079}}; οὐδὲν προὔκοπτον εἰς.. **they made** no **progress** towards.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.6; π. οὐδὲν ἐς πρόσθεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 961; ἐν παιδείᾳ προκεκοφότες 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.69; π. ἐν Ἰουδαϊσμῷ NT.Gal.1.14; ἐν τοῖς μαθήμασι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 63: with _dative_ modi, τοῖς πλούτοις -κεκοφότες 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 34 / 5.2.26; σοφίᾳ καὶ ἡλικίᾳ NT.Luke.2.52.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_ _of things_, τοῦ ναυτικοῦ μέγα μέρος προκόψαντες **having made improvements** in their navy to a great extent, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.56}}; ἡμῶν προκοπτόντων τῆς ἀρχῆς ἐκείνοις **since** we **promote the increase of** their empire, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.60}}; ἐπὶ πλεῖον π. ἀσεβείας **having advanced** further **in** impiety, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|NT.2Tim.2.16: _absolutely_, ἐπὶ τοσοῦτο π. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.23.2; ἐπὶ πλεῖον π. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.98.}}
**II.3** especially in Philos., of moreal and intellectual progress, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.56, Chrysipp.[prev. work]2.337, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.543e, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.4.1, 3.2.5, etc.; κατὰ φιλοσοφίαν π. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 17; ὁ λόγος π. 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.240}}; προκοπτούσης τῆς θεραπείας if the treatment **succeeds,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.413, compare 1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus in “Rh.Mus.” 58.103}}; εἴωθε προκόπτειν ἡ.. ἀγωγή the treatment is usually **successful,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 46.9.1}}; -κεκοφυίας τῆς νόσου as the disease **improves,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 9.13.}}
**II.3.b** of Time, προκοπτούσης ὁδοῦ **as** the journey **advanced,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 111.4; ἡ νὺξ προέκοψεν}}**is far spent,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Rom.13.12, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.4.6}}; **to be advanced** in years, τῇ ἡλικίᾳ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 708.18 (Istropolis, 2nd c.BC)}}; ὁ μὲν -κέκοφεν, ὁ δὲ νέος ἐστίν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.60 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}

4299:
** πρό-κρῐμα**, ατος, τό,
**prejudgement,** {{NT|NT.1Tim.5.21, Anonymus cited in 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus, 12th c.AD: Gregorius Corinthius Grammaticus in Rhetores Graeci 7.1123 W.}}
**2** = **praejudicium,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 5(1).21 ii 7 (Sparta, 2nd c.AD), Mitteis “Chr.” 88 ii 30 (2nd c.AD), “PFlor.” 68.13 (2nd c.AD), Codex 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator 10.11.8.5.}}
